-- advances-and-prepayments-deferral-and-classification: content_json backfill (the document content was serialised from)
with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A payment made before delivery buys a right to goods or services the entity controls, and that right is the asset. What the right turns into decides its path. A prepaid expense is used up into expense over time; an advance to a supplier becomes the inventory, fixed asset or oil and gas property it pays for."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The current and non-current split follows when the benefit is consumed. A prepayment is divided by time, the next twelve months current and the rest non-current; an advance usually takes the classification of the asset it will become."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Because the holder expects goods or services and no cash, advances and prepayments are non-monetary and non-financial. They keep the exchange rate of the payment date and stay outside the expected-credit-loss model, until delivery fails and the right becomes a claim for a refund. A cash call that the operator must refund in cash may be the exception. PHE's line holds 179,428 at the end of 2024, of which 99,099 is prepayments and 76,196 cash calls paid to operators."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report advances and prepayments, current portion, of 179,428 at 31 December 2024 against 177,429 a year earlier, and a non-current portion of 56 against 2,242, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The current line is 2.2 percent of current assets, and the two lines together are 0.6 percent of total assets (1.8 and 0.6 percent a year earlier). This page uses those numbers as the starting point for its examples. It also covers other current assets of 60,913 (210,253) and other non-current assets of 432,852 (455,380), most of which PHE calls deferred expenses (Note 14, Daftar 5/105–5/106, PDF pp. 122–123)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After reading it a student should be able to explain why a payment in advance is an asset, record and release it under both recording methods, split it between current and non-current, measure it in a foreign currency, and recognise when it has to be written down."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"No single chapter of an intermediate text covers this line. The asset definition follows the FASB and IASC frameworks and the Conceptual Framework chapter of Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield's "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intermediate Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":" (IFRS edition); the deferral and adjusting entries follow Kieso's accounting-cycle chapter; the mirror image on the supplier's side follows Kieso's chapter on current liabilities. The order below runs from definition to recognition, measurement, classification, impairment and presentation."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Why a payment in advance is an asset"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cash has gone, and nothing has arrived yet. The question is whether that is an expense or an asset. The FASB and IASC definitions give an asset three characteristics: future economic benefit, control by the entity, and an origin in a past transaction or event. An insurance premium paid in October for a year of cover meets all three. The cover still to run is a future benefit, the entity alone can claim under the policy, and the payment is the past transaction. The 2018 Conceptual Framework (KKPK in Indonesia) words it as a present economic resource, a right with the potential to produce economic benefits, controlled by the entity as a result of past events. A prepayment is that right in its simplest form, a right to goods or services whose price is already settled."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Control matters more than ownership; the 1989 IASC Framework said that ownership is not essential to an asset. Nobody owns the unexpired part of an insurance policy in the way they own a truck, yet the payer controls the benefit and can keep others from it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The line holds three kinds of right, distinguished by what each turns into."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Prepaid expenses"},{"type":"text","text":": insurance, rent, travel and similar services consumed in operations. They become expense."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advances to suppliers"},{"type":"text","text":": payments ahead of delivery of goods, such as materials. They become inventory, then expense."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advances for capital spending"},{"type":"text","text":": payments to fabricators or contractors for equipment or wells. They become fixed assets or oil and gas properties and reach profit through depreciation or depletion."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's Note 15 shows a fourth kind specific to joint operations, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"cash call advances"},{"type":"text","text":", \"advances made to PSC operators for oil and natural gas exploration and production activities\" (Note 15, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123). Section 10 explains them."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Recognition: the accrual basis and the two recording methods"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under the cash basis the premium would be an expense in the month it is paid, and the months it actually protects would show nothing. The accrual basis, one of the assumptions listed in Kieso's Conceptual Framework chapter, records the effect when the benefit is consumed. A cost is first recognised as an asset and then charged as expense in the periods that use up its benefit. The part not yet used up stays on the balance sheet. That is a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"deferral"},{"type":"text","text":", and the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"adjusting entry"},{"type":"text","text":" at period end moves the used part across."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso shows two ways to book the payment, and they end in the same place. For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary pays 48,000 on 1 October 2024 for twelve months of offshore facilities insurance, part of the kind of balance behind PHE's 99,099 of prepayments."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost per month = 48,000 / 12 = 4,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Expired at 31 December 2024 = 3 × 4,000 = 12,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unexpired at 31 December 2024 = 48,000 − 12,000 = 36,000"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Asset method"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Expense method"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1 Oct 2024, payment"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Prepaid insurance 48,000 / Cr Cash 48,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Insurance expense 48,000 / Cr Cash 48,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 Dec 2024, adjustment"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Insurance expense 12,000 / Cr Prepaid insurance 12,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Prepaid insurance 36,000 / Cr Insurance expense 36,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Result"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prepaid 36,000; expense 12,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prepaid 36,000; expense 12,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The asset method suits a payment that will straddle a year end; the expense method suits a routine payment usually used up before the next close, with the adjustment made only when it is not. If the adjustment is forgotten, the asset method overstates the asset and understates expense by 12,000, and the expense method does the reverse by 36,000. Either error reverses next year, so both years are misstated."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Measurement after recognition: amortisation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A prepayment is measured at cost and released as the benefit is consumed. PHE's policy is that \"prepayments are amortized on a straight-line basis over the estimated beneficial periods of the prepayments\" (Note 3k, Daftar 5/53, PDF p. 70). Straight-line suits time-based services such as insurance and rent, where each month delivers the same cover."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Not every prepayment is consumed with time. A prepaid service that is used in events is released when each event happens. For example (illustration), 900 paid in December 2024 for crew flights in January 2025 is still a prepayment at the year end, and the whole 900 becomes expense in January when the crews fly."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 Dec 2024: prepaid travel 900. January 2025: Dr Travel expense 900 / Cr Prepaid travel 900"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Leases are the large exception. Under PSAK 116 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Leases"},{"type":"text","text":" (IFRS 16), lease payments made at or before commencement are part of the cost of the right-of-use asset (para. 24(b)), so rent paid in advance on a lease longer than twelve months leaves this line at commencement. Only short-term and low-value leases, which PHE leaves outside its single lease model (Note 3n, Daftar 5/57, PDF p. 74), still produce prepaid rent. PHE's right-of-use note even carries a column headed \"prepaid expense reclassifications\", moving 450 within its 2024 cost movements (Note 18, Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Advances to suppliers and for capital spending"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An advance is never released to expense from this line. It waits for delivery and then moves, in one entry, into whatever was delivered. The supplier sees the same transaction from the other side. Kieso's current-liabilities chapter records cash received before delivery as "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"unearned revenue"},{"type":"text","text":" (under PSAK 115, a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"contract liability"},{"type":"text","text":"), which becomes revenue when the goods are handed over. The payer's advance and the supplier's contract liability are the two halves of one contract."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary orders drilling chemicals for 10,000 and pays a 30 percent advance of 3,000. The supplier delivers the next month and invoices the remaining 7,000."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Buyer (PHE subsidiary)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Supplier"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Advance paid"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Advances to suppliers 3,000 / Cr Cash 3,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 3,000 / Cr Contract liability 3,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Delivery"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Inventories 10,000 / Cr Advances to suppliers 3,000 / Cr Trade payables 7,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Contract liability 3,000, Dr Trade receivables 7,000 / Cr Revenue 10,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The chemicals then follow the inventory rules of "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/inventories-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"inventories-net"},{"type":"text","text":" and become production cost when used, or part of a well if issued to drilling."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An advance for capital spending follows the same pattern with a different destination. For example (illustration), a subsidiary pays a fabricator 40 percent of a 25,000 compressor package, that is 10,000, and on delivery the package is installed on a platform under construction."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Oil and gas properties, assets under construction 25,000 / Cr Advances for capital spending 10,000 / Cr Trade payables 15,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"No expense arises at any step. The 25,000 reaches profit only as the platform is depleted."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Classification: current or non-current"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 201 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Presentation of Financial Statements"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) classifies an asset as current when the entity expects to realise it, or intends to sell or consume it, within its normal operating cycle, holds it mainly for trading, or expects to realise it within twelve months after the reporting date (para. 66). Everything else is non-current."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Prepayments are split by time."},{"type":"text","text":" For example (illustration), a subsidiary pays 72,000 on 1 July 2024 for a three-year policy."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost per month = 72,000 / 36 = 2,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Expense 2024 = 6 × 2,000 = 12,000; unexpired at 31 December 2024 = 72,000 − 12,000 = 60,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Current (2025) = 12 × 2,000 = 24,000; non-current (2026 to June 2027) = 60,000 − 24,000 = 36,000"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Presentation entry: Dr Prepayments, non-current 36,000 / Cr Prepayments, current 36,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Each year the next twelve months move back into current. PHE's non-current prepayments fell from 62 to nil in 2024, consistent with a long-dated prepayment running down into the current portion or being expensed (Note 15)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Advances follow their destination."},{"type":"text","text":" An advance for inventory is current, because inventory is. For an advance towards a fixed asset or oil and gas property, PSAK 201 has no specific paragraph. Common practice presents it as non-current whatever the delivery date, because what is held is an undelivered part of a long-lived asset and its cost will be recovered over many years. Some preparers instead read \"realised within twelve months\" as covering conversion into the asset and present a short-dated capital advance as current. The choice moves working capital. A 10,000 capital advance presented as current would add 10,000 to current assets that will never fund suppliers or short-term debt. PHE's non-current advances fell from 2,180 to 56 in 2024 (Note 15); the note does not say what they were."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Foreign currency and financial-instrument status"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's functional currency is the US dollar (Elnusa's is the rupiah; Note 3, Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83), but many suppliers bill in rupiah. PSAK 221 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 10, IAS 21) retranslates "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"monetary items"},{"type":"text","text":", rights to receive or obligations to pay a fixed or determinable number of currency units, at the closing rate. Non-monetary items carried at historical cost keep the rate of the transaction date, and the standard lists amounts prepaid for goods and services as an example (para. 16). The holder of an advance expects goods, so a weaker rupiah does not reduce what it is owed. ISAK 122 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Foreign Currency Transactions and Advance Consideration"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly ISAK 33, IFRIC 22) adds that the asset eventually received is translated at the rate of the day the advance was first recognised, with a separate date for each payment (paras. 8–9)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary orders spare parts for IDR 80 billion. It pays an advance of IDR 48 billion on 15 December 2024 at IDR 16,000 per USD. The year-end rate is IDR 16,200 per USD. The parts arrive on 15 March 2025, when the balance of IDR 32 billion is paid at IDR 16,400 per USD."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Advance = IDR 48,000,000 thousand / 16,000 = 3,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"If wrongly retranslated at year end = IDR 48,000,000 thousand / 16,200 = 2,963; loss that must not be booked = 37"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Balance on delivery = IDR 32,000,000 thousand / 16,400 = 1,951"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost of the parts = 3,000 + 1,951 = 4,951"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"15 Mar 2025: Dr Inventories 4,951 / Cr Advances to suppliers 3,000 / Cr Cash 1,951"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The same reasoning removes the line from financial-instrument accounting. PSAK 232 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Financial Instruments: Presentation"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32) states that assets such as prepaid expenses, whose benefit is the receipt of goods or services, are not financial assets (para. AG11). So PSAK 109's expected-credit-loss model does not apply to them."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Impairment and recoverability"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A prepayment is worth carrying only while its benefit is still coming. If the benefit disappears, the unexpired amount is written off at once. For example (illustration), if the shore-base yard behind a six-month prepaid rent is closed after three months with no refund, the remaining three months are expensed in the month of closure."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An advance is exposed to the supplier. While delivery is still expected, the question is whether the supplier can perform, judged by its financial condition, delivery record and any guarantee. When delivery fails and the contract is terminated, the right to goods becomes a right to a refund, which is monetary and a financial asset. The advance is derecognised and a refund claim is recognised. Because the claim is credit-impaired from the start, it is recognised at what is expected to be recovered, and later changes go through PSAK 109's impairment model. For example (illustration), the chemicals supplier of section 4 becomes insolvent before delivering, and the administrator expects unsecured creditors to recover 40 percent."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Expected recovery = 3,000 × 40% = 1,200; loss = 3,000 − 1,200 = 1,800"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Refund receivable 1,200, Dr Loss on supplier default 1,800 / Cr Advances to suppliers 3,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Timing follows PSAK 210 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Events after the Reporting Period"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 8, IAS 10). If the insolvency reveals a condition that existed at the reporting date and is known before the statements are authorised, the write-down belongs to that year; if it arises later, it belongs to the next. Suppliers that take large advances often provide an "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"advance payment guarantee"},{"type":"text","text":", a bank guarantee that repays the advance if they fail. It does not change the classification, but it changes the expected recovery and so the loss."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Advances and prepayments across industries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The deferral logic is universal, and several industries add rules of their own."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Insurers."},{"type":"text","text":" Commissions paid to brokers before a group of insurance contracts is recognised are a prepayment of sorts. PSAK 117 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Insurance Contracts"},{"type":"text","text":" (IFRS 17) recognises them as an asset for "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"insurance acquisition cash flows"},{"type":"text","text":", which is assessed for recoverability and derecognised into the measurement of the contracts once they are recognised, unless the insurer expenses these cash flows under the simplified premium allocation approach."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Property buyers and developers."},{"type":"text","text":" Indonesian companies often pay large advances to buy land long before title transfers. They are non-current when the land will be a fixed asset, investment property or a land bank for future development. An advance for land a developer will build on and sell within its operating cycle follows inventory and is current. Either way it keeps the payment-date rate in a foreign-currency entity. On the other side, a developer selling apartments off plan holds the buyers' payments as contract liabilities under PSAK 115 until control of the unit passes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Islamic finance."},{"type":"text","text":" In a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"murabahah"},{"type":"text","text":" sale the customer may pay a down payment ("},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"uang muka"},{"type":"text","text":") before the contract to show commitment. PSAK 402 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Murabahah"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 102) calls it "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"hamish jiddiyah"},{"type":"text","text":", which differs from "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"'urbun"},{"type":"text","text":", a deposit forfeited on cancellation. The bank holds the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"hamish jiddiyah"},{"type":"text","text":" as a liability and, when the sale is made, deducts it from the receivable (or from the cost of the asset sold); if the customer withdraws, the bank keeps only what covers its actual costs and returns the rest. For example (illustration), a customer pays an advance of 50 on a 1,000 murabahah. If the sale goes ahead, the receivable is 1,000 − 50 = 950; if the customer withdraws and the bank's actual cost of the cancellation is 20, the bank keeps 20 and refunds 30."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Taxes paid in advance."},{"type":"text","text":" Prepaid income tax and input VAT look like prepayments but are claims on the government under tax law. They are presented separately. Prepaid income tax follows PSAK 212, and input VAT the VAT rules; PHE shows them on its own lines ("},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/corporate-dividend-taxes-receivable-current","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"corporate-dividend-taxes-receivable-current"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-taxes-receivable-current"},{"type":"text","text":")."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 15 and 14"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 201 asks for material classes to be shown separately, in the statement or the notes. PHE splits the line into three classes (Note 15, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 Dec 2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 Dec 2023"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cash call advances"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"76,196"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"132,275"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prepayments"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"99,099"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"24,188"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Others"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4,133"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"20,966"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Current portion"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"179,428"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"177,429"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Advances, non-current"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"56"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,180"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prepayments, non-current"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"62"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Non-current portion"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"56"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"2,242"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prepayments: 99,099 − 24,188 = +74,911, or +309.7%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cash call advances: 76,196 − 132,275 = −56,079, or −42.4%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Current portion: 179,428 − 177,429 = +1,999, or +1.1%"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The flat total hides two large movements in opposite directions. Prepayments, described as insurance, rent, travel and other operating activities, quadrupled, while cash call advances fell by 42 percent. The note does not explain either change."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"One classification point needs care. PHE's financial-instrument table for 2023 lists \"advances and prepayment\" of 179,671, the two portions combined, as financial assets at amortised cost, while the 2024 table omits the line (Note 47, Daftar 5/222–5/223, PDF pp. 239–240). Under PSAK 232 AG11 the prepayments at least are non-financial. Cash call advances are less clear, since unspent cash can be offset or returned; section 10 comes back to them."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Note 14, other assets."},{"type":"text","text":" PHE holds a larger block of deferred costs outside this line (Note 14, Daftar 5/105–5/106, PDF pp. 122–123)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 Dec 2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 Dec 2023"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred expenses"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"56,828"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"206,781"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Employee receivables"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3,904"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3,472"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Others"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"181"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Other current assets"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"60,913"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"210,253"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred expenses"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"304,148"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"84,222"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Restricted funds for site restoration in joint operations"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"66,719"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"296,555"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unrealised hedge gain"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"50,769"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"66,533"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dead stock material"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6,809"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6,231"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Others"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4,407"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,839"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Other non-current assets"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"432,852"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"455,380"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred expenses, both portions = 56,828 + 304,148 = 360,976 (2023: 206,781 + 84,222 = 291,003)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Against prepayments in Note 15 = 360,976 / 99,099 = 3.6 times"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"In theory a deferred expense is the prepayment of section 2 under another name, a cost already paid and held as an asset until the periods it benefits, split by time as in section 5. PHE's description fits that only in part. Its deferred expenses relate to an escrow account for a Gabon Oil Company carry receivable, to underlifting, to a bank guarantee and to other assets of M&P; the current portion also holds receivables from underlifting for non-operators in PIEP and an allocation account for a finance lease receivable at Elnusa. Recovery \"is based on the contracts in the activity's normal operating cycle\". Escrow balances, underlift claims and lease receivables are claims to cash or oil, closer to receivables than to costs awaiting expense, so the label says less about their nature than the description does. In 2024 the current portion fell 149,953 and the non-current portion rose 219,926, a shift the note does not explain."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The rest of Note 14 belongs to other pages. Employee receivables are loans to employees of PT Badak NGL, financial assets normally measured at amortised cost. The restricted funds are cash that PSC operators deposit to fund site restoration in joint operations, recognised by PHE's subsidiaries in line with their participating interests. They fell 229,836, or 77.5 percent, and the note says funds not yet transferred by SKK Migas were moved, with their interest, to a joint account of SKK Migas and PHR, without linking that to the fall. How such a fund relates to the restoration provision is the subject of "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"decommissioning-site-restoration-provision"},{"type":"text","text":". The unrealised hedge gain is the fair value of cash flow hedges of interest rates on the JTB loan, a derivative whose effective portion is deferred in OCI, with 1,395 of ineffectiveness in finance costs; hedge accounting belongs to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"oci-cash-flow-hedge"},{"type":"text","text":". The note does not describe the dead stock material."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Upstream note: cash calls in a joint operation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a joint operation the operator spends on behalf of all partners and bills each its share through a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"joint interest billing"},{"type":"text","text":" (JIB). Partners fund the operator in advance through monthly "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"cash calls"},{"type":"text","text":". Under PSAK 111, PHE recognises its share of the joint operation's assets and expenses (Note 3y, Daftar 5/74, PDF p. 91). A cash call paid before the spending is therefore an advance that will turn into PHE's share of whatever the operator spends."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), PHE holds 30 percent of a block and pays a cash call of 9,000. The operator's JIB for the month reports spending of 25,000, of which 10,000 is operating cost and 15,000 drilling of a development well."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's share = 25,000 × 30% = 7,500 (operating 3,000, drilling 4,500); unspent advance = 9,000 − 7,500 = 1,500"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Production expenses 3,000, Dr Oil and gas properties, assets under construction 4,500 / Cr Cash call advances 7,500"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"If the operator had spent 35,000, PHE's share of 10,500 would exceed the 9,000 paid, and PHE would owe the operator 1,500. PHE reports such a balance, \"due to operators – under cash calls\" of 28,933 in other payables (Note 21, Daftar 5/115, PDF p. 132). When PHE is itself the operator, its policy recognises a receivable from or payable to the non-operators for the difference between their share of spending and the cash calls received (Note 3y, Daftar 5/75, PDF p. 92)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Whether an unspent cash call is a non-monetary advance or a monetary claim on the operator depends on the joint operating agreement. If unspent amounts are refundable in cash, the balance looks more like a receivable; if they can only be applied to future spending, it looks like an advance. PHE's 2023 table is weak evidence either way, because it also lists the prepayments, which are not financial assets."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Related lines"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"advances-prepayments-non-current"},{"type":"text","text":": the non-current portion covered on this page."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/inventories-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"inventories-net"},{"type":"text","text":": the destination of advances for materials."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"oil-gas-properties-net"},{"type":"text","text":": the destination of capital advances and the drilling share of cash calls."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"right-of-use-assets-net"},{"type":"text","text":": where rent paid in advance on long leases now sits."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-payables","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-payables"},{"type":"text","text":": amounts owed to operators under cash calls."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-payables","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"trade-payables"},{"type":"text","text":": the balance due on delivery after an advance."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"other-current-assets"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"other-non-current-assets"},{"type":"text","text":": Note 14, covered in section 9."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"decommissioning-site-restoration-provision"},{"type":"text","text":": the obligation behind the restricted restoration funds."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"oci-cash-flow-hedge"},{"type":"text","text":": where the effective part of the hedge gain is deferred."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Least-confident assertions"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Presenting a capital advance as non-current whatever the delivery date is common practice; PSAK 201 states no such requirement, and some preparers present short-dated capital advances as current."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The reading of cash call advances as advances that may be monetary depends on joint operating agreements that PHE does not disclose. PHE's 2023 financial-instrument table, which also lists the prepayments, is weak evidence, and its omission of the line in 2024 may be a change of view or an oversight."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two readings of PHE's balances are inferences. That the non-current prepayments of 62 ran down into the current portion rests on two balances. That several of Note 14's \"deferred expenses\" (escrow, underlifting, the lease receivable allocation) are receivable-like rests on PHE's one-sentence description; the note gives no amounts by component."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"hamish jiddiyah"},{"type":"text","text":" treatment is taken from IAI's 2019 exposure draft of PSAK 102 (now PSAK 402); the final text was not opened, so its wording may differ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The refund claim is recognised at the 40 percent expected to be recovered, undiscounted. Treating it as credit-impaired at recognition, with no day-one allowance, follows PSAK 109 as understood here; the paragraphs were not opened."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); Note 3k (Daftar 5/53, PDF p. 70); Note 3n (Daftar 5/57, PDF p. 74); Note 3y (Daftar 5/74–5/75, PDF pp. 91–92); Note 14 (Daftar 5/105–5/106, PDF pp. 122–123); Note 15 (Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123); Note 18 (Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129); Note 21 (Daftar 5/115, PDF p. 132); Note 47 (Daftar 5/222–5/223, PDF pp. 239–240). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes this series follows, \"TA – Chapter 7 – Assets\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): three characteristics of an asset drawn from the FASB (SFAC 6 para. 25), IASC and AASB definitions, control over ownership. NOTES. The notes name no author for these points and use the pre-2018 definition; this page adds the 2018 Conceptual Framework wording. The 1989 Framework's \"ownership is not essential\" wording VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation material quoting the Framework)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes, \"Inter 1 – Chapter 2 – Conceptual Framework\": accrual basis among the assumptions, expense recognition. NOTES. The notes call the framework \"PSAK 15\"; it carries no PSAK number."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes, \"TA – Chapter 10 – Expenses\": cost first recognised as an asset, then charged as expense; systematic allocation. NOTES. The notes quote Suwardjono, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Teori Akuntansi: Perekayasaan Pelaporan Keuangan"},{"type":"text","text":", 3rd ed., p. 397, for the two stages (book not opened)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 13 – Current Liabilities\": customer advances and unearned revenue. NOTES. The notes do not cover prepaid expenses or adjusting entries (Kieso's accounting-cycle chapter is not in the folder); sections 2–3 follow Kieso (ref. 6)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso, D. E., Weygandt, J. J. and Warfield, T. D., "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intermediate Accounting, IFRS Edition"},{"type":"text","text":" (Wiley), chapters on the conceptual framework, the accounting information system (adjusting entries, asset and expense methods) and current liabilities. Edition not checked. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Godfrey, J., Hodgson, A., Tarca, A., Hamilton, J. and Holmes, S., "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Accounting Theory"},{"type":"text","text":" (Wiley), the text the TA notes are thought to follow; not cited in the body, because the book is not on disk. NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 201 (IAS 1), para. 66. VERIFIED (web, cpdbox.com and ACCA study material summarising IAS 1.66)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 221 (IAS 21), para. 16. VERIFIED (web, IASB and EFRAG documents quoting the paragraph)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"ISAK 122 (IFRIC 22), paras. 8–9. VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation text and illustrative examples at ifrs.org). Number ISAK 122 VERIFIED (IAI renumbering list)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 232 (IAS 32), para. AG11. VERIFIED (web, IFRS Interpretations Committee staff paper on long-term prepayments, 2014, and a reproduction of the paragraph; rechecked 2026-10-11)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 116 (IFRS 16), para. 24(b). VERIFIED (web, AASB copy of IFRS 16)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 109 (IFRS 9), PSAK 115 (IFRS 15), PSAK 111 (IFRS 11), PSAK 117 (IFRS 17), PSAK 210 (IAS 10), PSAK 212 (IAS 12), PSAK 402. Numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia"},{"type":"text","text":"). PSAK 117 paras. 28B, 28C, 28E VERIFIED (web, AASB copy of IFRS 17). PSAK 402 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"hamish jiddiyah"},{"type":"text","text":" and the two recognition options VERIFIED (web, IAI, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"DE PSAK 102, ISAK 101 dan ISAK 102"},{"type":"text","text":", 2019, paras. 4, 12, 19; exposure draft, final text not opened). Other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lessons salvaged from the independent recheck of the superseded draft (2026-10-09), and the v0.5 recheck (2026-10-11)."}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb
   where e.id = 'edfe0641-4ab9-4978-9faf-790f494d7bb1'
     and e.section = 'accounting'
