-- consolidated-cash-flows-and-mid-year-acquisitions: content_json backfill (the document content was serialised from)
with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A consolidated cash flow statement reports only cash that crosses the group's boundary. Cash moving between parent and subsidiary is eliminated, so of a subsidiary's dividend only the part paid to the non-controlling interests (NCI) survives, as a financing outflow. In the indirect method the reconciliation starts from consolidated profit, NCI's share included, and deducts the profit of equity-accounted investees (associates and joint ventures carried at the investor's share of their net assets) that was not received in cash."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deals that change the group are classified by what they do to control. Obtaining or losing control of a subsidiary is one investing line, net of the subsidiary's own cash acquired or given up (PSAK 207 paras. 39 and 42). Buying from or selling to the NCI while control continues is financing (para. 42A). The acquired company's balances never pass through operating cash flows."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A subsidiary bought during the year enters the consolidated income statement and cash flow statement from the acquisition date. The lecture notes' second approach, consolidating the full year and deducting \"preacquisition earnings\", is no longer allowed. Its only remnant is a note disclosure of the acquiree's revenue and profit since acquisition and on a full-year pro forma basis (PSAK 103 para. B64(q))."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Barito Pacific Tbk and subsidiaries (BRPT) paid dividends of 60,121 to the NCI of its subsidiaries in 2025, in USD thousand (BRPT FS 2025, consolidated statement of cash flows, PDF p. 19). Its largest acquisition of the year, Aster Chemical and Energy Pte. Ltd. (ACE), was completed on 1 April 2025, a quarter into the year."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After reading this page a student should be able to prepare a consolidated cash flow statement by the indirect and direct methods, classify every cash flow that involves the NCI or a change in the group, and consolidate a subsidiary bought or partly sold in the middle of a year."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from BRPT's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The order follows the lecture notes for AKL Chapter 4 (the cash flow part) and Chapter 8 (acquisitions during the period). How NCI is measured and how ownership changes are recorded in equity is in the Non-Controlling Interest essay ("},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"25-non-controlling-interest"},{"type":"text","text":"); this page takes those entries as given and follows the cash."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. One entity, one cash pool"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The group reports as if it were one company, so its only cash flows are flows with parties outside the group. The notes open the topic with the \"single concept\" of cash and cash equivalents, defined as in PSAK 207 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Statement of Cash Flows"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7) and covered in "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"08-cash-and-cash-equivalents"},{"type":"text","text":". BRPT's policy states the consequence: all cash flows \"relating to transactions between members of the Group are eliminated in full on consolidation\" (Note 3c, PDF p. 68)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Adding up each company's statement and removing intragroup flows gives the same answer as the notes' route, which derives the statement from the consolidated balance sheets and income statement."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The intragroup dividend shows why elimination matters. For example (illustration), parent P owns 80 percent of S, and S pays a dividend of 50."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"In S's own statement: financing outflow (50)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"In P's own statement: dividend received 80% × 50 = 40, operating or investing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Added together: 40 − 50 = (10)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Eliminate the 40 that stayed inside the group: consolidated financing outflow (10), dividends paid to NCI"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Only the 10 paid to the NCI left the group, and only that remains in the consolidated statement."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. The indirect method: from consolidated profit to operating cash"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under PSAK 207 the reconciliation starts from profit before attribution, so NCI's share of profit is never \"added back\" (paras. 18(b) and 20). The indirect method removes items that did not involve cash and adjusts for changes in working capital. The notes' example is Pop Corporation and an 80 percent-owned subsidiary, with consolidated profit of 200 for 2017, of which 150 is the controlling share and 50 is NCI's, and cash rising from 180 to 255. The notes start from the controlling share and add the NCI share back, since it increases consolidated net assets \"persis seperti\" the controlling share. That layout reflects older US practice, where minority interest was deducted as if it were an expense. Both reach the same figure."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Notes (Beams)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 207"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Starting point"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Controlling share 150"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit for the year 200"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"+50"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"already included"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Undistributed income of equity investee (15 − 10)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Loss on sale of land"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"+10"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"+10"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Depreciation, equipment 100 and buildings 20"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"+120"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"+120"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Patent amortisation"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"+10"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"+10"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Increase in receivables"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(105)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(105)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Increase in inventories"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(45)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(45)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Decrease in payables"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(20)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(20)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Net cash from operating activities"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"165"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"165"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investing: proceeds from sale of land 10"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Financing: dividends to Pop's shareholders (80) and to NCI (20) = (100)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net increase = 165 + 10 − 100 = 75; cash 180 + 75 = 255"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Only the cash part of an investee's profit stays in operating cash."},{"type":"text","text":" Pop's income from its equity investee is 15, but only the 10 received as dividends was cash, so the notes deduct the \"undistributed income\" of 5. Dividends received may be classified as operating or investing, consistently from period to period (paras. 31 and 33). If Pop chose investing, the whole 15 comes out of the reconciliation and the 10 appears below."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Operating = 165 − 10 = 155; investing = 10 + 10 = 20; net increase still 75"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The notes leave a non-cash purchase undisclosed."},{"type":"text","text":" Their balance sheets show equipment rising from 600 to 800 after depreciation of 100, so 800 − 600 + 100 = 300 of equipment was added, and a note payable due 2019 of 300 appears where there was none. No cash line covers either, which fits equipment bought with the note, though the notes do not say so. PSAK 207 keeps such transactions out of the statement and requires them to be disclosed elsewhere (para. 43); BRPT does this in its Note 44."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"NCI is reconciled, not reported."},{"type":"text","text":" NCI moves from 80 to 110, that is 80 + 50 − 20. The 50 is non-cash attribution and the 20 is a financing outflow. Nothing about NCI belongs in operating cash flows except the profit inside the starting figure."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. The direct method"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The direct method lists gross receipts and payments, so the NCI does not appear in operating activities at all. The notes rebuild Pop's 2017 operating cash flow from the same statements."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cash received from customers = sales 750 − increase in receivables 105 = 645"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividends received from equity investee = 10"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cash paid to suppliers = cost of goods sold 300 + increase in inventories 45 + decrease in payables 20 = 365"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paid to employees 54, other operating expenses 47, interest 24"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net cash from operating activities = 645 + 10 − (365 + 54 + 47 + 24) = 655 − 490 = 165"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 207 encourages the direct method (para. 19). BRPT uses it (Note 3b, PDF p. 67), so its operating section runs from receipts from customers of 7,939,631 to net operating cash of 309,608, and its profit attributable to NCI appears nowhere in the statement."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Cash flows with the non-controlling interests"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every cash flow between the group and its NCI is a transaction with owners, so it belongs in financing. That covers three kinds of flow."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Event"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated classification"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Basis"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Subsidiary pays dividends to NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Financing outflow"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Para. 34 permits financing or operating; the notes use financing"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Subsidiary issues new shares to NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Financing inflow"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Para. 17, contributions from owners"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Parent buys shares from NCI, or sells shares to new NCI, control kept"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Financing outflow or inflow"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paras. 42A–42B"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A parent's purchase of more shares in a subsidiary is an investing outflow in its separate statements. In the consolidated statements it is a payment to one class of the group's own owners, since the subsidiary's assets were already fully consolidated. European enforcers required an issuer that had shown such a purchase in investing to move it to financing (ESMA, decision EECS/0119-01)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), P buys a further 10 percent of S from the NCI for 12,000 and keeps control."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"P's separate statement: investing outflow (12,000), acquisition of investment in subsidiary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated statement: financing outflow (12,000), acquisition of non-controlling interest"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equity: NCI falls by its carrying amount for 10 percent; the difference goes to equity attributable to the parent (essay 25, section 5)"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The reverse runs through the same activity. When a subsidiary issues shares to new NCI, the cash is a financing inflow even though the parent's percentage falls. Take BRPT's subsidiary PT Chandra Daya Investasi Tbk (CDI) as the starting point. It issued shares to Phoenix Power B.V. for 95,000 (Note 31, PDF p. 217) and listed on the stock exchange in July 2025, raising 77,036 + 68,428 − 3,880 of issue costs = 141,584 (Note 1, PDF p. 36). Together they cut CAP's interest in CDI from 70 to 60 percent. Both are financing inflows; BRPT shows the second as \"proceeds from limited public offering of a subsidiary\"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"BRPT's line for subscriptions by NCI, 367,032, can be largely split from Note 1, though BRPT does not split it (inference). Glencore subscribed 146,690 for its 20 percent of CAPGC on 26 March 2025 (PDF p. 43), Phoenix paid 95,000, and the remaining 367,032 − 146,690 − 95,000 = 125,342 is close to BPN's pro-rata share of the capital injected into CSI and MIM on 1 October 2025 (section 9). The Glencore money came in at CAPGC, the company that bought ACE a week later, so NCI financing paid for part of an investing outflow."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Obtaining control: one investing line, net of cash acquired"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When the group buys a subsidiary it acquires that company's assets and liabilities in one transaction, and PSAK 207 presents the whole transaction as a single investing line (para. 39). The amount is the cash paid less the cash and cash equivalents the subsidiary already held (para. 42), because that cash comes into the group with it. The note must disclose the total consideration, the part paid in cash, the cash in the subsidiary, and the other assets and liabilities by major category (para. 40)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"BRPT's acquisition of ACE gives every element (Note 43, PDF p. 227). The consideration is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"provisional"},{"type":"text","text":", meaning the price is still subject to final adjustment, here through arbitration with the seller."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Provisional consideration = 671,970"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Less deferred consideration, not yet paid = 283,225"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cash paid on acquisition = 671,970 − 283,225 = 388,745"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Less ACE's cash and cash equivalents acquired = 215,431"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net cash outflow = 388,745 − 215,431 = 173,314"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"With 6,377 for Aster Polymer Solutions Pte. Ltd. (APS), bought on 1 August (13,076 paid less 6,699 acquired, PDF p. 229), the investing line is 173,314 + 6,377 = 179,691. The 283,225 still owed is a non-cash investing transaction in Note 44 (PDF p. 235). IAS 7 does not say how its later payment is classified. Practice varies between investing, financing and a split, and IASB staff proposed in September 2026 that deferred consideration with the character of a borrowing be financing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The acquired balances bypass operating activities."},{"type":"text","text":" A statement built from consolidated balance sheets must strip out the balances that arrived with the subsidiary, or their arrival looks like operating activity. Take BRPT's inventories as the starting point: 398,837 at the end of 2024 and 1,273,619 at the end of 2025 (statement of financial position, PDF p. 14), with 970,281 acquired through ACE and 39,608 through APS (Note 43, PDF pp. 226 and 228)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Change on the balance sheet = 1,273,619 − 398,837 = 874,782 increase"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Inventories acquired = 970,281 + 39,608 = 1,009,889"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Change from operations = 874,782 − 1,009,889 = 135,107 decrease"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read naively, the balance sheet suggests 874,782 tied up in stock. With the acquisitions removed, inventories released 135,107 of cash (ignoring write-downs and translation). Likewise ACE's acquired property, plant and equipment of 1,604,141 is not capital expenditure."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Bargain purchase gains bring no cash."},{"type":"text","text":" A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"bargain purchase"},{"type":"text","text":" arises when the identifiable net assets acquired are worth more than the price. ACE's were valued at 2,444,134 against consideration of 671,970, a gain of 1,772,164; APS added 95,875 (108,951 − 13,076). BRPT's income statement shows 1,868,039 of such gains (PDF p. 17). An indirect reconciliation deducts all of it, and in BRPT's direct statement it never appears. How a bargain purchase is tested belongs to K1."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Losing control and the full map of ownership events"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Losing control mirrors obtaining it: one investing line, the proceeds received net of the cash the former subsidiary takes out of the group (paras. 39 and 42)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), continue essay 25's subsidiary: net assets of 425,000 including cash of 35,000 (an assumption for this page), goodwill 60,000, NCI 170,000. The parent sells 40 percent for 230,000 cash and keeps 20 percent, worth 115,000, as an associate."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gain on loss of control = (230,000 + 115,000 + 170,000) − (425,000 + 60,000) = 30,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investing inflow = 230,000 − 35,000 = 195,000, disposal of subsidiary, net of cash disposed"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Retained 20 percent at 115,000, and the gain of 30,000: non-cash, removed in the indirect method"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Had the subsidiary held more cash than the sale raised, the line would be a net investing outflow. Cash flows from obtaining and losing control are not offset against each other (para. 41)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ownership event"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Control"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated cash flow"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gain or loss"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Buy a subsidiary"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"gained"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investing, net of cash acquired"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Only on a previously held interest or a bargain purchase"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Buy shares from NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kept"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Financing"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"None, equity"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sell shares, keep control"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kept"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Financing"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"None, equity"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Subsidiary issues shares to new NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kept"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Financing"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"None, equity"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sell enough to lose control"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"lost"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investing, net of cash disposed"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"In profit or loss"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Acquisitions during the year: preacquisition earnings"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A subsidiary's income before the acquisition date was earned for its former owners and was paid for in the purchase price. The notes call it "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"preacquisition earnings"},{"type":"text","text":", and the dividends paid out of it before that date "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"preacquisition dividends"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The notes' example has Pop buying 90 percent of Sun for 427,500 on 1 April 2016. (The notes write Son, Sun and Pam for the same companies, and the first column of their table reads \"Jan 1 – Mar 1\" where the quarter ends on 31 March.)"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sun's profit: 1 January–31 March 25,000; 1 April–31 December 75,000; year 100,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sun's dividends: before 1 April 20,000; after 30,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sun's equity at 1 April = 470,000 + 25,000 − 20,000 = 475,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost = 90% × 475,000 = 427,500, so there is no excess to allocate"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Approach 2 overstates sales by 50,000."},{"type":"text","text":" The notes describe two ways to exclude preacquisition earnings: leave Sun's revenue and expenses before 1 April out of the consolidated income statement, or include Sun's full year and deduct \"preacquisition earnings\" as a separate line. They describe the second only in words; the column below is computed here from their data."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Approach 1: from acquisition date"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Approach 2: full year less preacquisition earnings (computed here)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sales"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"600,000 + 150,000 = 750,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"600,000 + 200,000 = 800,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Expenses"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"400,000 + 75,000 = 475,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"400,000 + 100,000 = 500,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Preacquisition earnings"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"none"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(25,000)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"275,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"275,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share, 10% × 75,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7,500"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7,500"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Controlling share"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"267,500"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"267,500"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The bottom line agrees; the top line does not. PSAK 110 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Consolidated Financial Statements"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) includes a subsidiary's income and expenses only from the date control is gained (para. B88), and BRPT's policy says so in nearly the same words (Note 3c, PDF p. 67). Approach 1 is the only one allowed. Approach 2 was the method ARB 51 called \"usually\" preferable (para. 11) until FAS 160 amended it. The notes' own worksheet already follows approach 1, eliminating Sun's first-quarter sales of 50,000 and cost of sales of 25,000."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"PSAK measures NCI on the acquisition date."},{"type":"text","text":" The notes measure NCI from 1 January, 10% × 470,000 plus 10% of the first quarter's profit, 49,500, and give NCI 10 percent of the full year's dividends, 5,000, of which 2,000 was paid before Pop arrived. PSAK 103 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Business Combinations"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3) measures NCI at the acquisition date, here at its share of net assets because book value equals fair value."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI at acquisition = 10% × 475,000 = 47,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI at 31 December = 47,500 + 7,500 − 10% × 30,000 = 52,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Notes' route: 49,500 + 7,500 − 5,000 = 52,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Check: 10% × Sun's equity at year end of 520,000 = 52,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The closing figure agrees, but the 2,000 of dividends paid in the first quarter is not a group transaction. It reduced Sun's net assets before the acquisition and so reduced the price. Under PSAK the worksheet eliminates Sun's equity at the acquisition date."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Capital stock 400,000; Dr Retained earnings at 1 April 75,000 / Cr Investment in Sun 427,500; Cr NCI 47,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Income from Sun 67,500 / Cr Dividends 27,000; Cr Investment in Sun 40,500"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The second entry corrects the notes, which print \"Dividend $18.000 / Investment in Son $49.500\". Pop received 90% × 30,000 = 27,000 after the acquisition, and the notes' own worksheet carries 27,000 and 40,500, which is what clears Pop's investment of 427,500 + 67,500 − 27,000 = 468,000."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Sun's cash enters through the acquisition line."},{"type":"text","text":" The consolidated cash flow statement follows the income statement: Sun's flows count from 1 April, and the purchase is one investing line. For example (illustration), if Sun held cash of 60,000 on 1 April:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investing outflow = 427,500 − 60,000 = 367,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividends to NCI in financing = 10% × 30,000 = 3,000, not the notes' 5,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"B64(q) moves preacquisition results into the notes."},{"type":"text","text":" Leaving the first quarter out makes the year hard to compare with the next, so PSAK 103 para. B64(q) asks for the acquiree's revenue and profit since the acquisition date, and the group's revenue and profit "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"pro forma"},{"type":"text","text":", that is, recomputed as if the acquisition had happened at the start of the year. BRPT gives both for ACE (Note 43, PDF pp. 227–228)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"ACE since 1 April: revenue 4,634,352, loss 337,222"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Group pro forma for 2025: revenue 8,735,677, profit 1,589,142, against reported 7,630,662 and 1,615,634"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pro forma less reported: revenue 1,105,015 higher, profit 26,492 lower"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"A part-sale is recorded on the day it happens."},{"type":"text","text":" The notes' Chapter 8 also lets a parent that sells 10 percent of a 90 percent subsidiary on 1 April record the sale as if it happened on 1 January. PSAK 110 attributes profit by the ownership actually held, so the sale is recorded on 1 April. With the subsidiary's profit of 36,000 for the year, the NCI share is 36,000 × (10% × 3/12 + 20% × 9/12) = 900 + 5,400 = 6,300, not 7,200, and the credit to the parent's equity is 40,000 − 32,900 = 7,100, not 8,000. The 40,000 received is a financing inflow either way."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Consolidated cash flows across industries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Investment entities do not report acquisitions net of cash."},{"type":"text","text":" A venture capital fund or similar entity that meets PSAK 110's investment-entity definition does not consolidate its investees but measures them at fair value through profit or loss (para. 31). Buying an investee is then an investing purchase of a financial asset, and para. 42A does not apply to its stakes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Asset acquisitions in a corporate wrapper still use the para. 39 line."},{"type":"text","text":" Concessions, plantation land and power projects are often bought by buying the company that holds them. If the company is not a business under PSAK 103, the purchase is an asset acquisition, with no goodwill or bargain purchase, but the buyer still obtains control of a subsidiary. BRPT's wind subsidiary bought 51 percent of PT Lombok Timur Bayu Energi and PT Sukabumi Bayu Energi in January 2024 as asset acquisitions, and their cash outflows of 3,373 and 4,055 are shown as investing activities (Note 43, PDF pp. 232–233)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Joint-venture investors show large non-cash deductions."},{"type":"text","text":" Equity-accounted ventures in property and infrastructure contribute profit without cash. BRPT's share of profit of associates and joint venture was 60,470 in 2025, and all dividends received were 5,168 (PDF pp. 17 and 19), so at least 60,470 − 5,168 = 55,302 of that profit stayed in the investees."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Presentation and analysis: Barito Pacific's cash flow statement and Notes 34, 43 and 44"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"BRPT's group-structure flows put more cash into the group from NCI than they took out, and seven lines of its direct-method statement show them (PDF p. 19)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2025"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Activity"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Acquisition of subsidiaries, net of cash acquired"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(179,691)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(104,608)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investing"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Additional investment in subsidiaries from NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(160,975)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investing"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Proceeds from NCI subscribing for shares in subsidiaries"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"367,032"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"35,578"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Financing"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Proceeds from limited public offering of a subsidiary (CDI's IPO)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"141,584"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Financing"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Payment of treasury stocks"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5,317)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Financing"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividends paid by subsidiaries to NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(60,121)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(68,367)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Financing"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividends paid by the Company"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5,000)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Financing"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The dividend line ties to the NCI movement in Note 34 (PDF p. 221), where \"dividend distributed and capital reduction by subsidiary to non-controlling interests\" is also 60,121 (68,367 in 2024). The treasury line equals the buybacks in the statement of changes in equity, 154 by BRPT and 5,163 by subsidiaries (PDF p. 18)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Profit and cash diverge for the NCI."},{"type":"text","text":" NCI was attributed 1,125,827 of the year's profit of 1,615,634 (PDF p. 17)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of profit = 1,125,827 / 1,615,634 = 69.7%, against 66,412 / 122,896 = 54.0% in 2024"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"BRPT's effective interest in ACE = (34.63% + 3.92%) × 80% = 38.55% × 80% = 30.84%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividends to NCI / profit attributable to NCI = 60,121 / 1,125,827 = 5.3%"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"BRPT holds Chandra Asri (CAP) directly and through Marigold at 38.55 percent (Note 1, PDF p. 24), and ACE sits under CAPGC, 80 percent owned within CAP. The NCI therefore took most of the non-cash bargain purchase gain of 1,868,039."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net cash from NCI = 367,032 + 141,584 − 60,121 − 160,975 − 5,163 = 282,357"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"BRPT paid no dividends to its own shareholders in 2025 and spent 154 buying back its own shares."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The 160,975 sits on a classification judgement."},{"type":"text","text":" It is the price paid on 1 October 2025 for the 51 percent of PT Chandra Shipping International (CSI) and PT Marina Indah Maritim (MIM) held by PT Buana Primatama Niaga (BPN); the shares went to CDI (Note 31, PDF p. 216; Note 1, PDF pp. 41–42). BRPT accounts for it as a business combination under common control, by the pooling method: the 321 difference between the price and the book value of net assets acquired, 161,296, goes to additional paid-in capital (Note 3e, PDF pp. 70–71; Note 29, PDF p. 212). The text supports that reading. BPN's beneficial owner is the ultimate shareholder of CDI (Note 45h, PDF p. 236), and PSAK 338 para. 09 excludes from its scope only purchases of NCI from holders not under the same common control. A business combination is a para. 39 item, hence investing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The other reading starts from the fact that CSI and MIM were already consolidated at 49 percent through control of their boards (Note 1, PDF pp. 41–42), so control did not change, which is the case para. 42A sends to financing. BRPT's own caption, \"from non-controlling interest\", describes it that way. On the same day the shareholders of CSI and MIM injected new capital pro rata, BPN's 51 percent share being about 138,000 (Note 1, PDF pp. 41–42), which likely sits in the 367,032 financing inflow while the payment to BPN sits in investing. The equity result is the same under both readings; only the activity differs. Read as financing, investing would be 2,434,204 − 160,975 = 2,273,229 and financing 3,524,898 − 160,975 = 3,363,923."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Related essays"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/cash-and-cash-equivalents","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"cash-and-cash-equivalents"},{"type":"text","text":": the definition of cash, the three activities, interest and dividend classification and IFRS 18."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"non-controlling-interest"},{"type":"text","text":": NCI measurement, attribution and the equity entries for ownership changes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"long-term-investments"},{"type":"text","text":": equity-accounted associates and joint ventures."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/share-capital","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-equity-component"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/share-capital","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"additional-paid-in-capital"},{"type":"text","text":": where differences from transactions with NCI and common-control restructurings sit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/consolidation/business-combinations-acquisition-method-goodwill-common-control","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"K1"},{"type":"text","text":": consideration, deferred consideration, bargain purchase, asset acquisitions and PSAK 338."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/consolidation/consolidation-worksheet-elimination-entries","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"K2"},{"type":"text","text":": the elimination entries this page's worksheet steps rely on."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/consolidation/mutual-holdings-complex-group-structures","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"K6"},{"type":"text","text":": effective interests in multi-layer groups such as BRPT–CAP–CAPGC."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Least-confident assertions"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The CSI/MIM classification is a judgement with support on both sides. That BPN's capital injection of about 138,000 sits in the 367,032 financing inflow is inference; BRPT does not split that line, and the US dollar amount of the CSI injection depends on the rate applied to the deed's rupiah figure."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The split of 367,032 into Glencore 146,690, Phoenix 95,000 and about 125,342 from BPN is inference. The BPN residual matches its pro-rata injection less the 11,623 of shares issued by converting payables (Note 44) only to within about 1,250."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The CDI dates conflict inside the FS. Note 31 dates the Phoenix issue 14 March 2025 and attributes the whole fall from 70 to 60 percent to it; Note 1 shows the 14 March deed raising authorised capital, the issue to Phoenix on 11 April (to 66.67 percent) and the IPO in July (to 60.0 percent). This page follows Note 1."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"That the ACE pro forma difference reflects ACE's first quarter assumes the pro forma adds only that quarter with no other adjustments. BRPT does not explain how it was compiled."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The inventory calculation in section 5 ignores write-downs, translation and measurement-period changes to ACE's provisional figures, so 135,107 approximates the operating movement; BRPT does not report it."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Barito Pacific Tbk dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2025 and 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (PDF pp. 14–16); statement of profit or loss and OCI (p. 17); statement of changes in equity (p. 18); statement of cash flows (p. 19); Note 1 ownership table (p. 24), CDI's IPO (p. 36), CSI and MIM (pp. 41–42), CAPGC and Glencore (p. 43); Note 3b–3c (pp. 67–68); Note 3e (pp. 70–71); Note 29 (p. 212); Note 31 (pp. 216–217); Note 34 (pp. 220–221); Note 43 (pp. 226–233); Note 44 (p. 235); Note 45h (p. 236). BRPT FS. Page numbers computed from the text extraction and checked against the PDF."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes this page follows: \"AKL – Chapter 4 – Consolidation Technique and Procedures\", section IV.5 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Consolidated statements of cash flows"},{"type":"text","text":" (pp. 10–13), and \"AKL – Chapter 8 – Consolidation: Change in Ownership Interest\", section VIII.1 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Acquisition during an accounting period"},{"type":"text","text":" and VIII.2B "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Sale of interest"},{"type":"text","text":" (pp. 1–4, 7–11) (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan), summarising Beams et al., "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advanced Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":". NOTES. Corrections made here: the indirect method starts from the controlling share and adds back the NCI share (PSAK 207 starts from profit for the year); the non-cash equipment and note payable of 300 are not disclosed; the first-quarter column reads \"Jan 1 – Mar 1\"; the first elimination entry reads \"Dividend $18.000 / Investment in Son $49.500\" against the worksheet's 27,000 and 40,500; NCI is measured from 1 January with full-year dividends (PSAK 103 measures it at the acquisition date); the full-year-less-preacquisition-earnings approach and the option to record a 1 April sale at 1 January are not permitted under PSAK 110. The notes do not cover acquisitions or disposals in the cash flow statement; sections 5 and 6 follow PSAK 207 paras. 39–42B."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 207 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Laporan Arus Kas"},{"type":"text","text":" (IAS 7): paras. 17, 18(b), 19, 20, 31, 33, 34, 39, 40, 40A, 41, 42, 42A, 42B, 43, 48 VERIFIED (web, AASB 107 compiled June 2023, standards.aasb.gov.au and aasb.gov.au). ESMA, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"23rd Extract from the EECS's Database of Enforcement"},{"type":"text","text":", ESMA32-63-717, decision EECS/0119-01 (para. 42A). VERIFIED (web)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"IASB staff paper AP20A, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Statement of Cash Flows and Related Matters: classification of acquisition-related payments"},{"type":"text","text":", September 2026, paras. 4, 27, 32. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org, per independent recheck)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 103 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis"},{"type":"text","text":" (IFRS 3), para. B64(q) VERIFIED (web, IASB agenda paper 18B, November 2021). PSAK 110 (IFRS 10), paras. 20, 31 and B88 VERIFIED (web, AASB 10 compiled January 2015). PSAK 338 para. 09 scope: as read in the K1 recheck, STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED here."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"ARB 51 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Consolidated Financial Statements"},{"type":"text","text":", para. 11, as before its amendment by FAS 160. VERIFIED (web, FASB storage copy and NY Attorney General copy, per independent recheck)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"IFRS 18 amendments to IAS 7 and PSAK 118 (effective 1 January 2027): as verified in essay 08, ref. 7."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Renumbering PSAK 2 → 207, PSAK 22 → 103, PSAK 65 → 110: VERIFIED (web, IAI, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia"},{"type":"text","text":"), as in essays 08 and 25."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beams, Anthony, Bettinghaus and Smith, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advanced Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":", chs. 4 and 8. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED."}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb
