-- consolidation-worksheet-elimination-entries: content_json backfill (the document content was serialised from)
with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated statements are never recorded in anyone's ledger. They are built on a worksheet that adds the parent's and the subsidiaries' statements line by line and then removes what is internal to the group: the parent's investment against the subsidiary's equity, intragroup balances, and intragroup income, expenses and dividends (PSAK 110 B86)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The elimination entries run in a fixed order. Reverse the parent's income from the subsidiary and the subsidiary's dividends, record the non-controlling interest's (NCI's) share, eliminate opening equity against the investment and opening NCI, allocate and depreciate the excess paid over book value, and cancel reciprocal balances. The worksheet has three parts, profit or loss, then retained earnings, then the balance sheet, each feeding the next."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The method the parent uses in its own books (cost or equity method, both allowed by PSAK 227) changes the first entry and adds one conversion entry, but never the consolidated result. A worksheet that balances can still be wrong, so the ties to the parent's equity and to NCI matter more than the totals."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Barito Pacific Tbk and subsidiaries (BRPT) list each subsidiary's \"total assets before elimination\" in their 2025 statements. PT Barito Renewables Energy Tbk (BREN), 64.67 percent owned, has 3,867,410 at 31 December 2025, in USD thousand (BRPT FS 2025, Note 1d, PDF p. 26). What happens between such \"before elimination\" figures and the group's statements is the subject of this page."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After reading it a student should be able to prepare a three-part consolidation worksheet in the year of acquisition and in later years, write each elimination entry in order, adapt the worksheet to a parent that uses the cost method, and find the error when the worksheet does not balance."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from BRPT's and BREN's statements are cited to their note; every other number is an illustration. Control, NCI measurement and profit attribution are taught in the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"Non-Controlling Interest essay"},{"type":"text","text":" and used here without re-deriving them."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. The parent's own books decide what must be eliminated"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The parent's investment in a subsidiary sits in its own books as one asset. How that asset is measured in the parent's "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"separate financial statements"},{"type":"text","text":" (the parent's own statements, in which subsidiaries are investments) is a policy choice. PSAK 227 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Separate Financial Statements"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 4, IAS 27) allows cost, PSAK 109 or the equity method, applied to each category of investment consistently (para. 10). The equity-method option was added in Indonesia by the amendment to PSAK 4 ratified in November 2015 and effective from 1 January 2016, following the 2014 IAS 27 amendment. Dividends from a subsidiary go to profit or loss under cost or PSAK 109, and reduce the investment under the equity method (para. 12)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The lecture notes (AKL Chapter 4) assume the parent uses the equity method \"secara komplit\", the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"complete equity method"},{"type":"text","text":". The parent books its share of the subsidiary's profit after the depreciation of the excess it paid over book value, so its profit equals the consolidated profit attributable to its owners. Beams calls this "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"one-line consolidation"},{"type":"text","text":"; the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"Long-Term Investments essay"},{"type":"text","text":" (section 5) explains the mechanism."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The running illustration."},{"type":"text","text":" BRPT holds 64.67 percent of BREN (Note 1d). Its interest in BREN's geothermal business came through common-control transactions. BRPT acquired Star Energy Group Holdings (SEGHPL) in 2018, pooled as if combined from the start (Note 1d, PDF p. 52) at book values (policy Note 3e, PDF p. 70), and BREN acquired SEGHPL from BRPT in 2022 for new BREN shares (BREN FS 2025, Note 19b, PDF p. 115). No fair value excess arose at BRPT's level, although goodwill from Star Energy's earlier purchase of Salak and Darajat was carried across at its carrying amount; the Business Combinations essay (K1) covers both. To show every entry, suppose instead (illustration) that on 1 January 2024 BRPT had bought 65 percent of a geothermal company from outside owners. The notes call their subsidiary \"Son\"; this page calls it Sub. A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"fair value uplift"},{"type":"text","text":" is the amount by which an asset's fair value at acquisition exceeds its carrying amount in the subsidiary's books."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consideration paid = 520,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sub's book equity = share capital 400,000 + retained earnings 200,000 = 600,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fair value uplifts: power plant +100,000 (20 years left, so 5,000 a year); spare-parts inventory +10,000 (used in 2024)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Identifiable net assets at fair value = 600,000 + 100,000 + 10,000 = 710,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI at its proportionate share = 35% × 710,000 = 248,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill = 520,000 + 248,500 − 710,000 = 58,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excess over book value = 520,000 + 248,500 − 600,000 = 168,500 = 100,000 + 10,000 + 58,500"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 103 lets the acquirer choose fair value or the proportionate share for NCI, deal by deal (essay 25, section 2). The proportionate share is used here. Deferred tax on the uplifts is ignored to keep the steps short; K1 adds it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sub earns 90,000 and pays dividends of 40,000 in 2024, then earns 110,000 and declares dividends of 50,000 in 2025, of which 20,000 is still unpaid at year end."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024 profit as the group sees it = 90,000 − 10,000 inventory − 5,000 plant = 75,000; parent 65% = 48,750; NCI 35% = 26,250"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2025 profit as the group sees it = 110,000 − 5,000 plant = 105,000; parent 65% = 68,250; NCI 35% = 36,750"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Parent's books"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost method"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equity method"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1 Jan 2024, purchase"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in Sub 520,000 / Cr Cash 520,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"same"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024 profit of Sub"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"no entry"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in Sub 48,750 / Cr Income from Sub 48,750"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024 dividend, 65% × 40,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 26,000 / Cr Dividend income 26,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 26,000 / Cr Investment in Sub 26,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2025 profit of Sub"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"no entry"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in Sub 68,250 / Cr Income from Sub 68,250"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2025 dividend, 65% × 50,000 = 32,500"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 19,500, Dr Dividend receivable 13,000 / Cr Dividend income 32,500"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 19,500, Dr Dividend receivable 13,000 / Cr Investment in Sub 32,500"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investment at 31 Dec 2025"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"520,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"520,000 + 48,750 − 26,000 + 68,250 − 32,500 = 578,500"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The equity-method balance already contains the parent's share of Sub's retained profits and of the depreciated uplift. The cost-method balance contains neither, so the worksheet has to put them there before it can eliminate anything (section 6)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. The worksheet has three parts, and each feeds the next"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Each part of the worksheet ends in the figure the next part needs. The notes stack three parts (income statement, retained earnings statement, balance sheet), with columns for the parent, the subsidiary, the eliminations and the consolidated result."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit attributable to the owners of the parent, the last line of part one, is carried into part two."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Closing retained earnings, the last line of part two, is carried into part three."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Part three must then balance, total assets against total liabilities and equity."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 201 replaced the retained earnings statement with a statement of changes in equity, but the logic is unchanged. Every year starts again from the separate statements and repeats the entries for past events, such as the acquisition-date allocation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 110 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Consolidated Financial Statements"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) describes the three steps the entries perform (B86): combine like items of assets, liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows; offset the parent's investment against its portion of the subsidiary's equity; and eliminate intragroup assets, liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows in full. BRPT's policy repeats the third step: \"All intragroup assets and liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows relating to transactions between members of the Group are eliminated in full on consolidation\" (Note 3c, PDF p. 68)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. The standard elimination sequence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The notes give six entries in a fixed order. Each removes something that would otherwise be counted twice."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Step"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Problem it solves"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Entry (equity-method parent)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(1) Income from subsidiary and dividends"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The parent's income line repeats Sub's revenues and expenses; dividends to the parent move cash inside the group"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Income from Sub / Cr Dividends declared (Sub, parent's share); Cr Investment for the difference"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(2) NCI share"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated profit belongs partly to outside owners, and their dividends reduce their claim"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr NCI share of profit / Cr Dividends declared (NCI's share); Cr NCI for the difference"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(3) Opening equity against investment and NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sub's opening equity is already represented by the investment and opening NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Share capital and opening retained earnings (Sub), Dr Unamortised excess / Cr Investment (opening), Cr NCI (opening)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(4) Allocate the excess"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"unamortised excess"},{"type":"text","text":" is a worksheet suspense account to be assigned to the assets it paid for"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Plant and equipment, Dr Goodwill (and inventory or cost of sales) / Cr Unamortised excess"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5) Depreciate the excess"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Group depreciation follows acquisition-date fair values (PSAK 110 B88); Sub depreciates book values"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Depreciation / Cr Plant and equipment"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(6) Reciprocal balances"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Balances between group companies are neither assets nor liabilities of the group"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr the payable / Cr the receivable; Dr intragroup income / Cr intragroup expense"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Entries (1) and (2) together remove all of Sub's dividends. Entry (1) returns the investment to its opening balance, which is why entry (3) can eliminate it against opening equity. The intragroup profit eliminations of K3 and K4 can sit anywhere in the sequence without changing the result."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. The year of acquisition"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"In the first year the opening equity in entry (3) is the equity at the acquisition date, and the inventory uplift reaches cost of sales because the spares were used during the year. For example (illustration), the 2024 worksheet for Sub:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(1) Dr Income from Sub 48,750 / Cr Dividends declared 26,000; Cr Investment in Sub 22,750"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(2) Dr NCI share of profit 26,250 / Cr Dividends declared 14,000; Cr NCI 12,250"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(3) Dr Share capital, Sub 400,000; Dr Retained earnings, Sub 200,000; Dr Unamortised excess 168,500 / Cr Investment in Sub 520,000; Cr NCI 248,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(4) Dr Cost of sales 10,000; Dr Plant and equipment 100,000; Dr Goodwill 58,500 / Cr Unamortised excess 168,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5) Dr Depreciation 5,000 / Cr Plant and equipment 5,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Entry (3) balances at 768,500 on each side. The investment, 520,000 + 48,750 − 26,000 = 542,750, is credited 22,750 + 520,000 = 542,750 and falls to zero. NCI closes at 248,500 + 12,250 = 260,750, which is 35 percent of Sub's net assets at group carrying amounts (book values plus the uplifts not yet written off)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"35% × (600,000 + 90,000 − 40,000 + 100,000 − 5,000) = 35% × 745,000 = 260,750"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under the proportionate method goodwill is not part of NCI. Had control been obtained during the year, Sub's income and expenses would enter only from the acquisition date (B88), a case K5 takes up."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. A later year: the full worksheet"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"From the second year on, entry (3) uses Sub's retained earnings at the start of the year and the excess still unamortised at that date. In 2025 three intragroup transactions also need entry (6)."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"On 1 July 2025 the parent lent Sub 30,000 at 6 percent a year. Half a year's interest, 30,000 × 6% × 6/12 = 900, is accrued by both and unpaid."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"On 30 December Sub repaid 5,000 of the loan by bank transfer, which reached the parent on 2 January. Sub shows a loan payable of 25,000, while the parent still shows a receivable of 30,000. The 5,000 is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"cash in transit"},{"type":"text","text":", money that has left one group company and not yet reached the other."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sub's unpaid dividend of 20,000 includes 13,000 owed to the parent."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), the opening amounts for 2025:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sub retained earnings, 1 Jan 2025 = 200,000 + 90,000 − 40,000 = 250,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unamortised excess, 1 Jan 2025 = 168,500 − 10,000 − 5,000 = 153,500 (plant 95,000 + goodwill 58,500)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investment, 1 Jan 2025 = 542,750; NCI, 1 Jan 2025 = 260,750"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The entries:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(1) Dr Income from Sub 68,250 / Cr Dividends declared 32,500; Cr Investment in Sub 35,750"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(2) Dr NCI share of profit 36,750 / Cr Dividends declared 17,500; Cr NCI 19,250"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(3) Dr Share capital, Sub 400,000; Dr Retained earnings 1 Jan, Sub 250,000; Dr Unamortised excess 153,500 / Cr Investment in Sub 542,750; Cr NCI 260,750"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(4) Dr Plant and equipment 95,000; Dr Goodwill 58,500 / Cr Unamortised excess 153,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5) Dr Depreciation 5,000 / Cr Plant and equipment 5,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(6a) Dr Cash in transit 5,000 / Cr Loan to Sub 5,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(6b) Dr Loan from parent 25,000 / Cr Loan to Sub 25,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(6c) Dr Interest payable 900 / Cr Interest receivable 900"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(6d) Dr Interest income 900 / Cr Finance costs 900"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(6e) Dr Dividend payable 13,000 / Cr Dividend receivable 13,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Entry (6a) is an adjustment, not an elimination; the reciprocal balances must agree before (6b) cancels them. The notes handle a cheque in transit the same way, with \"Cash $20.000 / Note receivable – Son $20.000\" ahead of the eliminations."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The worksheet, Parent and Sub, year ended 31 December 2025 (illustration)."},{"type":"text","text":" Credit balances in the income statement and equity are shown positive, expenses and dividends in parentheses; the elimination columns show the entry number."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Parent"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sub"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cr"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"para