-- deferred-tax-assets-deductible-differences-and-recoverability: content_json backfill (the document content was serialised from)
with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A deferred tax asset exists because the income statement and the tax return recognise the same cost in different years. When a cost is expensed in the books before the tax law lets it be deducted, the future deduction is worth the tax it will save, measured at the rate expected when it is used."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unlike a deferred tax liability, a deferred tax asset is recognised only to the extent that taxable profit is probable in the same taxable entity, under the same tax authority, before the deduction expires. Unused tax losses face the strictest version of that test."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax assets are always non-current, are not discounted, and are offset against deferred tax liabilities of the same taxpayer where the law allows set-off. The reported line is therefore a net figure per taxpayer, and in an upstream group most deductible differences sit inside the liability line."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report deferred tax assets of 55,781 at 31 December 2024 against 95,263 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 0.18 percent of total assets at the end of 2024 (0.31 percent in 2023). This page also covers deferred tax liabilities, 2,894,578 against 2,625,374 (Note 43e), which are 9.5 percent of total assets and 51.9 times the asset line (27.6 times in 2023); section 11 explains the gap. It also covers the profit or loss line income tax expense, net, 1,771,541 against 1,766,491 (Note 43c)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After reading it a student should be able to compute deferred tax assets and liabilities from temporary differences, test whether an asset can be recognised, remeasure both for a rate change, record a tax loss, reconcile tax expense to accounting profit, and read a tax note's deferred tax table."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Every number not cited to PHE is labelled an illustration."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Two measures of profit"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every deferred tax balance exists because a company measures profit twice. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Accounting profit"},{"type":"text","text":" (Kieso calls it "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"pretax financial income"},{"type":"text","text":") is profit before tax under SAK, prepared for investors and creditors. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Taxable profit"},{"type":"text","text":" is profit as the tax law defines it, and it determines the tax payable to the state. PSAK 212 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Income Taxes"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12) calls the tax on taxable profit "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"current tax"},{"type":"text","text":" and the total charged to profit or loss "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"tax expense"},{"type":"text","text":", made up of current tax plus deferred tax (paras. 5–6)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The two measures differ in two ways. A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"permanent difference"},{"type":"text","text":" enters one measure and never the other. A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"temporary difference"},{"type":"text","text":" enters both, in different years, so it reverses. Kieso explains the gap as accrual books against cash-basis tax, but Indonesian taxable profit is largely accrual-based. The differences come from specific rules: provisions deductible only when paid, tax depreciation on its own schedule, income taxed under a final regime."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Future taxable amounts: the deferred tax liability as the mirror"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When the tax return deducts a cost earlier than the books, the company pays less tax now and more later. The later tax is a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"deferred tax liability"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys equipment for 90,000 with a three-year life. The books depreciate it straight line, 30,000 a year. The tax rules allow 45,000, 36,000 and 9,000. Profit before depreciation is 110,000 a year, and the combined tax rate is 40 percent (section 6 explains where a PSC rate comes from)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Accounting profit = 110,000 − 30,000 = 80,000 in each year"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Taxable profit = 110,000 − 45,000 = 65,000; 110,000 − 36,000 = 74,000; 110,000 − 9,000 = 101,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Current tax = 26,000; 29,600; 40,400 (total 96,000)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Carrying amount at year end = 60,000; 30,000; 0. Tax base = 45,000; 9,000; 0"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax liability = (60,000 − 45,000) × 40% = 6,000; (30,000 − 9,000) × 40% = 8,400; 0"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax expense = 6,000; 8,400 − 6,000 = 2,400; 0 − 8,400 = −8,400"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Year 1"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Year 2"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Year 3"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Income tax expense"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"32,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"32,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"32,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Deferred tax liability"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8,400"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cr Deferred tax liability"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,400"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cr Income tax payable"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"26,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"29,600"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"40,400"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tax expense is 32,000 every year, which is 40 percent of the 80,000 accounting profit, while the cash tax swings from 26,000 to 40,400. Deferred tax exists so that expense follows the profit reported while the timing of payment sits on the balance sheet."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Future deductible amounts: the deferred tax asset"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A deferred tax asset is the same mechanism run in reverse. When the books expense a cost before the tax law deducts it, the company pays more tax now and less later. The tax it will save is an asset (PSAK 212.5, 24)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 212 measures it from the balance sheet. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"tax base"},{"type":"text","text":" of an asset is the amount deductible against future taxable income; the tax base of a liability is its carrying amount less whatever will be deductible when it is settled (paras. 7–8). A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"deductible temporary difference"},{"type":"text","text":" is a gap that will reduce future taxable profit, and the deferred tax asset is that gap times the rate."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The lecture notes this series follows illustrate it with an environmental clean-up. The largest item in PHE's deferred tax asset line is a provision of the same family, for decommissioning and site restoration, of 66,352 (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172). Unlike the clean-up, it is capitalised into the asset, so the same transaction also creates a taxable difference (section 10)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), in 2024 a PHE subsidiary is ordered to restore an area damaged by drilling, and an appraisal puts the cost at 5,000. The work is done and paid in 2025, and assume the tax law deducts the cost when paid. Revenue is 200,000 and other operating expenses 120,000 in both years."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Books: 2024 profit = 200,000 − 120,000 − 5,000 = 75,000; 2025 profit = 80,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tax: 2024 taxable = 80,000; 2025 taxable = 75,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Current tax at 40% = 32,000 (2024); 30,000 (2025)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"End 2024: provision carrying amount 5,000, tax base 5,000 − 5,000 = 0, deductible difference 5,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax asset = 5,000 × 40% = 2,000"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 Dec 2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 Dec 2025"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Restoration expense 5,000 / Cr Restoration provision 5,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Restoration provision 5,000 / Cr Cash 5,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Income tax expense 30,000, Dr Deferred tax asset 2,000 / Cr Income tax payable 32,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Income tax expense 32,000 / Cr Deferred tax asset 2,000, Cr Income tax payable 30,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tax expense is 30,000 in 2024 (40 percent of 75,000) and 32,000 in 2025 (40 percent of 80,000). In 2024 the company paid 2,000 of tax that belongs to 2025, and the asset carries it until the deduction arrives."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Recoverability: when a deferred tax asset can be recognised"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A deferred tax liability is recognised for almost every taxable temporary difference, because the tax will be payable whatever happens (para. 15). A deferred tax asset is different. A deduction saves tax only if there is taxable income to deduct it from. So PSAK 212 recognises the asset only to the extent that taxable profit against which the deduction can be used is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"probable"},{"type":"text","text":" (para. 24, 27), which practice reads as more likely than not."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Where the taxable profit can come from."},{"type":"text","text":" The standard ranks the evidence (paras. 28–29)."}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Taxable temporary differences"},{"type":"text","text":" in the same taxable entity, under the same tax authority, that reverse in the same periods. They are the strongest support."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Forecast taxable profit"},{"type":"text","text":" in those periods, ignoring new deductible differences."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Tax planning opportunities"},{"type":"text","text":", actions the entity would take to create taxable profit in time (para. 30)."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Where tax law restricts which income a deduction may be used against, only income of that type counts (para. 27A). A PSC's ring-fence is that kind of restriction, a point section 12 returns to."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Reducing the asset."},{"type":"text","text":" The test is repeated at every reporting date. The asset is reduced to the extent that profit is no longer probable, and the reduction is reversed if profit becomes probable again (paras. 37, 56). For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary outside PSC activities, taxed at 22 percent, has deductible differences of 30,000. Its forecast shows only 20,000 of taxable profit in the reversal periods."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Full asset = 30,000 × 22% = 6,600"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Recognisable = 20,000 × 22% = 4,400; not recognised = 6,600 − 4,400 = 2,200"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"US GAAP records the full asset with a valuation allowance; PSAK 212 recognises only the recoverable amount."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Allowance method (US GAAP)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Direct method (PSAK 212)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Deferred tax asset 6,600 / Cr Deferred tax benefit 6,600"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Deferred tax asset 4,400 / Cr Deferred tax benefit 4,400"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Deferred tax expense 2,200 / Cr Allowance to reduce deferred tax asset 2,200"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"no entry"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net on balance sheet 4,400"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4,400"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE says it \"provides a valuation allowance\" where losses may expire unused (Note 43e, Daftar 5/156, PDF p. 173), but its table shows a negative row, \"unrecognized deferred tax assets\", of 15,545 at the end of 2024 against 20,052 a year earlier."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"When the estimate behind the difference changes."},{"type":"text","text":" In a second kind of reduction the difference itself shrinks. Suppose (illustration) that in mid-2025, before work starts, the appraisal of section 3 is cut from 5,000 to 2,500."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax asset = 2,500 × 40% = 1,000, down from 2,000"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Restoration provision 2,500 / Cr Restoration expense 2,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Deferred tax expense 1,000 / Cr Deferred tax asset 1,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under PSAK 208 (formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8) a revised estimate goes to profit or loss of the period of revision, even though the 2024 books are closed; only a prior-period "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"error"},{"type":"text","text":" is corrected through opening retained earnings."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Temporary and permanent differences"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Permanent differences"},{"type":"text","text":" change the effective tax rate and create no deferred tax, because nothing reverses. Kieso lists fines and penalties, donations and tax-exempt interest. In Indonesia the common ones are non-deductible costs, dividends that are exempt or already taxed, and income subject to final tax, which is excluded from taxable profit because a separate final tax covers it. Section 9 shows them in PHE's reconciliation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Temporary differences"},{"type":"text","text":" reverse. Kieso describes them as timing differences between book and tax. PSAK 212 defines them through the balance sheet, which captures more, because it counts any gap between carrying amount and tax base, including revaluations and business-combination adjustments (paras. 5, 17–18)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary computes its 2024 tax."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Accounting profit before tax = 100,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Permanent: non-deductible costs + 2,000; interest subject to final tax − 1,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Temporary: restoration provision not yet deductible + 1,000; tax depreciation above book − 3,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Taxable profit = 100,000 + 2,000 − 1,500 + 1,000 − 3,000 = 98,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Current tax = 98,500 × 40% = 39,400"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax asset = 1,000 × 40% = 400; deferred tax liability = 3,000 × 40% = 1,200"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tax expense = 39,400 + 1,200 − 400 = 40,200"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Income tax expense 40,200, Dr Deferred tax asset 400 / Cr Deferred tax liability 1,200, Cr Income tax payable 39,400"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The check is (100,000 + 2,000 − 1,500) × 40% = 40,200. The temporary differences change only the timing of tax. The permanent ones move the effective rate to 40,200 / 100,000 = 40.2 percent."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Tax rate considerations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Which rate."},{"type":"text","text":" A deferred tax asset is measured at the rate expected to apply when it is used, based on rates "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"enacted or substantively enacted"},{"type":"text","text":" (passed in all but formality) at the reporting date (para. 47). It is not discounted, however far away the reversal (para. 53). When the rate varies by year, the reversal is scheduled. For example (illustration), a deductible difference of 9,000 reverses 3,000 a year in 2025–2027 under a law that sets 22 percent for 2025 and 20 percent from 2026."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax asset = 3,000 × 22% + 3,000 × 20% + 3,000 × 20% = 660 + 600 + 600 = 1,860"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"A change in the rate."},{"type":"text","text":" When a new rate is enacted, existing balances are remeasured, and the effect goes to tax expense in the year of enactment, except where the original item went to other comprehensive income or equity (paras. 60–61A). In Indonesia, Law 2/2020 cut the corporate rate to 22 percent and scheduled 20 percent from 2022; the HPP law of October 2021 (Law 7/2021) kept 22 percent. For example (illustration), an entity outside PSC activities holds deductible differences of 10,000 reversing from 2022."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"End 2020, at the enacted 20% = 2,000. End 2021, at 22% = 2,200"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Deferred tax asset 200 / Cr Deferred tax benefit 200"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The PSC rule."},{"type":"text","text":" A production sharing contract (PSC) fixes its own tax terms. PHE's policy states that deferred taxes from activities outside PSCs use the rate enacted at the reporting date, while those related to PSC activities use \"the tax rates applied on the effective date of the PSC or the extension date or date of amendment\" (Note 3s, Daftar 5/69, PDF p. 86). The rate combines corporate income tax with a tax on profit after corporate tax, which PHE calls dividend tax (Note 2a(ii), Daftar 5/9, PDF p. 26)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A PSC signed when corporate tax was 25 percent kept its 25% + 20% × (1 − 25%) = 40% through the HPP change; its rate moves only when the contract does. For example (illustration), a PSC holding deductible differences of 50,000 at 40 percent is extended on terms that apply the prevailing law."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"New rate = 22% + 20% × (1 − 22%) = 37.6%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax asset = 50,000 × 37.6% = 18,800, down from 50,000 × 40% = 20,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Deferred tax expense 1,200 / Cr Deferred tax asset 1,200"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Net operating losses: carryback and carryforward"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A tax loss is a deduction that arrived before the income. A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"carryback"},{"type":"text","text":" applies it to earlier years' taxable profit and reclaims tax paid; a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"carryforward"},{"type":"text","text":" applies it to future years."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso (US rules)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Indonesia"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Carryback"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"two years; refund of past tax is a current tax asset (PSAK 212.13)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"not allowed"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Carryforward"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"twenty years"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"five years (UU PPh art. 6(2)); ten for gross split PSCs (PP 53/2017)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSC cost recovery"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"n/a"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"unrecovered costs carried within the contract"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Without a carryback, an Indonesian loss produces at most a deferred tax asset, and only if the probable-profit test of section 4 is met. Because a loss is \"strong evidence that future taxable profit may not be available\", an entity with recent losses needs sufficient taxable temporary differences or \"convincing other evidence\" (para. 35), judged against the expiry date, the causes of the loss and tax planning (para. 36)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary outside PSC activities has a 2024 tax loss of 50,000 at 22 percent, and signed sales contracts make profit before expiry probable."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024: Dr Deferred tax asset 11,000 / Cr Income tax benefit, loss carryforward 11,000 (50,000 × 22%)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2025: taxable profit before the loss = 30,000; loss used = 30,000; current tax = 0"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Deferred tax expense 6,600 / Cr Deferred tax asset 6,600 (30,000 × 22%)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Remaining asset = 20,000 × 22% = 4,400, usable until the end of 2029"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under a cost-recovery PSC, costs not yet recovered from production wait in the contractor's unrecovered-cost pool, so the five-year clock is replaced by the contract's own terms. PHE recognises deferred tax assets for both \"unused tax losses and unrecovered costs (for cost recovery PSC)\" under the same probable test (Note 3s, Daftar 5/69, PDF p. 86). Its 2024 table shows a tax-loss item of 67,614 inside the liability line used down to −1,313, a charge of 68,927 to profit or loss (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Presentation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Classification."},{"type":"text","text":" Deferred tax assets are never current, even the part that reverses next year (PSAK 201.56, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Offset."},{"type":"text","text":" Deferred tax assets and liabilities are offset only if the entity has a legally enforceable right to set off current tax balances and both relate to the same authority and the same taxable entity, or to entities that intend to settle net (PSAK 212.74). Indonesia has no group taxation, so each subsidiary is its own taxable entity and the consolidated line is the sum of the subsidiaries in a net asset position. For example (illustration), two subsidiaries, each netting its own balances:"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Subsidiary A"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Subsidiary B"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Group"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax assets, gross"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax liabilities, gross"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(1,000)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(20,000)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,000 asset"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"12,000 liability"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,000 asset and 12,000 liability, shown separately"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"B's 8,000 of deductions appear nowhere on the face of the statement."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Profit or loss and OCI."},{"type":"text","text":" Tax follows the item it relates to: to profit or loss, other comprehensive income or equity (para. 61A)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Disclosure."},{"type":"text","text":" Beyond the expense and its reconciliation (section 9), PSAK 212 asks for the deferred tax balances by type of difference (para. 81(g)), unrecognised deductible differences and losses with expiry dates (81(e)), and the evidence for an asset that depends on future profit after a loss (82)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Income tax expense and the effective tax rate"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The major components of income tax expense are disclosed separately (para. 79). Para. 80 lists what they may include: current tax, adjustments to current tax of prior periods, deferred tax from temporary differences, and separately the effects of rate changes, of losses or differences not recognised before, and of write-downs of deferred tax assets. PHE shows three lines (Note 43c, Daftar 5/152, PDF p. 169); its 2023 deferred line of 14,651 also absorbs a rate change of −11,720 and a release of unrecognised assets of 26,730 (Note 43e, PDF p. 173)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2023"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Current tax"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,473,547"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,847,853"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Adjustments in respect of prior years"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(96,013)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"297,994"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"14,651"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Income tax expense, net"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"1,771,541"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"1,766,491"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit before tax (Note 43d)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4,892,574"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4,492,029"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Current tax and its corrections, such as the 2023 credit of 96,013, belong to the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/corporate-dividend-taxes-receivable-current","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"corporate-dividend-taxes-receivable-current"},{"type":"text","text":" page. The deferred component is the profit-or-loss movement in the balances of sections 2–7, 38,095 + 259,899 = 297,994 in 2024 (section 11)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The effective rate."},{"type":"text","text":" The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"average effective tax rate"},{"type":"text","text":" is tax expense divided by accounting profit (para. 86)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024: 1,771,541 / 4,892,574 = 36.2%; 2023: 1,766,491 / 4,492,029 = 39.3%"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 212 asks for a reconciliation of tax expense to accounting profit times the applicable rate, or of the effective rate to the applicable rate, stating how the applicable rate is computed (para. 81(c)) and why it changed (81(d)). Temporary differences recognised at the applicable rate do not appear in it, because deferred tax charges each at its rate, as section 5's check showed. The rate moves with permanent differences, profit taxed at other rates, deductible differences left unrecognised, rate changes and corrections of earlier years."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Which applicable rate."},{"type":"text","text":" Often the most meaningful is the domestic rate where the entity is domiciled; an entity in several jurisdictions may aggregate reconciliations at each jurisdiction's rate (para. 85). Groups whose subsidiaries carry different contract rates, as PSCs do, often start instead from a weighted average of those rates, disclosing how it is computed (para. 81(c)). For example (illustration), a PSC subsidiary taxed at 40 percent earns profit before tax of 300,000 and has 5,000 of non-deductible costs; a subsidiary outside PSC activities, taxed at 22 percent, earns 100,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tax expense = (300,000 + 5,000) × 40% + 100,000 × 22% = 122,000 + 22,000 = 144,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Effective rate = 144,000 / 400,000 = 36.0%"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Domestic rate, 22%"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Weighted average of the subsidiaries' rates"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Applicable rate"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"22%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(300,000 × 40% + 100,000 × 22%) / 400,000 = 35.5%"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tax at applicable rate"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"88,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"142,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit taxed at the PSC rate"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"300,000 × 18% = 54,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"inside the rate"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Non-deductible costs"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5,000 × 40% = 2,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tax expense"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"144,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"144,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The first shows the PSC rate's excess over 22 percent as a line; the second hides it in a starting rate that moves whenever the profit mix between subsidiaries changes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE prints both forms (Note 43d, Daftar 5/153–5/154, PDF pp. 170–171). The parent-only reconciliation at 22 percent runs to nil, because the parent's untaxed dividend income, 409,211 / 22% = 1,860,050, exceeds its profit of 1,619,797, and the resulting loss is not recognised (\"others\", 76,665). The consolidated one follows."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tax at weighted average rate = 1,663,757"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividend income − 409,211; income from associates − 35,776; income subject to final tax − 61,467; PSC adjustments + 614,238"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tax expense = 1,663,757 − 409,211 − 35,776 − 61,467 + 614,238 = 1,771,541"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividends and final-taxed income pull the rate down. The PSC adjustments, which PHE does not break down, push it up; non-recoverable contract costs are one likely component. PHE gives the weighted average rate as 38 percent for 2024, while its own figures give 1,663,757 / 4,892,574 = 34.0 percent, or the starting figure is stale (see Least-confident). PHE gives neither the basis of the 38 percent nor the reason it rose from 37."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Deferred tax assets across industries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Final-tax industries: construction and real estate."},{"type":"text","text":" Some Indonesian income, such as land and building transfers and construction services, is taxed under a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"final tax"},{"type":"text","text":" on gross receipts. PHE's policy notes that final tax is outside PSAK 212 (Note 3s, Daftar 5/70, PDF p. 87), and the revenue it covers creates no temporary difference. For example (illustration), a developer's sale of 1,000 bears final tax of 1,000 × 2.5% = 25 on the gross transfer value (PP 34/2016), an expense outside income tax with no deferred tax."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Lessees and decommissioning: airlines, telecom, retail, upstream."},{"type":"text","text":" A lessee under PSAK 116 (IFRS 16) recognises a right-of-use asset and a lease liability on one transaction; where tax deducts lease payments as paid, the asset creates a taxable difference and the liability a deductible one. A capitalised decommissioning obligation does the same. Since the 2021 amendment to IAS 12 (paras. 15(b), 22A), adopted into PSAK 212 (then PSAK 46) from 1 January 2023, both are recognised. For example (illustration), a lease liability and right-of-use asset of 1,000 each at 22 percent."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax asset = 1,000 × 22% = 220; deferred tax liability = 1,000 × 22% = 220; net nil at inception"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The two then unwind at different speeds, because depreciation is straight line while interest is front-loaded. PHE's table has \"asset rental\" rows on both sides (Note 43e)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Multinational groups and Pillar Two."},{"type":"text","text":" The deferred tax effects of the OECD global minimum tax (15 percent) are excluded by a temporary exception (PSAK 212.4A). PHE applies it and says Indonesia's rules, PMK 136/2024, take effect for fiscal years from 1 January 2025 (Note 3s, Daftar 5/68, PDF p. 85; Note 43h, Daftar 5/180, PDF p. 197)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 43e"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE prints a movement table for each side (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155–5/156, PDF pp. 172–173). The totals, with the liability shown positive, are below."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Assets 2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Assets 2023"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Liabilities 2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Liabilities 2023"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Opening balance"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"95,263"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"49,907"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,625,374"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,573,179"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Charged to profit or loss"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(38,095)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"43,466"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"259,899"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"69,837"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Effect of change in tax rate"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(11,720)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Charged to OCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(1,385)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"496"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(167)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(2,089)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Correction"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(2)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,394"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9,472"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(3,833)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Closing balance"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"55,781"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"95,263"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"2,894,578"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"2,625,374"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"95,263 − 38,095 − 1,385 − 2 = 55,781"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,625,374 + 259,899 − 167 + 9,472 = 2,894,578"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The asset's 2024 charge is printed without brackets; its sign follows from Note 43c (section 9)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three things stand out. First, both lines are already net. Inside the 55,781 are oil and gas properties of −37,197, a taxable difference netted within the same subsidiaries, and the unrecognised amount of −15,545. Inside the 2,894,578, a taxable difference of 3,376,189 on oil and gas properties is reduced by deductible items, mainly the decommissioning provision (412,586) and asset rental (75,227). Second, most deductible differences therefore sit inside the liability. Of the decommissioning provision's 478,938, only 66,352 is on the asset line; PHE does not show the netting by entity. Third, profit or loss drives both movements. The asset fell 39,482 (41.4 percent) as the decommissioning difference reversed by 10,003 and the netted oil and gas difference grew by 23,927. The liability rose 269,204 (10.3 percent), 174,986 of it from oil and gas properties."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax share of 2024 tax expense = 297,994 / 1,771,541 = 16.8%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net deferred tax liability = 2,894,578 − 55,781 = 2,838,797 (2023: 2,530,111)"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"12. Upstream note: the ring-fence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Each PSC usually sits in its own subsidiary, which restricts which income a deduction can be used against (para. 27A) and blocks offset across PSCs (para. 74). Gross split PSCs, taxed under the prevailing law, carry losses forward ten years (PP 53/2017) and can be assessed for ten years (Note 43f, Daftar 5/156, PDF p. 173)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"13. Related lines"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"deferred-tax-liabilities"},{"type":"text","text":": the other side of the netting, covered on this page."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"income-tax-expense-net"},{"type":"text","text":": current and deferred tax, and the rate reconciliation, covered on this page (section 9)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"decommissioning-site-restoration-provision"},{"type":"text","text":": the source of PHE's largest deductible difference."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/corporate-dividend-taxes-receivable-current","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"corporate-dividend-taxes-receivable-current"},{"type":"text","text":": current tax already paid and recoverable."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"oil-gas-properties-net"},{"type":"text","text":": the taxable difference that absorbs the assets."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Least-confident assertions"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two readings of PHE's Note 43e are inferences. That the decommissioning deferred tax inside the liability line is netted against oil and gas property differences in the same subsidiaries is not shown by entity. Reading non-recoverable costs into \"PSC adjustments\" of 614,238 is a guess at one component, since PHE gives no breakdown."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"That the restoration cost in the illustrations is deductible when paid is assumed. Under a PSC, abandonment and site restoration funding has its own cost-recovery rules, which were not researched."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 2020–2021 rate history (Law 2/2020 scheduling 20 percent from 2022, then the HPP law keeping 22 percent) and the ten-year gross split carryforward (PP 53/2017) were checked only in secondary sources, not in the regulation texts."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Whether the IFRS edition of Kieso uses a valuation allowance account was not checked; the book is not on disk. The page attributes the allowance method to US GAAP only."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's Note 43d reconciliation looks partly mechanical. The 2024 starting figure, 1,663,757, is almost identical to 2023's 1,663,801, which fits the stated 37 percent; at the stated 38 percent it would be 1,859,178 and the PSC adjustments 418,817 rather than 614,238. Which figure is wrong cannot be told from the note. The associates line is exactly 22 percent of the share of profit in Note 42 (162,620 × 22% = 35,776), and the dividend item (−409,211; 2023: −710,276) is copied from the parent-only reconciliation although consolidated profit contains no dividend income (Notes 40, 42). Profit before tax of 4,892,574 against 4,892,576 on the face is a rounding difference of 2. Section 9 uses the note's figures as printed."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); statement of profit or loss (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14); Note 2a(ii) (Daftar 5/9, PDF p. 26); Note 3s (Daftar 5/67–5/70, PDF pp. 84–87); Note 43c–43f (Daftar 5/152–5/156, PDF pp. 169–173); Note 43h (Daftar 5/180, PDF p. 197). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk; figures checked against the PHE FS 2024 data extract)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intermediate Accounting, IFRS Edition"},{"type":"text","text":" (Wiley), chapter on accounting for income taxes, the textbook the lecture notes summarise; edition not checked. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes this series follows, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 19 – Accounting for Income Taxes\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): pretax financial income and taxable income, future taxable and deductible amounts, recoverability, the environmental revision, permanent and temporary differences (PT ASBM), future and revised rates (Wang Group, Hostel Co), loss carryback and carryforward (Garuda, Groh), comprehensive example. NOTES. Corrected here for Indonesian loss rules, Indonesian accrual-based taxable profit, and the treatment of a revised estimate. The notes do not cover offset, disclosure, the rate reconciliation, the PSC rate rule or the industry cases; sections 8–10 follow IAS 12.4A, 15, 22A, 61A, 71–86 and the sources named."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 212 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Pajak Penghasilan"},{"type":"text","text":" (IAS 12 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Income Taxes"},{"type":"text","text":"), paras. 5–8, 13, 15, 17–18, 24, 27–30, 34–37, 46–47, 51–53, 56, 71–75, 79–82. VERIFIED (web, AASB 112 text at standards.aasb.gov.au, which reproduces IAS 12 paragraph by paragraph; paras. 28–29, 35–36 also in IFRS Interpretations Committee agenda paper, January 2014). Single-transaction amendment (paras. 15(b), 22A; leases and decommissioning obligations; effective 1 January 2023): VERIFIED (web, IAS Plus, May 2021; Grant Thornton; IAI exposure draft of the PSAK 46 amendment). Pillar Two mandatory temporary exception (para. 4A): VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation news, April 2023; IAS Plus, May 2023). Paras. 79–80, 81(c)–(d), 85–86 (components of tax expense, rate reconciliation, applicable rate, average effective rate): VERIFIED (web, AASB 112 text, standards.aasb.gov.au/node/694, 2026-10-11). Paras. 60–61A: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 201 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Penyajian Laporan Keuangan"},{"type":"text","text":" (IAS 1), para. 56; PSAK 208 (IAS 8). STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 116 (IFRS 16). Number and title VERIFIED (web, IAI, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia"},{"type":"text","text":"); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"UU Pajak Penghasilan, art. 6(2), five-year loss carryforward. VERIFIED (web, DDTCNews, \"Contoh penghitungan kompensasi kerugian fiskal\"). Corporate rate of 22 percent from tax year 2022 under UU 7/2021 (HPP). VERIFIED (web, pajak.go.id, \"Lima Jenis Tarif PPh Badan\"). The 20 percent scheduled by UU 2/2020: secondary sources only, NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PP 53/2017 on taxation of gross split PSCs, art. 18(2), ten-year loss carryforward. VERIFIED (web, secondary: DDTCNews; Bisnis.com, 28 Dec 2017). PP 34/2016, 2.5 percent final tax on land and building transfers by developers. VERIFIED (web, setkab.go.id summary)."}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb
