with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under a production sharing contract (PSC) the oil belongs to the state until it is lifted, and the contract sets how many barrels each party is entitled to. Cargoes rarely match entitlements, so at any date one party has lifted too much (overlifting) and the other too little (underlifting). A contractor's underlifting settled in cash is a claim on the state for barrels it was entitled to but did not lift."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revenue under PSAK 115 needs a customer who obtains control. An underlifted barrel has gone to no customer of the contractor, so the claim is a receivable and the credit goes outside revenue from customers. The domestic market obligation (DMO) is different. The state takes crude and pays a fee, so the fee is revenue and the unpaid fee a trade-type receivable."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both claims arise from a contract, so they are financial assets at amortised cost under PSAK 109 and need an expected credit loss allowance, however small a sovereign's default risk makes it. Statutory claims such as VAT refunds are not financial assets and sit elsewhere."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report due from the Government of 623,448 at 31 December 2024 against 501,429 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 2.0 percent of total assets and 7.8 percent of current assets (1.6 and 5.1 percent a year earlier), and it rose 24.3 percent in the year. This page uses those numbers as the starting point for its examples."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After reading it a student should be able to compute a contractor's entitlement, explain why lifting and entitlement diverge, record an underlift and a DMO delivery, decide whether each claim is revenue, a financial asset or neither, and recognise similar claims on governments in other industries."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand and volumes in thousand barrels (kbbl) unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Why the Government can owe a contractor"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A private company cannot own Indonesian oil in the ground. The state owns it and hires a contractor through a PSC to find and produce it. The contractor bears the costs and the risk, and in return is paid in barrels. What the contractor receives is its "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"entitlement"},{"type":"text","text":", a share of each year's production set by the contract."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under a cost recovery PSC, PHE's Note 2a describes the order (Daftar 5/10–5/11, PDF pp. 27–28). "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"First tranche petroleum"},{"type":"text","text":" (FTP), 10 to 20 percent of production, is taken off the top before costs and shared between the Government and the contractor. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Cost recovery"},{"type":"text","text":" then returns the contractor's recoverable costs in barrels: the current year's non-capital costs, the current year's depreciation of capital costs, and earlier costs not yet recovered. What remains, the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"equity to be split"},{"type":"text","text":", is divided in the contract's ratio. Crude is valued at the Indonesian Crude Price (ICP), the government reference price, and gas at the price in each gas sales contract."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a block produces 4,000 kbbl in a year at an ICP of USD 75 per barrel. FTP is 20 percent, the contractor's share of FTP and of equity is 25 percent, and recoverable costs are 120,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gross production value = 4,000 × 75 = 300,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FTP = 4,000 × 20% = 800 kbbl; contractor's share = 800 × 25% = 200 kbbl"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost recovery = 120,000 / 75 = 1,600 kbbl"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equity to be split = 4,000 − 800 − 1,600 = 1,600 kbbl; contractor's share = 1,600 × 25% = 400 kbbl"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contractor's entitlement = 1,600 + 200 + 400 = 2,200 kbbl, worth 165,000; the state's = 1,800 kbbl"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ownership passes at "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"lifting"},{"type":"text","text":", the point where crude is measured and loaded at the terminal. Until then the barrels are the state's. That is why the contractor's \"asset\" during the year is a right to barrels, and why a gap between barrels owed and barrels taken has to be settled."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Entitlement versus lifting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Entitlement accrues with production, day by day. Lifting happens in cargoes. A tanker takes several hundred thousand barrels at once, and terminal slots are shared among the parties, so at any date one party has taken more than its entitlement to date and another less. The party that has lifted more is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"overlifted"},{"type":"text","text":" and owes the other; the one that has lifted less is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"underlifted"},{"type":"text","text":" and is owed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Continue the example. Of the contractor's 2,200 kbbl, 250 kbbl go to the domestic market obligation (section 4), leaving 1,950 kbbl to lift and sell, about 162.5 kbbl a month. It lifts cargoes of 325 kbbl every two months."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cargo"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Size"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cumulative entitlement at month end"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cumulative lifted"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Position"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"February"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"325"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"325"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"325"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"balanced"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"April"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"325"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"650"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"650"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"balanced"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"June"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"325"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"975"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"975"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"balanced"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"August"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"325"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,300"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,300"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"balanced"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"October"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"325"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,625"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,625"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"balanced"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"December, slot taken by the state's lifter"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,950"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,625"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"underlifted 325"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The December slot went to a cargo of the state's share, so the year ends with the state overlifted by 325 kbbl and the contractor underlifted by the same amount. PHE's note describes the same position. Its underlifting receivables arise \"due to SKK Migas's crude oil and natural gas lifting volumes exceeding its entitlements\" (Note 9, Daftar 5/97, PDF p. 114). SKK Migas is the regulator that administers PSCs for the Government."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two settlements are possible in principle. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"In kind"},{"type":"text","text":", the contractor gets extra barrels next year. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"In cash"},{"type":"text","text":", the Government pays the value of the barrels. PHE's movement in Note 9 shows settlement by payment, \"payment received\" of 786,361 against underlifting in 2024. Such payments, together with DMO fee claims, were governed during 2024 by PMK 118/2019 (amended by PMK 51/2023) and from 31 December 2024 by PMK 139/2024, the Minister of Finance regulation on paying claims of upstream contractors."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Receivable or revenue?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The question is whether the 325 kbbl is a sale. PSAK 115 (formerly PSAK 72, IFRS 15) recognises revenue when a performance obligation to a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"customer"},{"type":"text","text":" is satisfied by transferring control. A customer is a party that has contracted to obtain goods or services that are an output of the entity's ordinary activities in exchange for consideration. Kieso's five steps (identify the contract, the performance obligations, the price, allocate it, recognise when control passes) all start from such a contract."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"No step fits the underlift. The contractor has transferred nothing to anyone; the state's lifter took its own share early. The IFRS Interpretations Committee reached the same conclusion in March 2019 for a joint operator whose output received differed from its entitled share. It recognises revenue only for output it has sold to its customers. The analogy has two limits. The state is not a joint operator, and the Committee's fact pattern was a shortfall made up in later deliveries, with only revenue addressed. The measurement routes below are this page's reading for a claim settled in cash."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"That leaves three ways to book the imbalance, which differ in where the margin on the 325 kbbl appears. Continue the example. The five cargoes sold were 1,625 × 75 = 121,875; the full lifting entitlement is worth 1,950 × 75 = 146,250; production cost attributable to the 1,950 kbbl is USD 40 per barrel, 78,000."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Entitlement method"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sales method, imbalance at cost"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sales method, imbalance at price (PHE)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revenue"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"146,250"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"121,875"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"121,875"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Underlift recorded"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Receivable 24,375 / Cr Revenue 24,375"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Underlift asset 13,000 / Cr Production cost 13,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Due from the Government 24,375 / Cr Direct cost of sales 24,375"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost charged"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"78,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"78,000 − 13,000 = 65,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"78,000 − 24,375 = 53,625"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gross margin"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"68,250"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"56,875"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"68,250"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Underlift at price = 325 × 75 = 24,375; at cost = 325 × 40 = 13,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Margin difference = 68,250 − 56,875 = 11,375 = 325 × (75 − 40)"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The entitlement method puts non-customer barrels in revenue, which PSAK 115 does not allow. The cost route suits settlement in kind, where the asset is a right to future barrels. The price route suits settlement in cash, where the claim is a fixed sum."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE takes the third route. Underlifting and overlifting are valued at the \"weighted average annual Crude Oil price\" for crude and the gas sales contract price for gas, and because the adjustments \"are not transactions with customers\", they are recorded as part of direct cost of sales (Note 3p, Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80; the Indonesian reads \"dicatat sebagai bagian dari biaya langsung penjualan\"). Revenue from crude and gas is recognised on \"the actual lifting amount and sold to customers\" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/62, PDF p. 79)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The volume can still change after the year. PHE's policy measures imbalances against \"final entitlements\", which SKK Migas fixes after the period. If its calculation raises the contractor's 2024 entitlement by 13 kbbl:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Adjustment = 13 × 75 = 975; Dr Due from the Government 975 / Cr Direct cost of sales 975"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The price does not move the claim after year end, because the annual average is known by then. That keeps the claim a fixed sum, which matters in section 5."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. The domestic market obligation and its fee"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A PSC also obliges the contractor to supply part of its crude to the domestic market. PHE's Note 2a(v) sets the crude rule (Daftar 5/10, PDF p. 27). Take the smaller of two amounts, production multiplied by national demand over national production, and 25 percent of production, then multiply by the contractor's entitlement percentage. For gas it is 25 percent of production times that percentage (Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28). The DMO price for crude is the weighted average price of all crude sold by the contractor, or another price set in the PSC."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"In the example, assume the 25 percent figure is the smaller."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"DMO = 4,000 × 25% × 25% = 250 kbbl"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"DMO fee at the weighted average price = 250 × 75 = 18,750"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"If a PSC set the fee at 15% of ICP instead: 250 × 75 × 15% = 2,812.5, and the contractor gives up 15,937.5 of value"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here the Government is a customer. It contracts, through the PSC, to obtain crude and pays consideration for it, so the DMO delivery is a sale under PSAK 115 at the transaction price, the fee. A fee below market is still the transaction price; the value the contractor gives up is a cost of holding the contract and is never recorded as revenue."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Due from the Government, DMO fees 18,750 / Cr Revenue, DMO fees 18,750"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's revenue note shows DMO fees on crude of 429,402 in 2024 (2023: 448,841), under revenue from related parties and the Government (Note 35, Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165), and Note 9 describes the receivable as amounts due \"in relation to the Subsidiaries' obligations to supply crude oil to meet the domestic market demand in accordance with the PSCs\"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Is the claim a financial asset?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The classification decides which standard measures it. A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"financial asset"},{"type":"text","text":" includes a contractual right to receive cash from another entity (PSAK 232, formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32). The word that matters is contractual. Assets that are not contractual, such as income taxes created by statutory requirements imposed by governments, are not financial assets (IAS 32 AG12)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both of PHE's claims rest on the PSC, a contract between the contractor and the state. The underlift is a right to cash for barrels the contract entitled the contractor to; the DMO fee is consideration under the same contract. Both are financial assets, held to collect fixed amounts, so they sit at amortised cost under PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9). PHE's face statement points the line to Note 3g, its financial-instruments policy, and that note lists amounts due to the Government among financial liabilities (Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contrast a VAT refund. A contractor's right to have VAT reimbursed comes mainly from tax law and the regulations under it, so it is usually treated as a statutory claim, accounted for as a tax asset, and outside PSAK 109. Because reimbursement is also written into cost-recovery PSCs, the classification is a judgment. PHE keeps it out of this line. Reimbursable VAT of 505,861 (30,523 at the Company and 475,338 at subsidiaries) sits in other taxes receivable (Note 43a, Daftar 5/151, PDF p. 168)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Measurement and expected credit losses on the state"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Being owed by a sovereign lowers the risk, and the model still applies. PSAK 109 requires an expected credit loss (ECL) allowance on every financial asset at amortised cost. The two claims follow different approaches because they arise differently."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The DMO fee receivable arises from a PSAK 115 contract with a customer and has no significant financing component, so the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"simplified approach"},{"type":"text","text":" is required, with lifetime ECL from recognition."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The underlift does not come from a contract with a customer, so the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"general approach"},{"type":"text","text":" applies. It starts in stage 1 with 12-month ECL and moves to lifetime ECL only if credit risk increases significantly."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), assume a 12-month probability of default (PD) for the state of 0.2 percent, a loss given default (LGD), the share not recovered after default, of 45 percent, and an average life of 90 days for DMO fee claims and 212 days for underlift claims (section 9). Apply them to PHE's year-end balances, scaled to each claim's expected life."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Underlift, 12-month ECL over 212 days = 534,270 × 0.2% × 45% × 212 / 365 = 279.3"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"DMO fees, lifetime ECL over 90 days = 89,178 × 0.2% × 45% × 90 / 365 = 19.8"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Total = 299.1, or 0.0010% of PHE's total assets"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For a claim expected within a year, 12-month and lifetime ECL coincide, because 12-month ECL is defined as the part of lifetime ECL from defaults possible in the next twelve months. The stage matters only if settlement stretches beyond a year. An allowance this small is immaterial, but PSAK 109 still treats it as an expectation, and an expectation is never exactly zero."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Delay without loss."},{"type":"text","text":" A credit loss is the shortfall in cash flows discounted at the asset's original effective interest rate; PHE's policy uses \"an approximation of the original EIR\" (Note 3g, Daftar 5/43, PDF p. 60). A claim that bears no interest and was expected within months has an effective rate of about nil, so a payment that arrives late but in full creates almost no measurable loss. Delay changes presentation instead. A claim not expected within twelve months moves to non-current assets (PSAK 201, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1; PHE Note 3h)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Linked claims."},{"type":"text","text":" A government can delay paying one claim while another is unresolved. Section 9 gives a PHE example."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Settlement and the mirror liability"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The receivable leaves the balance sheet when SKK Migas, through the Ministry of Finance, pays it, or when it is offset against something the contractor owes. Overlifting is the mirror. When the contractor has lifted more than its entitlement, it owes the Government, and the liability is valued and booked the same way."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contractor overlifted 100 kbbl at an annual average of 75: Dr Direct cost of sales 7,500 / Cr Due to the Government 7,500"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE shows overlifting and DMO of 51,427 under due to the Government, current portion (Note 20, Daftar 5/114, PDF p. 131), and gas overlifting of 11,322 inside accrued expenses (Note 23, Daftar 5/117, PDF p. 134). PHE presents receivables and payables to the Government gross. Netting would require a legally enforceable right to set off and an intention to settle net (PSAK 232 para. 42)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A different imbalance arises between partners in the same block. When one working-interest holder lifts less than its share, the shortfall is usually made up in later cargoes, so it is a right to barrels, a non-financial item measured at cost. PHE's other current assets include \"deferred expenses related to underlifting\" and \"other receivables from underlifting for non-operators in PIEP whose settlement … refers to the operating cycle on the operator's side\" (Note 14a, Daftar 5/105, PDF p. 122), which looks like that cost route, though the note does not state the measurement basis."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Receivables from governments across industries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Many industries are owed money by governments. The accounting turns on the same two questions, whether there is a contract and whether the government is a customer."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Service concessions."},{"type":"text","text":" A company that builds a toll road or a power plant for a government and is paid fixed amounts by the government, whatever the traffic, holds a financial asset under ISAK 112 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Perjanjian Konsesi Jasa"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly ISAK 16, IFRIC 12). Where payment depends on users, it holds an intangible asset instead, the right to charge them. For example (illustration), an operator is promised 10,000 a year for five years from completion, and the market rate for such a claim is 7 percent."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Receivable at completion = 10,000 × (1 − 1.07⁻⁵) / 0.07 = 41,001.97"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"First-year interest income = 41,001.97 × 7% = 2,870.14"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The receivable follows PSAK 109 at amortised cost, with ECL on the grantor."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Government grants."},{"type":"text","text":" A grant the government has promised for meeting a condition becomes a grant receivable once there is reasonable assurance that the entity will comply and the grant will be received (PSAK 220, formerly PSAK 61, IAS 20). The credit goes to deferred income, to the asset's cost or to profit, never to revenue from customers, because the government is not buying anything."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Compensation for regulated prices."},{"type":"text","text":" State-owned fuel and power companies that sell below cost on government instruction may be compensated by the state. Whether such compensation is revenue, a grant or a separate receivable depends on what the government has committed to in writing and when. This page has not checked how Indonesian state-owned companies treat it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Health providers and contractors to government."},{"type":"text","text":" A hospital billing the national health insurer, or a builder billing a ministry, holds ordinary customer receivables (or contract assets) under PSAK 115, with the simplified ECL approach. A public counterparty changes the loss rate and leaves the model as it is."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Tax refunds"},{"type":"text","text":" of any kind are statutory claims, outside PSAK 109, as section 5 explained."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 9"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's note gives the composition and a movement for each claim (Note 9, Daftar 5/97, PDF p. 114)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Underlifting"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"DMO fees"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Total"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1 January 2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"422,720"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"78,709"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"501,429"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Additions"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"918,928"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"362,540"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Payments received"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(786,361)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(361,335)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Others"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(21,017)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9,264"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"31 December 2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"534,270"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"89,178"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"623,448"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Underlifting is 85.7 percent of the line. Dividing each closing balance by its year's additions gives a rough measure of how long claims wait."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Underlifting: 534,270 / 918,928 × 365 = 212 days"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"DMO fees: 89,178 / 362,540 × 365 = 90 days"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Underlifting claims wait longer, which fits a settlement tied to SKK Migas's calculation of final entitlements after the period. Both balances are still presented as current, consistent with Note 3h's one-period test."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 35 lists the Government among PHE's customers. SKK Migas appears among customers with more than 10 percent of revenue, at 1,133,213 (Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165), a figure equal to the DMO fees of 429,402 plus an \"Adjustment split PSC\" of 703,811 that the notes do not explain. Two figures do not tie. DMO fee revenue of 429,402 exceeds the 362,540 of DMO fee additions in Note 9, and cash receipts from the Government of 1,396,379 in the cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17) exceed the 1,147,696 received in Note 9. PHE reconciles neither, nor the \"others\" lines of (21,017) and 9,264. Note 9 states that the claims are \"fully collectible\", the language of collectibility rather than expectation. Note 45k shows how claims link. In 2022 PHE's subsidiary PHM, proposing to pay Badak LNG tariff arrears in instalments, hoped that \"PHM's DMO fee can be paid in due time\", and the energy minister asked the finance minister to pay it (Daftar 5/215, PDF p. 232)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Upstream note: gross split contracts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE also holds gross split PSCs, under which production is shared by a base split adjusted by variable and progressive components and ministerial discretion, without cost recovery (Note 2b, Daftar 5/17, PDF p. 34). Entitlement there is a percentage of gross production, so the waterfall in section 1 collapses to one line, but lifting in cargoes still creates imbalances. PHE's Note 2b does not describe the DMO under gross split, and this page does not assume it."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Related lines"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"due-to-government-current"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"due-to-government-non-current"},{"type":"text","text":": overlifting, DMO and other amounts owed to the state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-receivables-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"trade-receivables-net"},{"type":"text","text":": claims on customers for barrels lifted and sold."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/revenues","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"revenues"},{"type":"text","text":": where DMO fees are recognised."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/production-expenses","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"production-expenses"},{"type":"text","text":": the cost side, near where underlifting and overlifting adjustments are recorded."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-taxes-receivable-current"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-taxes-receivable-non-current"},{"type":"text","text":": reimbursable VAT."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-current-assets"},{"type":"text","text":": partner underlifting carried as deferred expenses."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Least-confident assertions"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"That PHE's underlifting is settled in cash rests on the \"payment received\" line in Note 9 and on PMK 139/2024. The regulation's existence, date and scope were confirmed on the web; its article-level content (SKK Migas pays an underlift, offsets allowed) comes from an earlier reading of the PDF, not re-checked here."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Classifying the underlift under the general ECL approach and the DMO fee under the simplified approach is this page's reading of PSAK 109; PHE recognises no allowance on either and does not say which approach it would use."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading PHE's partner underlifting in Note 14a as measured at cost is an inference from the label \"deferred expenses\"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Applying the 2019 IFRIC decision to cash-settled underlifts is an analogy twice removed (the state is not a joint operator, and the decision's shortfall was settled in kind). Treating the VAT refund as statutory is a judgment, since reimbursement is also a PSC term. How a claim whose amount moved with the oil price would be classified was not researched."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The sovereign PD and LGD are illustrative. Compensation receivables of Indonesian state-owned fuel and power companies were not researched, nor is it known what PHE's \"Adjustment split PSC\" revenue represents or where its receivable sits."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 2a(iii)–(vi) (Daftar 5/10–5/11, PDF pp. 27–28); Note 2b (Daftar 5/17, PDF p. 34); Note 3g (Daftar 5/43–5/44, PDF pp. 60–61); Note 3h (Daftar 5/51, PDF p. 68); Note 3p (Daftar 5/62–5/63, PDF pp. 79–80); Note 9 (Daftar 5/97, PDF p. 114); Note 14a (Daftar 5/105, PDF p. 122); Note 20 (Daftar 5/114, PDF p. 131); Note 23 (Daftar 5/117, PDF p. 134); Note 35 (Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165); Note 43a (Daftar 5/151, PDF p. 168); Note 45k (Daftar 5/215, PDF p. 232). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes this series follows, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 18 – Revenue Recognition\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): five steps, recognition when control passes. NOTES. The notes have no chapter on PSC accounting, government receivables or expected credit losses, and no textbook chapter covers this line, so the order follows the logic of the claim (ownership, entitlement, lifting, revenue or receivable, financial asset, ECL, settlement). The notes' Ch 17 impairment material uses the outdated incurred-loss model and was not used."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intermediate Accounting: IFRS Edition"},{"type":"text","text":" (Wiley), \"Revenue Recognition\" chapter (customer contracts, five steps). Edition and pages not checked. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision \"Sale of output by a joint operator (IFRS 11)\", March 2019, including its in-kind fact pattern and revenue-only scope. VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation agenda decision and staff paper AP8; IAS Plus meeting notes; EY "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"IFRS Developments"},{"type":"text","text":" 149, May 2019)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"IAS 32 AG12 (statutory assets such as income taxes are not financial assets). VERIFIED (web, IASB staff paper quoting AG12, ifrs.org). PSAK 232 para. 42 offsetting STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"ISAK 112 (IFRIC 12) financial asset model where the grantor has an unconditional obligation to pay. VERIFIED (web, IAS Plus IFRIC 12 summary; ICAEW)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PMK 139/2024 on paying claims on the Government in upstream oil and gas (DMO fees and/or underlifting, VAT reimbursement and related items), set, promulgated and in force 31 December 2024, revoking PMK 118/2019 as amended by PMK 51/2023 and PMK 119/2019, among others. VERIFIED (web, BPK peraturan.bpk.go.id/Details/312058, rechecked 2026-10-11; JDIH Kemenkeu). Article content from the 2026-10-09 recheck of the full text; not re-read here."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 115 (IFRS 15), PSAK 109 (IFRS 9), PSAK 201 (IAS 1), PSAK 220 (IAS 20). Numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia"},{"type":"text","text":"); simplified approach scope VERIFIED (web, IASB AP27C and BDO summaries); definitions of credit loss and 12-month ECL VERIFIED (web, AASB 9 Appendix A); other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb
   where e.id = '2671e055-567e-4d34-83e3-178af2661656'
     and e.section = 'accounting'
