-- intangible-assets-net-software-land-rights-goodwill: content_json backfill (the document content was serialised from)
with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An intangible asset is an identifiable non-monetary asset without physical substance. Identifiability, meaning separable or arising from a contract or legal right, is what divides it from goodwill."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Bought intangibles are recorded at cost, or at fair value when acquired in a business combination. Internally generated ones face a stricter rule. Research is always expensed, development is capitalised only from the date six criteria are all met, and brands and customer lists built in-house are never recognised."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A finite-life intangible is amortised over the shorter of its economic and legal life, normally straight-line with zero residual value. An indefinite-life intangible and goodwill are not amortised but tested for impairment every year, and a goodwill impairment is never reversed."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report intangible assets, net, of 71,814 at 31 December 2024 against 73,800 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 0.24 percent of total assets and 0.32 percent of non-current assets (0.24 and 0.35 percent a year earlier). It holds software and licences, land rights, and goodwill from two acquisitions (Note 13, Daftar 5/104, PDF p. 121)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A student should be able to recognise, measure, amortise and impair an intangible, and compute and test goodwill, with journals."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standards are PSAK 238 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Aset Takberwujud"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 19, IAS 38) and, for goodwill, PSAK 103 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. What an intangible asset is"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 238 defines an intangible asset as an identifiable non-monetary asset without physical substance (para. 8). The lecture notes, following Kieso, reduce this to four characteristics: identifiable, no physical form, benefits over more than one period, and not held for sale. Control normally comes from legal rights (para. 13), and benefits include cost savings (para. 17)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Identifiability"},{"type":"text","text":" separates intangible assets from goodwill (para. 11). An item is identifiable if it is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"separable"},{"type":"text","text":", capable of being sold, transferred or licensed on its own, or if it arises from contractual or other legal rights, transferable or not (para. 12). A software licence is both. A trained workforce is neither, so it stays in goodwill."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso sorts intangibles into types, which help when an acquisition is broken down (section 7)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Type"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Examples"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Marketing-related"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Trademarks, trade names, domain names"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Customer-related"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Customer lists, order backlogs, customer relationships"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Artistic-related"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Copyrights on books, music, pictures, video"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contract-related"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Franchises, licences, permits, concessions"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Technology-related"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Patents, software, databases, trade secrets"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The residual in a business combination (not identifiable)"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Boundaries."},{"type":"text","text":" Software integral to hardware, such as a machine's operating system, is part of the hardware under PSAK 216 (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16) (para. 4). PSAK 238 excludes exploration and evaluation assets, which follow PSAK 106 (formerly PSAK 64, IFRS 6), and oil, gas and mineral development and extraction (para. 2), but not software used there (para. 7)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Acquired intangible assets"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A separately acquired intangible is measured at cost (para. 24). The recognition tests, probable benefits and reliable cost, are treated as met for a purchase, because the price reflects the buyer's expectation of benefit (paras. 25–26). Cost is the purchase price, including non-refundable taxes, less trade discounts, plus every directly attributable cost of preparing the asset for use, such as professional fees and testing (paras. 27–28). Training staff and general overhead are expensed (para. 29)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), within PHE's 2024 software additions of 14,038 (Note 13), suppose one item is a perpetual licence for reservoir simulation software, list price 2,000, with a 10 percent volume discount. The vendor charges 250 to install and integrate it, testing before go-live costs 120, training the engineers costs 80, and first-year support is 150, expensed over the support year."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Purchase price = 2,000 × (1 − 10%) = 1,800"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost = 1,800 + 250 installation + 120 testing = 2,170"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kept out of cost = 80 training (expensed) + 150 support (prepaid, expensed over the year) = 230"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Intangible assets, software 2,170, Dr General and administrative expenses 80, Dr Prepaid expenses 150 / Cr Trade payables 2,400"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An intangible received in exchange for a non-monetary asset is measured at fair value unless the exchange lacks commercial substance or neither fair value can be measured reliably (paras. 45–46), the rule the Fixed Assets essay works through. One acquired in a business combination is measured at acquisition-date fair value, separately from goodwill (paras. 33–34)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Internally generated intangibles: research and development"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"No market price confirms the value of an intangible built in-house, so PSAK 238 divides each internal project into two phases to decide when spending starts creating an asset (para. 52)."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Research"},{"type":"text","text":" is original and planned investigation to gain new scientific or technical knowledge (para. 8), such as searching for and evaluating alternatives (para. 56). No asset is recognised from it, and the spending is expensed when incurred (para. 54)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Development"},{"type":"text","text":" is applying research findings to a plan or design for new or substantially improved products, processes or systems before commercial use (para. 8), such as designing and testing prototypes (para. 59). It is capitalised only when the entity can show all six criteria (para. 57):"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"technical feasibility of completing the asset;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"the intention to complete it and use or sell it;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"the ability to use or sell it;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"how it will generate probable future benefits, through a market or, if used internally, its usefulness;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"adequate technical, financial and other resources to finish it;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"the ability to measure its cost reliably."}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"If research cannot be told apart from development, the whole project is treated as research (para. 53). Cost starts from the date the criteria are first met, and earlier spending stays in expense and cannot be reinstated (paras. 65, 71). Only directly attributable costs qualify; overhead and training do not (paras. 66–67). The lecture notes, following Kieso, expense personnel and contract-service costs as incurred and record purchased R&D items at fair value. Under PSAK 238 both follow the phase, capitalised in development once the criteria are met, and separately purchased items are measured at cost (para. 24). Materials and equipment with alternative future uses become inventory or fixed assets, as the notes say."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary builds production-surveillance software in 2024. From January to March it studies feasibility and evaluates vendor products (300) and codes a rough prototype (150). On 1 April the budget is approved and the design is shown to work, so all six criteria are met. From April to December it spends 700 on programmers, 260 on contract coders and 140 on testing, and is charged 90 of head-office overhead and 60 for training field operators."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before 1 April (criteria not met)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"From 1 April (criteria met)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Expensed"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"research 300 + development before criteria 150 = 450"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"90 overhead + 60 training = 150"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Capitalised"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"none"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"700 + 260 + 140 = 1,100"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Research and development expense 450 / Cr Cash and accrued expenses 450"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Intangible assets, software under development 1,100, Dr General and administrative expenses 150 / Cr Cash and accrued expenses 1,250"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Of 1,700 spent, 1,100 becomes an asset. The 150 prototype is development work (para. 59), but it came before the criteria were met, so it stays in expense although it fed the final product (para. 71)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"What is never capitalised."},{"type":"text","text":" Internally generated brands, mastheads, publishing titles and customer lists are not recognised, because their cost cannot be told apart from developing the business as a whole (paras. 63–64). The notes let a self-developed trademark carry its attorney, registration, design, consulting and successful defence costs. PSAK 238.63 does not recognise an internally generated brand at all, so these costs are expensed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Cloud software."},{"type":"text","text":" The IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded in 2019 that a software-as-a-service (SaaS) contract is usually a service, and in 2021 that configuring or customising the supplier's software is expensed as the service is received unless it creates an asset the customer controls. For example (illustration), a three-year SaaS contract for a production-accounting system costs 150 a year, and the supplier charges 90 up front for configuration that is not distinct from the subscription. The 150 is expensed each year, and the 90 is a prepayment expensed at 90 / 3 = 30 a year."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The upstream parallel."},{"type":"text","text":" The notes give geologists' work as an example of research, and PHE likewise expenses geological and geophysical costs under PSAK 106 (Note 3m(i), Daftar 5/56, PDF p. 73; see the Oil and Gas Properties essay)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Amortisation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How an intangible is amortised depends on whether its useful life is finite or indefinite (paras. 88–89). An intangible with a finite life is amortised. One with an indefinite life, with no foreseeable limit to the period over which it will bring in net cash, is not (para. 107)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Useful life"},{"type":"text","text":" reflects expected use, obsolescence, competitors' actions, maintenance needed and legal limits (para. 90). A right arising from a contract or law is amortised over no more than its term (para. 94). Renewal periods count only if renewal is evidenced, its conditions will be met and its cost is small relative to its benefits (para. 96)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Residual value"},{"type":"text","text":" is assumed to be zero unless a third party has committed to buy the asset or an active market will exist at the end of its life (para. 100)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Method."},{"type":"text","text":" Amortisation starts when the asset is available for use and follows the pattern in which benefits are consumed, with straight-line when that pattern cannot be determined reliably (paras. 97–98). A revenue-based method is presumed inappropriate, because revenue reflects prices and volumes as well as use (para. 98A), unless the right itself is defined by a revenue limit (paras. 98B–98C). Period and method are reviewed each year end, and a change is a change in estimate under PSAK 208 (formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8) (para. 104)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), the surveillance software goes live on 1 January 2025 with a five-year life. The simulation licence is perpetual, but the subsidiary expects to replace it after seven years as the technology dates."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Surveillance software = 1,100 / 5 = 220 a year; simulation licence = 2,170 / 7 = 310 a year"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Amortisation expense 530 / Cr Accumulated amortisation, software 530"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"At the end of 2026 the surveillance software carries 1,100 − 2 × 220 = 660. If its remaining life is revised to two years, amortisation becomes 660 / 2 = 330 a year from 2027, with no restatement."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Land rights."},{"type":"text","text":" Indonesian land rights such as "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"hak guna bangunan"},{"type":"text","text":" (HGB, a right to build) run for a term and can be extended. PHE's policy puts the initial legal costs of a land right into land, undepreciated, and treats rights that are leases in substance under PSAK 116 (Note 3i, Daftar 5/52, PDF p. 69; the Right-of-Use Assets essay explains the ISAK 336 test). Extension and renewal costs have a term of their own, so they are commonly carried as an intangible and amortised over the extended term. For example (illustration), fees of 60 to extend an HGB for 20 years are amortised at 60 / 20 = 3 a year."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Indefinite life."},{"type":"text","text":" For example (illustration), a subsidiary buys a trade name for 5,000, renewable every ten years at a small fee, with no plan to stop using it. It is not amortised but tested for impairment each year (para. 108), and the life assessment is reviewed each period (para. 109). If in 2027 management decides to retire the name in four years, it is tested for impairment and then amortised at 5,000 / 4 = 1,250 a year."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Subsequent costs, revaluation and derecognition"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Later spending is only rarely capitalised, because most of it maintains benefits already expected (para. 20). The notes capitalise the cost of successfully defending a patent; under PSAK 238 a defence keeps existing benefits and is normally expensed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An entity may choose the revaluation model for a class of intangibles only where fair value can be measured in an "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"active market"},{"type":"text","text":" (paras. 72, 75). Patents, brands and software are unique, so the model is rare. PHE uses cost."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An intangible is derecognised on disposal or when no further benefit is expected, with the gain or loss in profit or loss. For example (illustration), take PHE's 2024 software deductions (Note 13), cost 5,580 and accumulated amortisation 2,768, as systems retired with no proceeds."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Carrying amount = 5,580 − 2,768 = 2,812"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Accumulated amortisation, software 2,768, Dr Loss on derecognition 2,812 / Cr Intangible assets, software 5,580"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Impairment"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An intangible is written down when its carrying amount exceeds its "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"recoverable amount"},{"type":"text","text":", the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use, under PSAK 236 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Penurunan Nilai Aset"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 48, IAS 36). The mechanism is taught in the Fixed Assets and Oil and Gas Properties essays; intangibles differ in when the test is required."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Asset"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When tested"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reversal"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Finite-life intangible"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When there is an indication of impairment"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Allowed, up to the carrying amount with no impairment"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Indefinite-life intangible"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every year, and on an indication"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Allowed, same cap"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Not yet available for use"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every year"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Allowed, same cap"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every year, within a cash-generating unit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Never (PSAK 236.124)"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The notes define the test against recoverable amount, but their patent example uses an appraised fair value. Fair value less costs of disposal is only one leg; value in use may be higher when the asset is worth more in the entity's own use. PHE's policy lists the same annual tests (Note 3w, Daftar 5/72, PDF p. 89)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), suppose instead that the surveillance software served one field, and in late 2025 the subsidiary's contract for that field is not extended. The software cannot be sold, so fair value less costs of disposal is nil, and value in use for the remaining months is 250."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Carrying amount at 31 December 2025 = 1,100 − 220 = 880"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Impairment loss = 880 − higher of (0, 250) = 630"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Impairment loss on non-financial assets 630 / Cr Allowance for impairment, software 630"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Goodwill"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill is what a buyer pays beyond the fair value of the identifiable assets and liabilities it gets, such as the assembled workforce and expected synergies. It is not identifiable, so it falls outside PSAK 238 (para. 3(f)) and is measured under PSAK 103. Internally generated goodwill is never recognised."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Measurement."},{"type":"text","text":" Under the acquisition method the acquirer measures identifiable assets and liabilities at fair value at the acquisition date, including intangibles the acquiree never recorded. Goodwill is the residual. Acquisition costs are expensed; PHE puts them in general and administrative expenses (Note 3d, Daftar 5/35, PDF p. 52)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill = consideration transferred + non-controlling interest (NCI) − fair value of identifiable net assets"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration, in the structure of the lecture notes' example), a PHE subsidiary buys 100 percent of an oilfield-services company for 120,000 cash."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Book value"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fair value"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fixed assets"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"48,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"61,500"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Inventories"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"12,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Receivables"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10,500"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10,500"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cash"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7,500"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7,500"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Customer contracts (order backlog)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"none"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Software"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,500"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Liabilities"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(16,500)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(16,500)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Net assets"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"62,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"84,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill = 120,000 − 84,000 = 36,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Fixed assets 61,500, Dr Inventories 12,000, Dr Receivables 10,500, Dr Cash 7,500, Dr Intangible assets, customer contracts 6,000, Dr Intangible assets, software 3,000, Dr Goodwill 36,000 / Cr Liabilities 16,500, Cr Cash 120,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Of the 58,000 paid above book value, 22,000 is fair value step-ups, including 6,000 of customer contracts the seller never recorded, and 36,000 is goodwill. Deferred tax on the step-ups is ignored here; PHE carries a deferred tax liability of 7,760 labelled \"excess fair value over NBV\" (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Non-controlling interest."},{"type":"text","text":" When less than 100 percent is bought, PSAK 103 lets the acquirer measure NCI, acquisition by acquisition, at fair value or at its proportionate share of identifiable net assets, a choice PHE's policy states (Note 3d). Suppose the subsidiary buys 80 percent for 96,000, and the other 20 percent has a fair value of 23,000."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI at proportionate share"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI at fair value"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"20% × 84,000 = 16,800"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"23,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"96,000 + 16,800 − 84,000 = 28,800"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"96,000 + 23,000 − 84,000 = 35,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Whose goodwill"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The parent's only"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The parent's and the NCI's"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Bargain purchase."},{"type":"text","text":" A price below the fair value of net assets is first rechecked, and any excess left is a gain in profit or loss. Bought for 80,000, the company above gives a gain of 84,000 − 80,000 = 4,000."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"No amortisation."},{"type":"text","text":" PSAK 22 (1994) amortised goodwill over five to twenty years; the revision that adopted IFRS 3 replaced amortisation with an annual impairment test. PHE's Note 13 still shows the old amortisation (section 9)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Impairment of goodwill."},{"type":"text","text":" Goodwill brings in no cash on its own, so from the acquisition date it is allocated to the cash-generating units (CGUs) expected to benefit from the combination (PHE Note 3d, Daftar 5/37, PDF p. 54), and each is tested every year (Note 3w, Daftar 5/73, PDF p. 90). A unit's impairment loss goes first to goodwill and then to its other assets pro rata to carrying amount (PSAK 236.104), with no asset taken below the highest of its fair value less costs of disposal, value in use and zero (para. 105). For example (illustration), a one-block CGU carries oil and gas properties of 400,000, goodwill of 50,000 and current assets of 30,000, and lower price forecasts cut its recoverable amount to 420,000. The current assets (receivables and inventories) are measured under their own standards and sit outside PSAK 236 (para. 2), so they take no share of the loss."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Impairment loss = (400,000 + 50,000 + 30,000) − 420,000 = 60,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"To goodwill = 50,000; to oil and gas properties = 60,000 − 50,000 = 10,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Impairment loss on non-financial assets 60,000 / Cr Goodwill (accumulated impairment) 50,000, Cr Allowance for impairment, oil and gas properties 10,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"If prices recover, the 10,000 may be reversed up to its ceiling, but the 50,000 never is (PSAK 236.124), because a later rise in the unit's value may be internally generated goodwill."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Intangible assets across industries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Telecommunications."},{"type":"text","text":" A mobile operator's up-front spectrum licence fee is amortised over the licence term, and annual frequency usage fees are expensed as they fall due. For example (illustration), an up-front fee of 1,000 for a ten-year licence is amortised at 1,000 / 10 = 100 a year."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Pharmaceuticals."},{"type":"text","text":" Feasibility and benefits are hard to show before a regulator approves a drug, so drug makers commonly expense development until approval. A compound bought in mid-development is capitalised at cost, because a purchase always meets the recognition test (para. 25)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Toll roads and power concessions."},{"type":"text","text":" Under ISAK 112 (formerly ISAK 16, IFRIC 12), an operator that builds infrastructure for a government that controls its use, its prices and the asset at the end does not recognise the road as a fixed asset. It recognises an intangible right to charge users when it bears traffic risk, or a financial asset when the grantor guarantees the cash. For example (illustration), a toll road built for 5,000 under a 40-year concession (intangible measured at the fair value of the construction service, here assumed equal to cost) is amortised at 5,000 / 40 = 125 a year straight-line, or at 5,000 × 2% = 100 under a traffic-based method in a first year carrying 2 percent of expected total traffic. A toll-revenue method is presumed inappropriate (para. 98A). A power producer selling to PLN, the state electricity company, may fall under ISAK 112 or the lease rules, depending on who controls the plant."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Plantations."},{"type":"text","text":" Plantation land is held through "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"hak guna usaha"},{"type":"text","text":" (HGU), a cultivation right of limited term. The right is tested under ISAK 336 (formerly ISAK 36; see the Fixed Assets essay), and extension costs have commonly been carried as amortised intangibles. For tax, the Income Tax Law lists the costs of extending HGB and HGU, and goodwill, among expenditure amortised in four-, eight-, sixteen- or twenty-year groups, or, since UU HPP (2021), over the actual useful life where it exceeds twenty years (UU PPh art. 11A(2), (2a); PMK 72/2023)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 13"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE gives the movement table PSAK 238.118 asks for (Note 13, Daftar 5/104, PDF p. 121)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 December 2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Accumulated amortisation"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Land rights"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"563"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(315)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"248"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Software and licences"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"93,921"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(75,691)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"18,230"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"113,470"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(26,755)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"86,715"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Allowance for impairment"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(33,379)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Intangible assets, net"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"207,954"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"(102,761)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"71,814"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Opening 73,800 + additions 14,038 − carrying amount derecognised (5,580 − 2,768) − net correction (2,257 − 2,195) − amortisation 13,150 = 71,814"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill dominates the line. PHE does not split the allowance of 33,379 by class. If all of it is the fully impaired Nunukan goodwill, the remaining ONWJ goodwill is 113,470 − 26,755 − 33,379 = 53,336, or 74.3 percent of the line, and software and land rights make up the other 18,478."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Goodwill and land rights in Note 13."},{"type":"text","text":" Goodwill arose from the 2009 acquisition of ONWJ Ltd. and the 2013 acquisition of PHE Nunukan Company, whose goodwill was fully impaired in 2021; when the Nunukan block was impaired by a further 53,303 in 2023, the Note 17 table's goodwill column showed none for it (Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129). On the 53,336 reading ONWJ's gross goodwill is 80,091, and the 26,755 of accumulated amortisation, unchanged in both years, is 33.4 percent of it, which fits amortisation charged before the revised PSAK 22 took effect in 2011. If, as secondary sources say, the revision required that amortisation to be eliminated against cost, PHE's gross presentation does not follow it, though net goodwill is unchanged. Land rights are HGB with terms ending between 2024 and 2045, amortised although management expects renewal, at a 2024 charge of 26 on average cost of 576.5, about 22 years. Since policy 3i sends initial legal costs to land, the amortised intangible is most likely extension costs, the treatment of the former ISAK 25."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Software amortisation rate = 13,124 / ((87,693 + 93,921) / 2) = 14.5% (2024); 6,505 / ((82,412 + 87,693) / 2) = 7.6% (2023)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Accumulated amortisation / cost, software = 75,691 / 93,921 = 80.6% (77.0% in 2023)"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"On straight-line, 14.5 percent implies an average life of about seven years and 7.6 percent about thirteen, measured on gross cost that still includes fully amortised software, so the life of assets still being amortised is shorter. Amortisation doubled from 6,532 to 13,150 while software cost rose 7.1 percent in 2024, and four-fifths of the software's cost is already consumed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The FS do not show useful lives, the amortisation method or the line item carrying amortisation for software and land rights (para. 118(a), (b), (d)), so the doubling cannot be explained. The policies cover only goodwill (Note 3d), and the DD&A line in profit or loss cites Notes 12, 16a and 17 but not 13 (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14). The \"correction\" columns (−2,230 of software cost, +2,181 of accumulated amortisation) are unexplained. In 2023 software deductions removed 865 of cost but 875 of accumulated amortisation, a negative carrying amount of 10. The critical estimates note calls \"goodwill or intangible assets not ready to use\" examples of indefinite useful life (Daftar 5/82, PDF p. 99, both language columns), but an asset not yet ready for use is tested annually because amortisation has not started, not because its life is indefinite."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Upstream note"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under PHE's cost recovery production sharing contracts (PSCs), materials and equipment bought for petroleum operations belong to the Government (Note 2a(vii), Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28). The FS do not say whether software falls under that clause. The goodwill here arose because PHE bought companies holding PSC interests (ONWJ Ltd. and PHE Nunukan Company)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Related lines"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"oil-gas-properties-net"},{"type":"text","text":": exploration and evaluation assets, PSC interests, and the CGUs that carry goodwill."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"fixed-assets-net"},{"type":"text","text":": software integral to hardware, and initial legal costs of land rights."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"right-of-use-assets-net"},{"type":"text","text":": land rights that are leases in substance."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"depreciation-depletion-amortization"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/impairment-non-financial-assets","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"impairment-non-financial-assets"},{"type":"text","text":": where amortisation and impairment are presumably charged."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"long-term-investments"},{"type":"text","text":": goodwill inside associates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"deferred-tax-liabilities"},{"type":"text","text":": deferred tax on fair value step-ups."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Least-confident assertions"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"That the allowance of 33,379 belongs wholly to Nunukan goodwill, so that ONWJ goodwill is 53,336 net, is an inference. Note 13 does not split the allowance, and the FS do not give each acquisition's original goodwill."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"That the 26,755 of accumulated amortisation predates the revised PSAK 22 is inferred from the 2009 acquisition date and the 33.4 percent ratio. PSAK 22 (2010), effective 1 January 2011, is reported in secondary sources to have required eliminating accumulated amortisation against goodwill cost on transition, as IFRS 3 (2004) did; PHE still presents the 26,755 gross. The transition paragraph itself was not opened."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The reading that PHE's intangible land rights are extension costs, as the former ISAK 25 is understood to have required, reconciles Note 13 with policy 3i, but PHE does not say so. No text of ISAK 25 or of ISAK 336's paragraph on renewal costs (DK09) could be found."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The industry paragraphs describe common practice under the standards, not checked against any named company's statements."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Expensing patent defence costs and trademark registration fees under PSAK 238, against Kieso's capitalisation, is this page's reading of paragraphs 20 and 63; neither paragraph names those costs."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); statement of profit or loss (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14); Note 2a(vii) (Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28); Note 3d (Daftar 5/35–5/37, PDF pp. 52–54); Note 3i (Daftar 5/52, PDF p. 69); Note 3l (Daftar 5/54, PDF p. 71); Note 3m(i) (Daftar 5/56, PDF p. 73); Note 3w (Daftar 5/72–5/73, PDF pp. 89–90); critical estimates (Daftar 5/82, PDF p. 99); Note 13 (Daftar 5/104, PDF p. 121); Note 17 (Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172). Indonesian and English columns read for Notes 3d, 3w, 13 and the critical estimates. VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes this series follows, \"Inter 1 – Chapter 12 – Intangible Assets\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan; file name spelled \"Intengible\"): definition and four characteristics, valuation of purchased and internally created intangibles (citing \"PSAK 19 paragraf 24\"), limited and indefinite lives, types, goodwill with the acquisition example, impairment and reversal, goodwill impairment, research and development criteria and cost categories. NOTES. Corrected, not copied: the patent impairment example uses fair value alone; personnel and contract-service R&D costs expensed whatever the phase; purchased R&D items \"at fair value\"; capitalised costs of developing trademarks and defending patents; goodwill as price above \"harga pasar\" rather than above the fair value of identifiable net assets. The notes put R&D last; this page follows the batch brief and places it with internally generated intangibles."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intermediate Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":", IFRS edition (Wiley), chapter on intangible assets, the textbook the notes summarise. Not opened; edition not confirmed. TEXTBOOK — NOT OPENED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 238 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Aset Takberwujud"},{"type":"text","text":" (IAS 38 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intangible Assets"},{"type":"text","text":"), paras. 2–4, 7–8, 11–13, 17, 20, 24–27, 33–35, 45–46, 52–54, 56–57, 59, 63–67, 71–72, 75, 88–90, 94, 96–98C, 100, 104, 107–109, 118. VERIFIED (web, AASB 138 August 2015, legislation.gov.au F2015L01558, which reproduces IAS 38 paragraph for paragraph). Derecognition (paras. 112–113): STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 236 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Penurunan Nilai Aset"},{"type":"text","text":" (IAS 36): annual tests for goodwill and for indefinite-life and not-yet-available intangibles, allocation to CGUs, no reversal of goodwill impairment, as restated in PHE Note 3w. VERIFIED (PHE FS). Paras. 104–105: VERIFIED (web, AASB 136 text; ACCA DipIFR, \"Impairment of goodwill\"). Para. 124: VERIFIED (web, secondary)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 103 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis"},{"type":"text","text":" (IFRS 3): acquisition method, NCI choice, acquisition costs expensed, bargain purchase reassessment, as restated in PHE Note 3d. VERIFIED (PHE FS). Standard text not opened: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee agenda decisions on cloud computing under IAS 38: customer's right to access the supplier's software (March 2019) and configuration or customisation costs (March 2021, approved by the IASB April 2021). VERIFIED (web, Grant Thornton IFRS Viewpoint; Queensland Audit Office). Indonesian application not checked."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 22 (1994) goodwill amortisation over 5–20 years and its replacement by impairment testing in the 2010 revision: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Jurnal JABIS, UII; Budi Luhur journal). Effective date 1 January 2011 and elimination of accumulated amortisation on transition: VERIFIED (web, secondary, Budi Luhur and UII JABIS; transition from one source). Standard text not opened."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"ISAK 112 (IFRIC 12), ISAK 336 (formerly ISAK 36), PSAK 106 (IFRS 6), PSAK 116 (IFRS 16), PSAK 208 (IAS 8), PSAK 216 (IAS 16): numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia"},{"type":"text","text":"). ISAK 336's substance test VERIFIED (web, secondary: Binus Accounting on ISAK 36). ISAK 112's intangible and financial asset models VERIFIED (web, secondary: IMF GFSAC paper on IFRIC 12, as in the Fixed Assets essay). Section 8 industry practice: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"UU No. 7/1983 on Income Tax as amended by UU No. 36/2008, art. 11A (amortisation of intangibles, including costs of extending HGB, HGU and hak pakai, and goodwill, in 4-, 8-, 16- and 20-year groups). VERIFIED (web, pasal.id). Art. 11A(2a) added by UU No. 7/2021 (HPP), with PP 55/2022 art. 22(5) and PMK 72/2023: VERIFIED (web, secondary: pajak.com; IAI, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Sosialisasi PMK 72 Tahun 2023"},{"type":"text","text":"; DDTCNews)."}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb
   where e.id = '29be61c1-9966-4d98-906b-b522bf8f8108'
