-- intercompany-profit-in-inventory: content_json backfill (the document content was serialised from)
with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A group earns revenue only when goods leave it. Consolidation therefore removes every intercompany sale and purchase in full, and removes the seller's profit on goods still held by another group company at year end, until those goods are sold to an outsider."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Who bears the deferred profit depends on direction. Profit on a downstream sale (parent to subsidiary) falls wholly on the parent's owners. Profit on an upstream sale (subsidiary to parent) belongs to a subsidiary that outside owners share in, so it is divided between the parent and the non-controlling interest (NCI)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The elimination changes the group's carrying amount of inventory but not its tax base, because Indonesian companies are taxed one by one. The difference is a deductible temporary difference under PSAK 212, so each deferral of profit carries a deferred tax asset at the buyer's tax rate, if the buyer expects taxable profit to use it."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Barito Pacific Tbk and subsidiaries (BRPT) eliminated inter-segment revenue of 4,538 in 2025 (5,136 in 2024), in USD thousand, to arrive at consolidated revenue of 7,630,662 (BRPT FS 2025, Note 46, PDF p. 239). Its policy states that \"all intragroup assets and liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows relating to transactions between members of the Group are eliminated in full on consolidation\" (Note 3c, PDF pp. 67–68)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After reading this page a student should be able to eliminate intercompany sales and unrealised profit in a consolidation worksheet, release last year's deferral, attribute both between the parent and NCI, compute the parent's equity-method income, and add the deferred tax."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from BRPT's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Why the group cannot profit from selling to itself"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A sale between two group companies moves goods from one pocket of the same owner to another, so from the group's point of view nothing has been sold. The lecture notes for AKL Chapter 5 justify the elimination by control over pricing, since the parent's management controls every intercompany transaction, \"termasuk otorisasi dan penentuan harga\" (including authorisation and pricing), and they treat the group as one reporting entity. A transfer price the parent sets itself cannot be evidence of value."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 110 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) para. B86(c) requires the group to \"eliminate in full intragroup assets and liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows\", and adds that profits or losses on intragroup transactions \"recognised in assets, such as inventory and fixed assets, are eliminated in full\". Revenue in the consolidated statements is therefore earned only when a group company sells to an "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"outside party"},{"type":"text","text":", any entity that is not the parent or one of its subsidiaries."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The NCI essay ("},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"non-controlling-interest"},{"type":"text","text":") covers how control is tested, how NCI is measured at acquisition and how profit, OCI and dividends are attributed to it. This page takes all of that as given and adds one complication. The subsidiary's profit \"as the group sees it\" excludes profit that is still sitting in another group company's warehouse."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Eliminating intercompany sales and purchases"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both group companies record an intercompany sale as if it were with an outsider, so the consolidated totals of sales and cost of sales are each overstated by the transfer price. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"transfer price"},{"type":"text","text":" is the price one group company charges another."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a parent sells goods that cost it 100,000 to its subsidiary for 120,000, a markup of 20 percent on cost. During the year the subsidiary sells goods that cost it 84,000 (at transfer price) to outside customers for 105,000. The remaining 36,000 is in its warehouse at year end."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Seller's books"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Buyer's books"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Purchase from outsider"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Inventory 100,000 / Cr Accounts payable 100,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Intercompany sale"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Due from subsidiary 120,000 / Cr Sales 120,000; Dr Cost of sales 100,000 / Cr Inventory 100,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Inventory 120,000 / Cr Due to parent 120,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sale to outsiders"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Accounts receivable 105,000 / Cr Sales 105,000; Dr Cost of sales 84,000 / Cr Inventory 84,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gross profit in the separate books = (120,000 − 100,000) + (105,000 − 84,000) = 20,000 + 21,000 = 41,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Group cost of goods sold to outsiders = 84,000 × 100 / 120 = 70,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Group gross profit = 105,000 − 70,000 = 35,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The first elimination removes the intercompany sale from both sides."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Sales 120,000 / Cr Cost of sales 120,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The entry is the same whatever happened to the goods afterwards. It changes no profit, only the size of the two lines. Without it, the group would report sales of 225,000 and cost of sales of 184,000, and 120,000 of that revenue, 53 percent, would be sales to itself. An unpaid balance of 120,000 would also be eliminated as a reciprocal balance (K2, entry (6))."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Unrealised profit in ending inventory"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Removing the sale leaves a gap of 41,000 − 35,000 = 6,000 between the separate books and the group. That gap is the seller's profit on goods the buyer still holds. Profit on an intercompany sale is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"unrealised"},{"type":"text","text":" while the goods remain inside the group, and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"realised"},{"type":"text","text":" when a group company sells them to an outsider."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ending inventory at transfer price = 36,000; at group cost = 36,000 × 100 / 120 = 30,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unrealised profit = 36,000 − 30,000 = 6,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cost of sales 6,000 / Cr Inventory 6,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After the first entry, consolidated cost of sales is 100,000 + 84,000 − 120,000 = 64,000. The debit brings it to 70,000, the group's cost of the goods sold to outsiders, and the credit brings inventory down to what the group paid outsiders. Consolidated inventory is then 30,000, measured at group cost, as PSAK 202 (formerly PSAK 14, IAS 2) requires for any inventory."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Markup on cost or margin on selling price."},{"type":"text","text":" Intercompany pricing is usually quoted one of two ways, and confusing them misstates the elimination."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Markup on cost (20%)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gross margin on selling price (20%)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Transfer price of 100 of cost"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"100 × 1.20 = 120"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"100 / 0.80 = 125"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unrealised profit as a share of the transfer price"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"20 / 120 = 16.67%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"20%"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unrealised profit in 36,000 of ending inventory"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"36,000 × 16.67% = 6,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"36,000 × 20% = 7,200"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The rule is that the unrealised profit is always computed on the ending inventory "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"at transfer price"},{"type":"text","text":", using the profit as a fraction of the transfer price. A markup "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"m"},{"type":"text","text":" on cost converts to a margin of "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"m"},{"type":"text","text":" / (1 + "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"m"},{"type":"text","text":"), so 20 percent on cost is 16.67 percent of price."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Realising last year's deferral"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit deferred at the end of one year is realised in the next, when the buyer sells the goods. The buyer's opening inventory still carries the transfer price, so its cost of sales this year is overstated by the profit eliminated last year."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), the subsidiary opened the year with a further 24,000 of goods bought from the parent at the same 20 percent markup, all sold during the year (their cost of sales is left out of section 3's figures)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unrealised profit in opening inventory = 24,000 × 20 / 120 = 4,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"This year's worksheet starts from the separate books, which never saw last year's elimination, so the deferral has to be repeated against opening equity and then released through cost of sales. With the parent on the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"equity method"},{"type":"text","text":" (it carries the investment at cost plus its share of the subsidiary's profit, less dividends; section 6), last year's deferral sits in the parent's investment account, so the notes debit the investment for a downstream sale."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in subsidiary 4,000 / Cr Cost of sales 4,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The credit lowers cost of sales, so this year's group profit rises by 4,000, the profit earned when the goods finally left the group. The debit restores the investment account, which the parent had reduced by 4,000 last year, so the later elimination of the investment against the subsidiary's equity balances. If the parent carries the subsidiary at cost in its separate statements, as PSAK 227 (formerly PSAK 4, IAS 27) allows, the same debit goes to opening retained earnings instead (K2 explains the difference)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Over two years the profit is not lost, only moved into the year the goods were sold outside."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Downstream and upstream sales"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Direction decides who bears the elimination. A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"downstream"},{"type":"text","text":" sale runs from parent to subsidiary, so the unrealised profit sits in the parent's own profit and falls wholly on the parent's owners. An "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"upstream"},{"type":"text","text":" sale runs from subsidiary to parent, so the unrealised profit sits in the subsidiary's profit, which outside shareholders share. The elimination of sales, cost of sales and inventory is the same in both cases. Only the attribution differs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The starting numbers are BRPT's. BRPT owns 34.63 percent of PT Chandra Asri Pacific Tbk (CAP) directly and 3.92 percent through its wholly owned Marigold Resources (Note 1, PDF pp. 24 and 28)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Parent's effective interest = 34.63% + 3.92% = 38.55%; NCI = 100% − 38.55% = 61.45%"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"BRPT consolidates CAP despite holding under half of it because, it judges, the composition of CAP's Board of Directors gives it power over CAP's relevant activities and variable returns (Note 4, PDF p. 112). The NCI essay explains the control test."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"CAP reported revenue of 7,020,007, expenses of 5,572,021 and profit of 1,447,986 for 2025, before intragroup eliminations (Note 34, PDF p. 221). Use that profit as the subsidiary's reported profit, and assume (illustration) that the parent's separate profit before investment income is 200,000 and that the section 2–4 sales run between the two, with opening unrealised profit of 4,000 and closing unrealised profit of 6,000."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Downstream (parent sells)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upstream (subsidiary sells)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Subsidiary's realised profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,447,986 (unaffected)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,447,986 + 4,000 − 6,000 = 1,445,986"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"61.45% × 1,447,986 = 889,787"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"61.45% × 1,445,986 = 888,558"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Parent's income from subsidiary"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"38.55% × 1,447,986 + 4,000 − 6,000 = 556,199"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"38.55% × 1,445,986 = 557,428"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit attributable to owners of the parent"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"200,000 + 556,199 = 756,199"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"200,000 + 557,428 = 757,428"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"200,000 + 1,447,986 − 2,000 = 1,645,986"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,645,986"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated profit is the same, because the same 2,000 of net deferral (6,000 − 4,000) leaves the group either way. The split moves. Downstream, the parent's owners absorb all 2,000. Upstream, they absorb 38.55 percent of it, 771, and NCI absorbs 1,229, which is the 757,428 − 756,199 gap."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of profit (upstream) = NCI % × (subsidiary's reported profit + opening upstream unrealised profit − closing upstream unrealised profit)"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"What PSAK says about the split."},{"type":"text","text":" PSAK 110 requires full elimination (B86(c)) and attribution of profit to the parent and NCI (B94), but does not say how an upstream elimination is attributed. The notes give the proportional method as the GAAP approach and mention an alternative of charging upstream profit wholly to the parent, as for downstream. IFRS 10 leaves the split to accounting policy. Proportional attribution is common, matches the notes and reflects that the profit being deferred is the subsidiary's, and this page and the NCI essay follow it."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. The parent's equity method and the worksheet"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under the equity method the parent's own profit already equals the consolidated profit attributable to its owners, which is why the notes call it a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"one-line consolidation"},{"type":"text","text":". The parent therefore has to make the same deferrals in its investment account."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Income from subsidiary = parent % × subsidiary's reported profit + opening unrealised profit released − closing unrealised profit deferred (downstream in full; upstream × parent %)"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the upstream case of section 5, the parent records:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in subsidiary 557,428 / Cr Income from subsidiary 557,428"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under PSAK the equity method is mainly a worksheet device for subsidiaries. In its separate statements a parent may carry a subsidiary at cost, under PSAK 109 or by the equity method (PSAK 227 para. 10)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The consolidation worksheet for the upstream case uses K2's numbering, with the profit eliminations between entries (5) and (6). The notes put them first; the order changes no total. This illustration has no fair value excess, so entries (4) and (5) are empty. Assume (illustration) that the parent's sales are 1,000,000, its cost of sales 700,000 and other expenses 100,000; that CAP's 5,572,021 of expenses splits into 5,000,000 of cost of sales and 572,021 of other expenses; and that the subsidiary paid no dividend."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Entry"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Debit"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Credit"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(1) Parent's income from subsidiary"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Income from subsidiary 557,428"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investment in subsidiary 557,428"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(2) NCI share of profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of profit 888,558"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI 888,558"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(3) Opening equity of subsidiary"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"as in K2"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"as in K2"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5a) Intercompany sales"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sales 120,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost of sales 120,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5b) Opening unrealised profit, upstream"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investment in subsidiary 1,542 (38.55% × 4,000); NCI 2,458 (61.45% × 4,000)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost of sales 4,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5c) Closing unrealised profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost of sales 6,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Inventory 6,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(6) Reciprocal balances"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"as in K2"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"as in K2"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Entries (1), (2) and (5a)–(5c): debits = 557,428 + 888,558 + 120,000 + 1,542 + 2,458 + 6,000 = 1,575,986; credits = 557,428 + 888,558 + 120,000 + 4,000 + 6,000 = 1,575,986"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Income statement"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Parent"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Subsidiary"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Eliminations"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sales"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,000,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7,020,007"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5a) −120,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7,900,007"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost of sales"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(700,000)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5,000,000)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5a) +120,000, (5b) +4,000, (5c) −6,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5,582,000)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Other expenses"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(100,000)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(572,021)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(672,021)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Income from subsidiary"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"557,428"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(1) −557,428"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"757,428"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,447,986"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,645,986"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(2) −888,558"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(888,558)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Attributable to owners of the parent"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"757,428"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"757,428"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The check is that the consolidated profit attributable to owners, 757,428, equals the parent's own profit under the equity method. Entry (5b) splits last year's deferral because the sale was upstream. The parent's 38.55 percent goes to the investment account and the NCI's 61.45 percent to opening NCI, since both were reduced when the profit was deferred; the notes' upstream example does the same with 32,000 and 8,000 at 80 percent. For a downstream sale, (5b) would debit the investment with the whole 4,000 and NCI would not appear."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Deferred tax on eliminated profit"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The worksheet changes the group's carrying amount of inventory without changing anyone's tax position. BRPT states that \"the Group is subject to income tax on an entity basis based on profit arising or derived from the tax jurisdiction in which the Group entities are domiciled and operates\" (Note 23, PDF p. 145). Indonesia has no consolidated tax return, so the seller has already paid tax on the 6,000, and the buyer's "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"tax base"},{"type":"text","text":" (the amount the tax authority will let it deduct) is the 36,000 it paid."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 212 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Pajak Penghasilan"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12) compares the consolidated carrying amount with that tax base. A carrying amount below the tax base is a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"deductible temporary difference"},{"type":"text","text":", which gives a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"deferred tax asset"},{"type":"text","text":" (DTA), tax that will be recovered when the buyer sells the goods and deducts 36,000 against revenue the group sees as costing 30,000. PSAK 110 B86(c) points to it directly, saying the income-tax standard \"applies to temporary differences that arise from the elimination of profits and losses resulting from intragroup transactions.\" The lecture notes leave tax out; Beams treats consolidated income taxes in a later chapter that the notes do not cover."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), both companies pay the general Indonesian corporate rate of 22 percent (whether CAP meets the listed-company conditions for 19 percent is not checked here)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Temporary difference = tax base 36,000 − carrying amount 30,000 = 6,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"DTA = 6,000 × 22% = 1,320"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Deferred tax asset 1,320 / Cr Income tax expense 1,320"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net reduction in group profit after tax = 6,000 − 1,320 = 4,680"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The DTA is measured at the rate that will apply when the difference reverses, which is the buyer's rate, because the buyer holds the inventory and will take the deduction. When the two companies sit in different tax jurisdictions the seller's tax paid and the buyer's DTA no longer offset. CAP's group spans Indonesia and Singapore (Note 1, PDF p. 24). If the buyer were a Singapore company taxed at 17 percent:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"DTA = 6,000 × 17% = 1,020; tax paid by the Indonesian seller = 1,320; difference left in tax expense = 300"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rates also differ inside Indonesia, and BRPT's own group shows it. Its geothermal JOC subsidiaries pay 34 percent (Note 23, PDF p. 145), so a DTA on inventory held by such a buyer would be 6,000 × 34% = 2,040. A listed company that meets the public-float conditions pays 3 points less than the general rate (19 percent), so the buyer's own status matters too."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Last year's deferral reverses with the goods. Its DTA of 4,000 × 22% = 880 was set up last year and is released this year, so for an upstream sale entry (5b) becomes:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in subsidiary 1,203 (38.55% × (4,000 − 880)); Dr NCI 1,917 (61.45% × 3,120); Dr Income tax expense 880 / Cr Cost of sales 4,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net effect on this year's group profit = −6,000 + 4,000 + 1,320 − 880 = −1,560"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upstream, NCI bears 61.45 percent of the after-tax effect and the parent 38.55 percent, so every percentage applied in section 5 is now applied to after-tax amounts. A DTA is recognised only if the buyer expects taxable profit to use it (PSAK 212 para. 24); BRPT, for instance, recognises none on the parent's fiscal losses (Note 41, PDF p. 225). A DTA can be offset only against a deferred tax liability of the same taxable entity, or of another entity that will settle net with the same authority, which Indonesia's entity-by-entity system rules out (PSAK 212 para. 74). That is why BRPT shows deferred tax assets of 122,742 and deferred tax liabilities of 263,652 for the CAP group side by side instead of netting them (Note 41, PDF p. 225)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"US GAAP, which Beams follows, instead defers the seller's tax paid and forbids a buyer's-rate DTA on intra-entity inventory; PSAK has no such exception."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Intercompany losses that signal impairment"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An intercompany sale below cost creates an unrealised loss, and the general rule eliminates it in the same way, by writing inventory back up to group cost. PSAK 110 B86(c) adds that \"intragroup losses may indicate an impairment that requires recognition in the consolidated financial statements\". If the loss exists because the goods are now worth less than they cost, the group would have had to write them down anyway under PSAK 202's lower of cost and net realisable value (NRV), so that part of the loss stays."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a parent sells goods that cost it 10,000 to a subsidiary for 8,000. At year end they are still unsold and their NRV is 8,500."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Loss in the parent's books = 10,000 − 8,000 = 2,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Write-down the group would recognise anyway = 10,000 − 8,500 = 1,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Loss to eliminate = 8,500 − 8,000 = 500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Inventory 500 / Cr Cost of sales 500"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated inventory is 8,500, the lower of group cost of 10,000 and NRV of 8,500. Eliminating the whole 2,000 would carry the goods at 10,000, above what they can be sold for."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Intercompany profit across industries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Associates and joint ventures."},{"type":"text","text":" An "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"associate"},{"type":"text","text":" is an entity over which the investor has significant influence but not control, usually through a 20–50 percent holding, accounted for by the equity method. A sale to an associate is a sale to an outside party, because the associate is not in the group, so the revenue stays in full. Only the investor's share of the profit in goods still held is deferred (PSAK 228, formerly PSAK 15, IAS 28, para. 28). Losses that show a fall in NRV or an impairment are recognised in full for downstream sales and to the extent of the investor's share for upstream ones (para. 29)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Subsidiary"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Associate or joint venture"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sales and cost of sales"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"eliminated in full"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"not eliminated"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unrealised profit, downstream"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"eliminated in full, against the parent"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"eliminated to the investor's share"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unrealised profit, upstream"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"eliminated in full, split parent / NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"eliminated to the investor's share"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Entry (illustration: 2,000 profit in goods held by a 45% associate)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Share of profit of associate 900 / Cr Investment in associate 900"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The IASB's 2024 exposure draft on the equity method proposes recognising these gains and losses in full. The IASB was still deliberating in 2026, so this row may change."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Vertically integrated groups."},{"type":"text","text":" Petrochemical producers moving olefins into polymer units, plantation groups selling crude palm oil to their own refineries, and coal miners supplying captive power plants all have large, recurring intercompany flows and inventory held at every stage. Segment reporting under PSAK 108 (formerly PSAK 5, IFRS 8) shows them when inter-segment revenue is part of the measure management reviews (para. 23(b)), together with the basis of accounting for transactions between segments (para. 27(a))."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Investment entities."},{"type":"text","text":" A parent that qualifies as an "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"investment entity"},{"type":"text","text":" does not consolidate its investees but measures them at fair value through profit or loss (PSAK 110 para. 31). Sales to those investees are then sales to outsiders and are not eliminated."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Transfer pricing."},{"type":"text","text":" Consolidation eliminates the profit whatever the price, so an arm's-length tax adjustment to a transfer price changes current and deferred tax but not consolidated inventory, which stays at group cost."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Presentation and analysis: Barito Pacific's Note 46"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 108 asks for revenue from external customers and, where management reviews it, from other segments. BRPT's segment note gives both, with the elimination column (Note 46, PDF p. 239)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Petrochemical and refinery"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Energy and resources"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Property"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Others"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Eliminations"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2025 external revenue"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7,020,007"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"605,187"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5,213"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"255"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7,630,662"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2025 inter-segment revenue"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4,505"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"33"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(4,538)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024 external revenue"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,785,364"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"596,823"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4,546"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"262"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,386,995"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024 inter-segment revenue"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4,926"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"210"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(5,136)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Inter-segment revenue as a share of revenue before elimination: 2025 = 4,538 / (7,630,662 + 4,538) = 0.06%; 2024 = 5,136 / (2,386,995 + 5,136) = 0.21%"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The two large segments report no inter-segment revenue at all. Petrochemical and refinery revenue of 7,020,007 equals CAP's revenue before intragroup eliminations, and energy and resources revenue of 605,187 equals the figure for PT Barito Renewables Energy Tbk (BREN), in the material-NCI summaries of Note 34 (PDF pp. 221–222). The only inter-segment revenue comes from the property and other segments, and the note does not say what it is. At BRPT's level, then, the eliminations this page describes are close to zero."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"They happen one level down. Each of the two large segments is a listed sub-group, and its own intragroup trade is eliminated before its figures reach BRPT. BREN's segment note, for example, eliminates 7,205 of revenue from contracts with customers in 2025 (6,519 in 2024), most of it the holding segment's 7,227 of revenue charged to the operating companies, and 6,053 of profit before tax (BREN FS 2025, Note 29, PDF p. 127). BRPT's statements do not show CAP's eliminations between its Indonesian plants, the Singapore business of Aster Chemical and Energy acquired on 1 April 2025 (Note 43, PDF p. 226) and its trading companies."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Some related-party sales are not eliminated at all. The group, in practice CAP, sold 147,273 to PT Synthetic Rubber Indonesia (SRI) and 45,211 to PT Nusantara Polymer Solutions (NPS) in 2025 (Note 45, PDF p. 236). NPS is a related party in which CAP holds shares, and SRI is a 45 percent associate (Note 14, PDF p. 124), so these sales stay in consolidated revenue, and only the group's share of profit in SRI's unsold year-end inventory is deferred under PSAK 228. BRPT does not disclose that amount, and SRI is carried at nil, so where such a deferral would go is itself a judgement. Related-party sales fell to 2.6 percent of revenue from 9.7 percent (Note 35, PDF p. 222), mostly because revenue more than tripled while these sales fell by 15 percent (195,438 against 231,262)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Related essays"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"non-controlling-interest"},{"type":"text","text":": control, NCI measurement and attribution, which this page assumes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/inventories-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"inventories-net"},{"type":"text","text":": cost formulas and the lower of cost and NRV, which set the group cost in sections 3 and 8."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"deferred-tax-assets"},{"type":"text","text":": temporary differences and offsetting rules behind section 7."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"long-term-investments"},{"type":"text","text":": the equity method for associates in section 9."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/revenues","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"revenues"},{"type":"text","text":": when revenue is recognised, which here waits for the sale outside the group."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"K1 Business Combinations; K2 Consolidation Worksheet (reciprocal balances, cost versus equity method); K4 Intercompany Plant Assets (the same logic for land and depreciable assets, including BRPT's elimination of a land gain between CAP and SRI); K5 Consolidated Cash Flows; K6 Mutual Holdings."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Least-confident assertions"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"B86(c) and B94 require full elimination and attribution but do not say how an upstream elimination is split, so it is an accounting-policy choice. Proportional attribution is common and is used here; the notes' alternative (all to the parent) is not ruled out by the text."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The source of BRPT's inter-segment revenue (property and others) and of the eliminations of 29 in segment result and 139,079 in segment assets is not explained in Note 46. That CAP's segment carries large intra-segment eliminations is an inference from its structure (Indonesian plants, a Singapore refinery and trading companies in Note 1), not a disclosed figure."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Treating BREN's eliminated revenue of 7,205 as mostly holding-company charges to the operating companies rests on the segment table alone. BREN's tax-line elimination of 998 could be deferred tax on eliminated profit or something else, and is not used here."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Measuring the DTA at the buyer's rate follows IAS 12's reversal principle as firms explain it; IAS 12 does not state the rule for this case in so many words. The 22 percent and 19 percent Indonesian rates and the 17 percent Singapore rate are from knowledge; only the 34 percent JOC rate is taken from BRPT (Note 23), and whether CAP qualifies for 19 percent was not checked."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The IASB's 2024 equity-method exposure draft may change PSAK 228 para. 28; the IASB decided in October 2025 to do further work and had not concluded by mid-2026. The current text was checked only through secondary sources. Where a downstream deferral goes when the associate (SRI) is carried at nil is a judgement BRPT does not disclose."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Barito Pacific Tbk dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2025 and 2024 (audited, USD thousand): Note 1 (PDF pp. 24, 28; printed 11, 15); Note 3c consolidation policy (PDF pp. 67–68, printed 54–55); Note 4 control over CAP (PDF p. 112, printed 99); Note 14 (PDF p. 124, printed 111); Note 23 (PDF p. 145, printed 132); Note 34 (PDF pp. 221–222, printed 208–209); Note 35 (PDF p. 222, printed 209); Note 41 (PDF p. 225, printed 212); Note 43 (PDF p. 226, printed 213); Note 45 (PDF p. 236, printed 223); Note 46 (PDF p. 239, printed 226). BRPT FS. PT Barito Renewables Energy Tbk, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2025, Note 29 (PDF pp. 126–127). BRPT FS (subsidiary)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes this page follows, \"AKL – Chapter 5 – Intercompany Profit Transactions: Inventories\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan), summarising Beams et al., "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advanced Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":". Section order: elimination of sales, ending inventory, beginning inventory, downstream and upstream, equity method and worksheets. NOTES. Corrections made here, after recomputing every example in the PDF: (a) p. 7 labels the second NCI case \"downstream\" where it is the upstream case ((50,000 − 20,000) × 20% = 6,000); (b) p. 8 writes the downstream income as \"[{$50.000 x 20%)-20.000)\" where it is 80% × 50,000 − 20,000 = 20,000; (c) p. 9 calls 6,250 the \"cost\" of the 15,000 sale, but 6,250 is the gross profit (cost 8,750), which is what makes 40% × 6,250 = 2,500 and \"$6.000 dikurangi $3.500\" consistent; (d) the 2017 downstream worksheet (p. 11) shows NCI of 3,750 and controlling interest of 33,750, copied from the 75 percent example, where they should be 10% × 15,000 = 1,500 and 36,000; (e) the 2017 upstream worksheet at 75 percent (p. 13) shows NCI of 11,250 and controlling interest of 148,750 where they should be 25% × (60,000 + 5,000) = 16,250 and 143,750; (f) p. 17 credits the investment with 44,000 where 88,000 − 40,000 = 48,000, the figure the worksheet uses (616,000 + 32,000 − 48,000 − 600,000 = 0); (g) the upstream worksheet (p. 18) shows closing NCI of 16,500 and a total of 5,166,500, copied from the downstream example, where NCI is 150,000 − 8,000 + 12,000 = 154,000 and the total 5,304,000, and its header reads \"Pam ... 2019\" for the Pop–Son 2017 case; (h) p. 6 labels the 2018 ending-inventory entry \"untuk tahun 2017\", and p. 16 describes the 2016 deferral as \"selama 2017\". The notes refer to \"GAAP\" for proportional upstream attribution and offer charging it all to the parent as an alternative; section 5 treats the split as an accounting-policy choice under PSAK 110 and follows the proportional method. The notes do not cover deferred tax (section 7), intercompany losses (section 8) or associates (section 9). Section 9 has no note."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 110 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian"},{"type":"text","text":" (IFRS 10), paras. B86(c) and B94. VERIFIED (web, AASB 10 compiled text, aasb.gov.au, which reproduces IFRS 10). Para. 31: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 212 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Pajak Penghasilan"},{"type":"text","text":" (IAS 12): no inventory exception under IFRS and the buyer's-rate measurement, as against the US GAAP inventory exception (ASC 740-10-25-3(e), ASC 810-10-45-8). VERIFIED (web, Deloitte Roadmap: Income Taxes, appendix E.6). Paras. 24 and 74: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 228 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Investasi pada Entitas Asosiasi dan Ventura Bersama"},{"type":"text","text":" (IAS 28), paras. 28–29. VERIFIED (web, PKF IAS 28 guide; IFRS Interpretations Committee papers on \"unrelated investors\"). IASB Exposure Draft ED/2024/7 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Equity Method of Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":" and its status (further work decided October 2025, staff papers into 2026): VERIFIED (web, FAR comment letter; IASB Update October 2025; IASB staff paper May 2026), content of the final standard unknown. Upstream attribution as a policy choice: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Disclosure Hub, 2014)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 202 (IAS 2), PSAK 227 para. 10 (IAS 27), PSAK 108 (IFRS 8): numbers from the IAI renumbering rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. Indonesian corporate rate of 22 percent and the 3-point reduction for listed companies (UU PPh art. 17 as amended by UU 7/2021), Singapore's 17 percent rate, and Indonesian transfer-pricing rules: NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beams, Anthony, Bettinghaus and Smith, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advanced Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":", ch. 5, the textbook the lecture notes summarise. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED."}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb
   where e.id = 'ff940d50-0c65-48a8-8459-9f0457aa0c40'
     and e.section = 'accounting'
     and e.content_json is null
     and md5(e.content) = '6a546405b42eab06bc8e931a6684da9c'
  returning e.id, e.title, e.snippet, e.status, e.content_json
