-- intercompany-profit-plant-assets: content_json backfill (the document content was serialised from)
with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When one group company sells a plant asset to another above its carrying amount, the seller books a gain the group has not earned, because the asset has not left the group. Consolidation removes the gain in full and puts the asset back at the seller's carrying amount, the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"group cost"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For land the gain stays eliminated until the land is sold outside the group. For a depreciable asset the buyer depreciates the transfer price, so each year's excess depreciation is corrected, and the gain comes back piece by piece over the remaining life."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Direction decides who bears the gain. A downstream sale (parent to subsidiary) falls wholly on the parent's owners; an upstream sale (subsidiary to parent) is shared with the non-controlling interest (NCI). Intercompany leases and bonds bought back within the group follow the same logic."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Barito Pacific Tbk and subsidiaries (BRPT) report property, plant and equipment, net, of 5,660,112 at 31 December 2025 against 3,489,851 a year earlier, in USD thousand (BRPT FS 2025, statement of financial position, PDF p. 14). Inside that group, PT Krakatau Chandra Energi (KCE) completed a land sale and purchase with PT Chandra Asri Alkali (CAA) and PT Chandra Cilegon Port (CCP) during 2025 for 26,223 (Note 45h, PDF p. 237). All three are subsidiaries of PT Chandra Asri Pacific Tbk (CAP), which BRPT consolidates (Note 1d, PDF pp. 24–25), and BRPT's policy is that intragroup transactions \"are eliminated in full on consolidation\" (Note 3c, PDF p. 68)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After reading this page a student should be able to eliminate an intercompany gain on land and depreciable assets in the year of transfer, in later years and on sale outside, split it between the parent and NCI, and treat intercompany leases and bond holdings the same way."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from BRPT's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Why an intercompany transfer creates a gain the group has not earned"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A group earns a gain on an asset only when the asset leaves the group. PSAK 110 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) requires profits and losses on intragroup transactions recognised in assets such as inventory and fixed assets to be eliminated in full (para. B86(c)), because for the economic unit nothing has changed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The lecture notes (AKL Chapter 6) start from the same point. A transfer above book value creates an "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"unrealised gain"},{"type":"text","text":", recorded by the seller but not earned by the group, which must be removed from the consolidated statements and, where the parent uses the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"equity method"},{"type":"text","text":" (the investment at cost plus its share of the subsidiary's profit, less dividends), from the parent's own "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"income from subsidiary"},{"type":"text","text":". The notes call the equity method a \"one-line consolidation\" (AKL Chapter 2): it must give the parent the same profit as full consolidation, condensed into one line."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI, the worksheet and depreciation itself come from earlier essays (section 11). Inventory (K3) works the same way; the difference is time. An inventory gain usually comes back within a year, a gain on equipment over its remaining life, and a gain on land only if the land is sold outside."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Land: the gain is eliminated until the land is sold outside"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Land is not depreciated (PSAK 216 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Aset Tetap"},{"type":"text","text":", formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16, para. 58), so nothing in the buyer's books ever corrects the gain. The consolidation entry has to remove it in the year of sale and put it back on the worksheet every year until the land leaves the group."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The notes add \"paten yang memiliki masa manfaat yang tidak terbatas\" to this class, but a patent has a finite legal life. The class is land and intangibles with an indefinite useful life, which PSAK 238 (formerly PSAK 19, IAS 38) does not amortise."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), take BRPT's 26,223 as the transfer price. Parent P owns 70 percent of subsidiary S, the same percentage CDI holds in KCE (Note 1d). The seller carried the land at 16,223."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gain in the seller's books = 26,223 − 16,223 = 10,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Group cost of the land = 16,223, whoever holds it"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Year of sale."},{"type":"text","text":" The worksheet entry is the same in both directions; what differs is whose profit carried the gain."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Gain on sale of land 10,000 / Cr Land 10,000"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Downstream: P sells to S"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upstream: S sells to P"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gain sits in"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"P's own profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"S's reported profit"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"P's equity-method entry"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Income from subsidiary 10,000 / Cr Investment in S 10,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Income from subsidiary 7,000 / Cr Investment in S 7,000 (70% × 10,000)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"30% × S's reported profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"30% × (S's reported profit − 10,000)"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Later years."},{"type":"text","text":" The holder still carries the land at 26,223 and the seller's retained earnings still contain the gain, so each year's worksheet repeats the elimination against opening equity, which under P's equity method sits in the investment account."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Downstream"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upstream"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in S 10,000 / Cr Land 10,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in S 7,000, Dr NCI 3,000 / Cr Land 10,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The notes print the upstream entry \"Investment in Sun $18.000 / Noncontrolling interest share $2.000 / Land $20.000\" among the 2016 entries, introduced only by \"Apabila Pop memutuskan untuk menggunakan tanah tersebut\". It is the later-years entry: in the year of sale the gain is still in the income statement, and the notes' own 2016 worksheet debits \"Gain on sale\". The debit is also to the opening NCI balance, not to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"NCI share"},{"type":"text","text":", which is the income-statement line."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Sale outside the group."},{"type":"text","text":" Suppose the holder sells the land to an outside buyer for 30,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Holder's gain = 30,000 − 26,223 = 3,777"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Group's gain = 30,000 − 16,223 = 13,777"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gain realised now = 13,777 − 3,777 = 10,000"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Downstream"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upstream"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in S 10,000 / Cr Gain on sale of land 10,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in S 7,000, Dr NCI 3,000 / Cr Gain on sale of land 10,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"P's books mirror it (Dr Investment in S / Cr Income from subsidiary, 10,000 or 7,000), and in the upstream case 3,000 of the 13,777 group gain goes to NCI."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Depreciable assets: the gain comes back through depreciation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A depreciable asset corrects itself slowly. The buyer depreciates the transfer price, the group should depreciate the group cost, and correcting the difference each year releases a slice of the gain into consolidated profit, the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"piecemeal recognition"},{"type":"text","text":" of the notes and Beams."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), on 1 January 2025 one company sells equipment to the other. BRPT depreciates machinery and heavy equipment straight-line over 4–37 years (Note 3n, PDF p. 90); take five years remaining, no residual value."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Seller's cost 50,000, accumulated depreciation 20,000, carrying amount 30,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Transfer price 40,000; gain = 40,000 − 30,000 = 10,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Buyer's depreciation = 40,000 / 5 = 8,000 a year"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Group's depreciation = 30,000 / 5 = 6,000 a year"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Depreciation correction = 8,000 − 6,000 = 2,000 a year = 10,000 / 5"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"At the end of 2025 the buyer carries 40,000 − 8,000 = 32,000 and the group 30,000 − 6,000 = 24,000; the 8,000 gap is the gain not yet realised."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Year of transfer."},{"type":"text","text":" Two entries."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Dr Gain on sale of equipment 10,000 / Cr Equipment 10,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Dr Accumulated depreciation 2,000 / Cr Depreciation expense 2,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A partial worksheet, with the parent as buyer:"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Buyer"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Seller"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cr"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gain on sale of equipment"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) 10,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"0"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Depreciation expense"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) 2,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equipment"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"40,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) 10,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"30,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Accumulated depreciation"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) 2,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Adjustments"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"12,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"12,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated equipment, net, is 30,000 − 6,000 = 24,000. Entry (a) is the notes' "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"net"},{"type":"text","text":" form. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"gross"},{"type":"text","text":" form restores the seller's cost and accumulated depreciation (Dr Gain 10,000, Dr Equipment 10,000 / Cr Accumulated depreciation 20,000), showing 50,000 less 26,000 at year end, also 24,000, and closer to what the group would disclose had the transfer never happened. The notes describe it in words but name no account for the 10,000 debit, which is Equipment."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Year-end transfers."},{"type":"text","text":" A sale on 31 December needs only entry (a) that year; correction (b) starts the next."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Downstream and upstream: who bears the gain"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The elimination entries are the same in both directions; the split between the parent's owners and NCI is not. A downstream gain is the parent's own, so NCI share is computed on the subsidiary's reported profit. An upstream gain is part of the subsidiary's profit, so the parent bears its percentage and NCI the rest."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The notes put this as \"unrealized profit yang harus dieleminasi juga sebesar kepemilikan parent\". That holds for the parent's equity-method income. On consolidation the whole gain is eliminated in both directions (PSAK 110 para. B86(c)), and only its allocation is proportional."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), continue the equipment case with P owning 70 percent of S. In 2025 S reports profit of 60,000 and P's own profit, before income from S, is 100,000; the 10,000 gain sits with the seller."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Downstream (P sells)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upstream (S sells)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"S's realised profit for the group"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"60,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"60,000 − 10,000 + 2,000 = 52,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"P's income from S"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"70% × 60,000 − 10,000 + 2,000 = 34,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"70% × 52,000 = 36,400"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"30% × 60,000 = 18,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"30% × 52,000 = 15,600"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"100,000 + 60,000 − 10,000 + 2,000 = 152,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"152,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Attributable to P's owners"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"152,000 − 18,000 = 134,000 = 100,000 + 34,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"152,000 − 15,600 = 136,400 = 100,000 + 36,400"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated profit is the same; only its attribution moves. In the downstream case the 2,000 correction goes wholly to P, because the excess depreciation in S's books comes from P's gain. The notes' upstream truck example gives 80% × 50,000 + 80% × 1,000 − 80% × 3,000 = 38,400 by the same formula. It lists the truck's remaining life as \"5 years\" but depreciates \"÷ 3 years\", and the investment reaches 80 percent of S's equity three years after the sale, so three years is the consistent figure."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The check."},{"type":"text","text":" Suppose S's equity was 200,000 at the start of 2025, P bought its 70 percent at book value, and no dividends were paid, so S's equity is 260,000 at year end."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upstream: P's investment = 140,000 + 36,400 = 176,400 = 70% × 260,000 − 70% × 8,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upstream: NCI = 60,000 + 15,600 = 75,600 = 30% × (260,000 − 8,000)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Downstream: P's investment = 140,000 + 34,000 = 174,000 = 70% × 260,000 − 8,000; NCI = 30% × 260,000 = 78,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The investment account always equals the parent's share of the subsidiary's book equity less the unrealised gain it bears, which is how the notes reconcile the investment in their combined example: 540,000 less 5,000 and 14,400 gives 520,600."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Later years and the sale outside the group"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"From the second year the gain has left the income statement and sits in opening retained earnings, so the worksheet removes it from opening equity and continues the depreciation correction. With the parent on the equity method, the opening debit goes to the investment account (downstream) or to the investment account and NCI (upstream)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), the worksheet at 31 December 2026 for the equipment case. The unrealised gain at the start of the year is 8,000, and accumulated depreciation has to be cut by two years of correction, 4,000."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Downstream"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upstream"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in S 8,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in S 5,600 (70% × 8,000), Dr NCI 2,400 (30% × 8,000)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Accumulated depreciation 4,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Accumulated depreciation 4,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cr Equipment 10,000, Cr Depreciation expense 2,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cr Equipment 10,000, Cr Depreciation expense 2,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Each side totals 12,000. In P's books, Dr Investment in S / Cr Income from subsidiary, 2,000 downstream or 1,400 upstream, repeats each year to 2029."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"If the parent keeps the investment at cost in its separate statements, as PSAK 227 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Laporan Keuangan Tersendiri"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 4, IAS 27) allows, the opening debits go to its opening retained earnings instead (K2)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Sale outside the group."},{"type":"text","text":" Suppose the buyer sells the equipment to an outsider on 31 December 2027 for 25,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Buyer's carrying amount = 40,000 − 3 × 8,000 = 16,000; buyer's gain = 25,000 − 16,000 = 9,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Group's carrying amount = 30,000 − 3 × 6,000 = 12,000; group's gain = 25,000 − 12,000 = 13,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unrealised at the start of 2027 = 10,000 − 2 × 2,000 = 6,000: 2,000 through depreciation, 4,000 as extra gain"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Downstream"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upstream"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in S 6,000 / Cr Depreciation expense 2,000, Cr Gain on sale 4,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in S 4,200, Dr NCI 1,800 / Cr Depreciation expense 2,000, Cr Gain on sale 4,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The asset has left both ledgers, so no asset account is touched, and nothing remains to eliminate."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The notes' combined Pam–Sun example (three transfers at once) recomputes cleanly: income from Sun 90% × 80,000 + 5,000 + 90% × 4,000 − 32,000 + 4,000 = 52,600, NCI share 10% × 84,000 = 8,400. Its NCI entry is reversed. The notes write \"Noncontrolling interest $8.400 / Dividends $3.000 / Noncontrolling interest share $5.400\"; it should be Dr NCI share 8,400 / Cr Dividends 3,000; Cr NCI 5,400."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Sideways sales, losses and deferred tax"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Sales between two subsidiaries."},{"type":"text","text":" A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"sideways"},{"type":"text","text":" (lateral) sale is treated like an upstream sale from the seller's side: the gain is eliminated in full and borne by the seller's owners, the parent's effective share and the seller's NCI. BRPT's KCE–CAA/CCP land sale is of this kind. Note 45h names KCE first and Note 16 (PDF p. 137) shows KCE holding investment-property land, so KCE is the likely seller, though the note does not say so. BRPT owns 34.63 percent of CAP directly and 3.92 percent through Marigold, 38.55 percent in all, which it treats as control because CAP's board composition gives it power over CAP's relevant activities (Note 4, PDF p. 112; see "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"non-controlling-interest"},{"type":"text","text":" on control with less than a majority). CAP holds CDI at 60 percent; CDI holds KCE at 70 percent and CSP at 99.99 percent; CSP holds CCP at 100 percent; CAP holds CAA through CAP2 at 99.99 percent (Note 1d, PDF pp. 24–25)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"BRPT's effective interest in KCE = 38.55% × 60% × 70% = 16.19%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"BRPT's effective interest in CAA = 38.55% × 100% × 99.99% = 38.55%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"BRPT's effective interest in CCP = 38.55% × 60% × 99.99% × 100% = 23.13%"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), if KCE were the seller and the gain were 10,000, BRPT's owners would bear 16.19% × 10,000 = 1,619 of the elimination and the NCI at the three levels the other 8,381. Had CAA or CCP sold, BRPT's owners would bear about 3,855 or 2,313. The Mutual Holdings essay (K6) works the layered allocation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Losses."},{"type":"text","text":" A loss on an intercompany sale is eliminated the same way with the signs reversed, unless the price shows the asset has lost value. PSAK 110 para. B86 adds that intragroup losses may indicate an impairment that must be recognised in the consolidated statements. For example (illustration), equipment carried at 30,000 is sold within the group for 22,000. If its "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"recoverable amount"},{"type":"text","text":" under PSAK 236 (formerly PSAK 48, IAS 36), the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use, is also 22,000, the 8,000 loss is an impairment the group would have booked anyway, and it stays; otherwise it is eliminated."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Deferred tax."},{"type":"text","text":" The notes do not mention tax. The buyer's "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"tax base"},{"type":"text","text":", the amount the tax authority lets it deduct, is the transfer price, while the consolidated carrying amount is the group cost. The gap is a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"deductible temporary difference"},{"type":"text","text":", one that lowers future taxable profit, under PSAK 212 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Pajak Penghasilan"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12). For example (illustration), with a 22 percent rate, the equipment case at the end of 2025:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Temporary difference = 32,000 tax base (tax depreciation of 8,000 assumed equal to book) − 24,000 group carrying amount = 8,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax asset = 22% × 8,000 = 1,760; Dr Deferred tax asset 1,760 / Cr Income tax expense 1,760"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The asset unwinds by 22% × 2,000 = 440 a year as the gain is realised. PSAK 212 measures it at the buyer's rate, and PSAK 110 para. B86(c) sends these differences to PSAK 212. Indonesian land transfers bear a 2.5 percent final tax on the gross price (PP 34/2016), outside PSAK 212's mechanics. That tax is paid to the state, so it is a real group expense and is not eliminated (about 656 on 26,223 if fully taxable); the land elimination itself usually carries no deferred tax."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Intercompany leases"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An intragroup lease is not a lease for the group, which owns and uses the asset. Under PSAK 116 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Sewa"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 73, IFRS 16) the lessee books a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"right-of-use (ROU) asset"},{"type":"text","text":" and a lease liability at the present value of the payments, then depreciation and interest, while the lessor of an operating lease keeps the asset and books rental income. Consolidation removes the lessee's side and the rental income, leaving the asset and its depreciation in the lessor's books."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"BRPT's property segment (building and hotel management) earned 4,505 of inter-segment revenue in 2025, eliminated with 33 from \"others\" (Note 46, PDF p. 239); the note does not say whether it is rent."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), take 4,505 as an annual rent, paid in arrears for five years, discounted at 8 percent."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Present value = 4,505 × (1 − 1.08⁻⁵) / 0.08 = 4,505 × 3.99271 = 17,987.2"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lessee's year 1: depreciation = 17,987.2 / 5 = 3,597.4; interest = 8% × 17,987.2 = 1,439.0; total expense 5,036.4"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lessee's year-end balances: lease liability = 17,987.2 × 1.08 − 4,505 = 14,921.1; ROU asset = 17,987.2 − 3,597.4 = 14,389.7"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Rental income 4,505.0, Dr Lease liability 14,921.1 / Cr ROU asset 14,389.7, Cr Depreciation expense 3,597.4, Cr Interest expense 1,439.0"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both sides total 19,426.1. Consolidated profit in year 1 is 5,036.4 − 4,505.0 = 531.4 above the two companies' combined profit, because the lessee's front-loaded expense goes with the lessor's flat income; over five years the differences net to zero. An intragroup sale and leaseback is eliminated the same way: no gain, no ROU asset, and the asset stays at group cost."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Intercompany bonds: constructive retirement"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When one group company buys another's bonds from outside holders, the group has retired its own debt, and consolidation books a gain or loss the issuer never recorded. The owner's notes have no AKL Chapter 7 (in Beams, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intercompany Profit Transactions — Bonds"},{"type":"text","text":"), so this section follows Beams and PSAK 109 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Instrumen Keuangan"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9). PSAK 109 says a debt repurchased by its issuer is extinguished (para. B3.3.2) and the difference between its carrying amount and the price paid goes to profit or loss (para. 3.3.3). The consolidated statements therefore show a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"constructive"},{"type":"text","text":" gain or loss on retirement, one that exists only in the group's view, in the year of purchase."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), BRPT issued Rp 400 billion of Series A shelf-registration bonds in July 2025, equivalent to 24,487 (Note 1c, PDF p. 24). Take a simplified USD version: P has bonds with face value 20,000 outstanding, an 8 percent coupon paid each 31 December, and an unamortised discount of 400 with four years left. On 1 January S, 70 percent owned, buys half of them in the market for 10,300."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Carrying amount of the half in P's books = 10,000 − 200 = 9,800"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Constructive loss = 10,300 − 9,800 = 500"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The interest lines then no longer cancel. Following Beams, discount and premium are amortised straight-line."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"P's interest expense on the half = 800 + 200 / 4 = 850"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"S's interest income = 800 − 300 / 4 = 725"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Difference = 850 − 725 = 125 a year = 500 / 4"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Year of purchase"},{"type":"text","text":", at 31 December:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Bonds payable 10,000, Dr Interest income 725, Dr Loss on constructive retirement 500 / Cr Unamortised discount 150, Cr Interest expense 850, Cr Investment in bonds 10,225"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated profit is 500 − 125 = 375 below the combined profit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Next year"},{"type":"text","text":", the unrealised loss is 500 − 125 = 375 at the start:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Bonds payable 10,000, Dr Interest income 725, Dr Investment in S 375 (where P's equity-method entries carried the loss) / Cr Unamortised discount 100, Cr Interest expense 850, Cr Investment in bonds 10,150"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beams assigns the whole constructive gain or loss to the issuer, here P, so NCI is unaffected; if S were the issuer, its NCI would share it. PSAK 109 does not settle that allocation. And PSAK 109 requires the effective interest method, not straight-line; the total of 500 is the same, only its timing across the four years differs."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Intercompany asset transfers across industries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Power and utilities."},{"type":"text","text":" An in-house contractor's margin on a plant built for a group power producer is an intercompany profit on a depreciable asset, released over the plant's life, which BRPT sets at 20–30 years (Note 3n). The deferred margin, and any deferred tax asset on it, stays in the consolidated balance sheet for decades."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Property."},{"type":"text","text":" When a developer sells land from its inventory to a group hotel or rental subsidiary, the group eliminates the revenue and margin and reclassifies the land to fixed assets or investment property (PSAK 240, formerly PSAK 13, IAS 40) at the developer's cost."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Mining."},{"type":"text","text":" Heavy equipment sold to a group mine and depreciated on units of production releases the gain with output rather than with time."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Associates and joint ventures."},{"type":"text","text":" Under PSAK 228 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Investasi pada Entitas Asosiasi dan Ventura Bersama"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 15, IAS 28) para. 28, the investor recognises gains on upstream and downstream transactions only to the extent of unrelated investors' interests, so it eliminates only its own share. The IASB has proposed full recognition instead (ED/2024/7); redeliberations began in October 2025."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Presentation and analysis: Barito Pacific's Notes 3c, 14, 45 and 46"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A correct elimination makes the transaction disappear, so BRPT's FS show four traces only."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Trace"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What BRPT discloses"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Source"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Policy"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Intragroup assets, liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows \"eliminated in full on consolidation\""}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 3c, PDF p. 68"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Intra-group land sale"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"KCE with CAA and CCP, completed in 2025, 26,223"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 45h, PDF p. 237"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Associate"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"Elimination of gain on sale of land between CAP and SRI\", (11,440) in 2025 and 2024; carrying amount of the interest nil"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 14, PDF p. 125"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Segments"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Inter-segment revenue 4,538 eliminated (2024: 5,136); elimination column adds 9,339 to depreciation (2024: 8,897)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 46, PDF p. 239"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The land sale."},{"type":"text","text":" KCE, CAA and CCP are all consolidated, so the 26,223 should leave the group's land at its original cost, and it leaves no visible trace. The place to look is KCE's investment-property land (Note 16, PDF p. 137), which moved only by translation, from 16,622 to 16,507 (Note 16b, PDF p. 135). The note names neither the seller nor any gain."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The associate."},{"type":"text","text":" PT Synthetic Rubber Indonesia (SRI) is 45 percent owned through CAP. The group's share of SRI's net assets is 7,044 (45% × 15,652, rounded), against an 11,440 land-gain elimination and a carrying amount of nil, which the note explains. SRI's 2025 profit share, 45% × 16,965 = 7,634, is disclosed as unrecognised, because the elimination exceeds the group's share of net assets, so the interest stays at zero until cumulative profits cover the 11,440 (policy, Note 3, PDF p. 86). On that reading the balancing \"excess\" row should be 11,440 − 7,044 = 4,396 (2024: 11,440 + 592 = 12,032), not the 11,440 printed, a presentation slip. If 11,440 is the 45 percent share under PSAK 228 para. 28, the whole gain was 11,440 / 45% = 25,422, a reading of the arithmetic."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The segments."},{"type":"text","text":" The four segments add to 203,474 + 93,449 + 1,623 + 980 = 299,526, and the elimination column takes consolidated depreciation up to 308,865. Correcting depreciation on intercompany gains lowers group depreciation (on losses it raises it), as section 3 showed, so a positive 9,339 must be dominated by something else, most likely depreciation of fair value uplifts from acquisitions recorded only at group level. The note does not show how much intercompany asset profit, if any, the column contains."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Related essays"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"fixed-assets-net"},{"type":"text","text":": depreciation methods, useful lives and impairment, which this page corrects rather than re-teaches."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"non-controlling-interest"},{"type":"text","text":": NCI measurement and attribution, used in sections 2–5."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"right-of-use-assets-net"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"lease-liabilities-non-current"},{"type":"text","text":": the lessee's side of PSAK 116, eliminated in section 7."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"deferred-tax-assets"},{"type":"text","text":": the temporary difference on eliminated gains."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"long-term-investments"},{"type":"text","text":": associates and the PSAK 228 partial elimination."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"K1 (business combinations), K2 (the worksheet and elimination order), K3 (intercompany inventory), K5 (consolidated cash flows), K6 (mutual holdings and layered allocation)."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Least-confident assertions"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The seller in the KCE–CAA/CCP land sale is probably KCE (Note 16) but not stated, and the seller's gain is not disclosed. The effective interests (16.19, 38.55 and 23.13 percent) use year-end Note 1d percentages; CAP's stake in CDI fell from 70 to 60 percent in 2025, and at 70 percent the effective interest in KCE would be 18.89 percent."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The \"excess of share of losses\" row in Note 14 repeats 11,440 where 4,396 (2024: 12,032) would balance. The treatment is clear from the unrecognised-profit disclosure, so the row is read as a presentation error. In 2013–14 the IFRS Interpretations Committee favoured eliminating the full share and presenting any excess as a deferred gain; the IASB's narrow-scope amendment was never finalised, and BRPT floors the interest at nil instead."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading the positive 9,339 depreciation elimination in Note 46 as acquisition fair value depreciation is inference. BRPT does not explain the column, and the 4,505 of property inter-segment revenue may not be rent."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"That the land elimination carries no deferred tax rests on reading final tax (PP 34/2016) as outside PSAK 212's scope. The 22 percent rate ignores the reduced rate some listed companies qualify for."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Allocating a constructive bond gain or loss wholly to the issuer follows Beams. PSAK 109 is silent on the allocation to NCI, and other textbooks split it between issuer and holder."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Barito Pacific Tbk dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2025 and 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (PDF p. 14); Note 1c and 1d (PDF pp. 24–25); Note 3c (PDF p. 68); Note 3 equity-method policy (PDF p. 86); Note 3n (PDF p. 90); Note 4, control over CAP (PDF p. 112); Note 14, including the unrecognised share of profit (PDF p. 125); Note 16 (PDF pp. 135, 137); Note 45 (PDF pp. 235–237); Note 46 (PDF p. 239). BRPT FS. Page numbers checked against the PDF text layer; the useful-life table and Note 46 were read from the second extraction because the two-column text is garbled there."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes this page follows, \"AKL – Chapter 6 – Intercompany Profit Transactions: Plant Assets\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan), summarising Beams et al., "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advanced Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":": land downstream and upstream, sale to outsiders, depreciable assets, piecemeal recognition, the truck example and the combined Pam–Sun example. NOTES. Corrections made here: patents removed from the non-depreciable class (finite legal life); the elimination \"sebesar kepemilikan parent\" clarified as applying to the parent's income only; in the upstream land case the separate incomes are swapped ($180,000 for Son and $140,000 for Pop, where 90% × $140,000 = $126,000 is the figure used), \"$108.000 ($126.000 − $108.000)\" should read $126,000 − $18,000, the entry \"Investment 18,000 / Noncontrolling interest share 2,000 / Land 20,000\" belongs to later years and debits NCI, not NCI share, and the worksheet's NCI rows are swapped (1 January 60,000 plus share 12,000 = 72,000); the gross-form debit of $10,000 has no account named (Equipment); the truck's remaining life reads \"5 years\" where three is used; the combined example's NCI entry is reversed (Dr NCI share 8,400 / Cr Dividends 3,000, Cr NCI 5,400); the worksheet headers read \"80%\" in 90 percent cases; the combined worksheet's NCI rows are misplaced as in the upstream land worksheet. The remaining figures in the notes' examples recompute. Sections 6 (deferred tax, losses), 7 and 9 have no note."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"No owner's note exists for intercompany bonds (Beams Chapter 7 is missing from the set). Section 8 follows Beams, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advanced Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":", ch. 7 (constructive retirement, allocation to the issuer, straight-line amortisation), not on disk, NOT VERIFIED; and PSAK 109 (IFRS 9) paras. 3.3.3 and B3.3.2, VERIFIED (web, secondary: IFRIC 19 text on ifrs.org; ifrscommunity.com, \"Derecognition of financial liabilities\")."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 110 (IFRS 10) para. B86(c), elimination in full and intragroup losses as an impairment indicator: VERIFIED (web, secondary: IFRS Interpretations Committee agenda papers on B86(c); reproductions in annual reports and study material); the official text was not opened."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 228 (IAS 28) para. 28 and the meaning of \"unrelated investors\": VERIFIED (web, IFRIC Update January 2018; PKF IAS 28 guide; IASB staff paper CMAC March 2023). ED/2024/7 proposal and October 2025 redeliberations: VERIFIED (web, FAR and BDO comment letters; IASB AP13B, October 2025). Elimination exceeding the carrying amount: VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org AP15 July 2013 and AP12B January 2014; IAS Plus)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 212 (IAS 12): that eliminating intragroup profit in PPE creates a temporary difference VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation IAS 12 illustrative examples index, cross-referencing IFRS 10 B86(c)); measurement at the buyer's rate VERIFIED (web, secondary: Deloitte DART E.6; PwC Viewpoint 8.10). PP 34/2016, 2.5 percent final tax on the gross transfer value: VERIFIED (web, secondary: setkab.go.id; pajak.go.id)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 116 (IFRS 16), PSAK 216 (IAS 16) para. 58, PSAK 219 (IAS 38), PSAK 227 (IAS 27), PSAK 236 (IAS 36), PSAK 240 (IAS 40): numbers from the IAI renumbering rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n), VERIFIED in the sibling essays; content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beams, Anthony, Bettinghaus and Smith, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advanced Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":", chs. 2, 4, 6, 7, the textbook the lecture notes summarise. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED."}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb
