-- long-term-investments-classification-equity-method: content_json backfill (the document content was serialised from)
with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An investment's accounting follows the investor's power over the investee. Debt securities and small equity stakes are financial assets under PSAK 109, sorted by business model and cash-flow terms into amortised cost, fair value through other comprehensive income (FVOCI) or fair value through profit or loss (FVTPL). Significant influence or joint control of net assets brings the equity method, and control brings consolidation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The equity method books the investor's share of the investee's profit as income when it is earned and treats a dividend as cash leaving the investment. Any price paid above the investor's share of book value is allocated to the investee's assets and goodwill, and the allocation is unwound through the share of profit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Loss recognition stops when the investment reaches zero, impairment tests the whole investment as one asset, and losing significant influence remeasures what is left at fair value. A participating interest in an oil and gas block is normally a joint operation, recognised line by line in oil and gas properties instead of on this line."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report long term investments of 1,103,172 at 31 December 2024 against 1,602,821 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line fell 499,649, or 31.2 percent, and its share of total assets fell from 5.2 to 3.6 percent. Note 16 splits it into investments in oil and gas blocks (591,684), investments in associates and joint venture (511,425) and other investment (63) (Daftar 5/107, PDF p. 124). This page uses those numbers as the starting point for its examples."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After reading it a student should be able to classify an investment, record it under each measurement basis, run the equity method from acquisition to disposal, and say where other industries depart from these rules."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. What the line holds and why classification comes first"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso's "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intermediate Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":" (IFRS edition) opens its investments chapter with two motives for investing in another entity's securities: to earn a return on spare cash, and to secure an operating or financial relationship with the investee. The motive matters because the accounting follows what the investor can do with the investee, and that runs on a scale."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investor's position"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical holding"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Standard"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Measurement"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Creditor (debt securities)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Bonds, notes"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 109 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Financial Instruments"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Amortised cost, FVOCI or FVTPL"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Little or no influence"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equity below 20%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 109"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FVTPL, or FVOCI by election"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Significant influence (associate)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equity of 20–50%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 228 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 15, IAS 28)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equity method"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Joint control of net assets (joint venture)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shared by unanimous consent"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 111 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Joint Arrangements"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11) with PSAK 228"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equity method"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Control (subsidiary)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Usually above 50%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 110 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Consolidated Financial Statements"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated line by line"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The percentages are rebuttable presumptions. Holding 20 percent or more of the votes is presumed to give significant influence unless it can be clearly shown not to, and holding less is presumed not to (PSAK 228.5). Influence usually shows in board representation, a part in policy and dividend decisions, material transactions, interchange of managers, or essential technical information (para. 6)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso lists an investment in a subsidiary as a long-term investment. That holds only in the parent's "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"separate"},{"type":"text","text":" statements (section 9); in consolidated statements such as PHE's the subsidiary's assets and liabilities replace it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An equity-method investment stays non-current unless it is held for sale under PSAK 105 (formerly PSAK 58, IFRS 5) (PSAK 228.15); a bond moves to current assets when it falls due within twelve months."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE keeps its financial-asset investments on other lines. Its policy puts quoted FVOCI debt instruments in other non-current financial assets, elects FVOCI for unlisted equity and leaves listed equity at FVTPL (Note 3g, Daftar 5/40–41, PDF pp. 57–58). Sections 2 to 4 teach rules PHE applies there."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Debt investments: business model and contractual cash flows"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 109 classifies a debt investment by two tests. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"business model"},{"type":"text","text":" test asks whether the entity holds the asset to collect contractual cash flows, to collect and sell, or for neither (trading). The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"cash-flow characteristics"},{"type":"text","text":" test asks whether those cash flows are solely payments of principal and interest, as a plain bond's are. Together they give three categories."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Held to collect"},{"type":"text","text":" and passing the cash-flow test: amortised cost."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Held to collect and sell"},{"type":"text","text":" and passing the cash-flow test: FVOCI, meaning fair value on the balance sheet with changes parked in other comprehensive income (OCI), the part of comprehensive income kept out of profit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything else, including trading: FVTPL, with every fair value change in profit or loss."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Amortised cost"},{"type":"text","text":" is the amount at initial recognition, less principal repaid, plus or minus the cumulative amortisation of any premium or discount by the effective interest method, less any loss allowance."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary invests surplus dollars on 1 January in a five-year USD bond with face value 10,000 and a 4 percent annual coupon, when the market yield is 5 percent."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Price = 400 × annuity factor (5%, 5 years) + 10,000 × discount factor (5%, 5 years) = 9,567.05"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Year 1 interest revenue = 9,567.05 × 5% = 478.35; coupon received = 400.00; discount amortised = 78.35"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Amortised cost at year end = 9,567.05 + 78.35 = 9,645.40"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"At year end yields have fallen and the bond's fair value is 9,800."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Amortised cost"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FVOCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FVTPL"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Purchase"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Debt investment 9,567.05 / Cr Cash 9,567.05"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"same"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"same"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Interest"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 400.00, Dr Debt investment 78.35 / Cr Interest revenue 478.35"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"same"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"same"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Year end"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"no entry; carried at 9,645.40"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Fair value adjustment 154.60 / Cr OCI, unrealised holding gain 154.60"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Fair value adjustment 154.60 / Cr Unrealised holding gain, profit or loss 154.60"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Interest revenue is the same 478.35 under all three, because FVOCI keeps the effective interest in profit and only parks the fair value change. Under FVTPL the split is a presentation choice; only the 632.95 in profit is fixed. If the FVOCI bond is sold on 2 January for 9,800, the 154.60 leaves OCI and enters profit as a realised gain. This "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"reclassification adjustment"},{"type":"text","text":" (the notes' \"recycling\", their chapter's last topic) stops the gain being counted twice in comprehensive income."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 9,800.00 / Cr Debt investment 9,645.40, Cr Fair value adjustment 154.60"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr OCI, unrealised holding gain 154.60 / Cr Gain on sale of debt investment 154.60"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Impairment of debt investments: expected credit losses"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 109 replaced the incurred-loss model that older editions of Kieso teach, which waits for a loss event and discounts the lost cash flows, with "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"expected credit losses"},{"type":"text","text":" (ECL) from the day of purchase. Stage 1 carries twelve months of expected loss. Stage 2 carries lifetime loss once credit risk has risen significantly. Stage 3, when the borrower is credit-impaired (in default or severe distress), keeps lifetime loss and computes interest on the carrying amount net of the allowance."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"ECL = probability of default × loss given default × exposure at default"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), on the bond above:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stage 1: 0.5% × 45% × 10,000 = 22.50"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stage 2, lifetime default probability 6%: 6% × 45% × 10,000 = 270.00"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Impairment loss 22.50 / Cr Loss allowance 22.50"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For an FVOCI bond the loss still goes to profit, with the credit to OCI, because the asset is already at fair value."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Equity securities below significant influence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Below significant influence PSAK 109 starts from FVTPL. Any shares not held for trading (and not contingent consideration in a business combination) may be designated at initial recognition, share by share and irrevocably, to FVOCI. Strategic stakes, such as one held to secure supply, are the usual case."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys 1,000,000 shares of a listed drilling contractor at USD 0.50 a share to secure rig access, paying broker fees of 5. At year end the price is USD 0.46, a dividend of 20 has been received, and next year the shares are sold at USD 0.55."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FVTPL"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FVOCI (elected)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Purchase"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Equity investment 500, Dr Transaction cost expense 5 / Cr Cash 505"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Equity investment 505 / Cr Cash 505"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividend"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 20 / Cr Dividend income 20"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 20 / Cr Dividend income 20"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Year end, value 460"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Unrealised holding loss, profit or loss 40 / Cr Fair value adjustment 40"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr OCI 45 / Cr Fair value adjustment 45"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sale at 550"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 550, Dr Fair value adjustment 40 / Cr Equity investment 500, Cr Gain on sale 90"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Fair value adjustment 90 / Cr OCI 90; Dr Cash 550 / Cr Equity investment 505, Cr Fair value adjustment 45; cumulative OCI 45 may move to retained earnings"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso's example records broker fees as an expense, which is right only under FVTPL; under every other basis they are part of cost. A dividend is income in both columns, because the investor has never booked a share of the investee's profit; the one exception is a dividend that clearly repays part of the cost (IFRS 9 B5.7.1). The FVOCI gain never reaches profit, even on sale, because FVOCI equity, unlike FVOCI debt, is never recycled and has no impairment test. PHE's own policy states the same three rules (Note 3g)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Significant influence and the equity method"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Once the investor can influence dividend policy, dividend income stops measuring what it earns, because the investor could time its income by voting for or against a payout. Beams' "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advanced Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":" gives this as the reason fair value is forbidden at this level. PSAK 228 also rules it out, except for the fund exemption in section 9, and instead extends the investor's statements to include its share of the investee's profit or loss (para. 11)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The mechanism has three moves (para. 10)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Initial carrying amount = cost"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"+ investor's share of the investee's profit after acquisition (to profit or loss), and of its OCI (to OCI)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"− distributions received"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost includes directly attributable costs such as professional fees."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Take PHE's 2024 numbers for its 29 percent associate PT Donggi Senoro LNG (DSLNG) as the starting point: opening carrying amount 299,171, share of profit 53,860, dividends 49,300 (Note 16b, Daftar 5/108, PDF p. 125). The comparison follows Beams' layout. The left column shows only the dividend, because under PSAK 109 the stake would also be remeasured to fair value."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Below significant influence (PSAK 109, dividend line only)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equity method"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investee earns 185,700"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"no entry"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in associate 53,860 / Cr Share of profit of associates 53,860 (29% × 185,700 = 53,853; PHE books 53,860)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investee pays dividends, 29% = 49,300"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 49,300 / Cr Dividend income 49,300"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 49,300 / Cr Investment in associate 49,300"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Income for the year"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"49,300"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"53,860"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Carrying amount before other changes"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"299,171"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"299,171 + 53,860 − 49,300 = 303,731"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Booking the dividend as income under the equity method would count DSLNG's profit twice, when earned and when paid out."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beams calls this "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"one-line consolidation"},{"type":"text","text":". Profit and equity come out as if DSLNG had been consolidated, but on one line in each statement. The test is whether the carrying amount equals the investor's share of the investee's net assets plus any unamortised excess paid. With DSLNG's net assets of 1,334,590 − 417,540 = 917,050 at the end of 2024, PHE's share is 29% × 917,050 = 265,944.5, against a carrying amount of 265,952. The near-match suggests that little excess over book value remains inside PHE's investment."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Paying above book value and transactions with the investee"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When the price differs from the investor's share of book value, the difference is explained asset by asset. Beams allocates it in order to the investee's identifiable assets and liabilities at fair value, with the remainder as goodwill (PSAK 228.32). Goodwill stays inside the investment and is not amortised; amounts allocated to inventory or plant are unwound as the investee sells the inventory or depreciates the plant."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys 29 percent of an LNG company for 300,000 on 1 January. Book net assets are 900,000; the plant's fair value exceeds its book value by 100,000 over a remaining life of 25 years; LNG inventory exceeds book by 5,000. Deferred tax on the uplifts is ignored to keep the steps short."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Share of book value = 29% × 900,000 = 261,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Allocated to plant = 29% × 100,000 = 29,000; to inventory = 29% × 5,000 = 1,450"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill = 300,000 − 261,000 − 29,000 − 1,450 = 8,550"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"In year 1 the investee earns 180,000, sells the inventory, and pays dividends of 140,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Share of profit = 29% × 180,000 − 1,450 inventory − 29,000 / 25 plant = 52,200 − 1,450 − 1,160 = 49,590"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividends received = 29% × 140,000 = 40,600"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Closing carrying amount = 300,000 + 49,590 − 40,600 = 308,990"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Investment in associate 49,590 / Cr Share of profit of associates 49,590"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 40,600 / Cr Investment in associate 40,600"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"If the price had been 285,000, the excess over book value (24,000) would fall short of the fair value uplift (30,450). That 6,450 is a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"bargain purchase"},{"type":"text","text":", included as income in the share of profit in the year of acquisition (para. 32(b)). Beams also describes an older US treatment that used it to reduce non-current assets instead."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit on sales between investor and investee is eliminated to the extent of the investor's interest (para. 28). For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary sells gas to a 29 percent associate at a profit of 2,000, and the associate still holds the LNG at year end; the 580 below is recognised when that LNG is sold to an outside buyer."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unrealised profit eliminated = 29% × 2,000 = 580: Dr Share of profit of associates 580 / Cr Investment in associate 580"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Losses, impairment and the end of the equity method"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Losses beyond zero."},{"type":"text","text":" The investor stops recognising its share of losses once its interest reaches zero (para. 38). The interest includes long-term interests that are in substance part of the net investment, such as a loan with no planned repayment, but not trade receivables or secured loans. Beyond that a liability arises only for legal or constructive obligations or payments made on the investee's behalf (para. 39). For example (illustration), a 25 percent exploration associate carried at 6,000 drills a dry well and loses 40,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Share of loss = 25% × 40,000 = 10,000; recognised = 6,000; unrecognised, tracked outside the accounts = 4,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Share of loss of associates 6,000 / Cr Investment in associate 6,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"If it earns 20,000 next year, the share of 5,000 first covers the 4,000 unrecognised loss, and only 1,000 is booked."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 228 is silent on the mirror case, a dividend above a carrying amount already at zero. With no obligation to repay, practice takes the excess to profit, as PHE did in 2023 (section 10)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Impairment."},{"type":"text","text":" Objective evidence of impairment includes financial difficulty, default, and adverse changes in the investee's market; for equity, a significant or prolonged fall in fair value below cost is itself evidence (paras. 41A–41C). The whole carrying amount is then tested under PSAK 236 (formerly PSAK 48, IAS 36) as a single asset against its recoverable amount, the higher of value in use and fair value less costs of disposal (para. 42). Because no part of the loss is allocated to the goodwill inside, the whole loss can later be reversed. For example (illustration), take Seplat's 2024 carrying amount of 214,773 (Note 16b) and suppose the market value of the stake fell to 180,000, costs of disposal were 2,000, and value in use were 195,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Recoverable amount = higher of 195,000 and 180,000 − 2,000 = 195,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Impairment loss = 214,773 − 195,000 = 19,773: Dr Impairment loss 19,773 / Cr Investment in associate 19,773"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Discontinuing."},{"type":"text","text":" When significant influence ends, the retained interest becomes a financial asset at fair value, and the difference between that fair value plus any proceeds and the carrying amount goes to profit (para. 22(b)). Amounts in OCI are recycled as if the investee had disposed of the related assets (para. 22(c)). For example (illustration), PHE sells 19 of its 29 percent of DSLNG for 190,000 and the remaining 10 percent is worth 100,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gain = 190,000 + 100,000 − 265,952 = 24,048"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 190,000, Dr Equity investment (FVOCI or FVTPL) 100,000 / Cr Investment in associate 265,952, Cr Gain on loss of significant influence 24,048"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beams, following US GAAP of its time, carries the retained stake at its old book value with no gain, and restates retained earnings retroactively when a stake grows from 10 to 20 percent. PSAK 228 does neither, and PHE's policy remeasures any retained investment at fair value (Note 3l, Daftar 5/55, PDF p. 72)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Joint arrangements: joint ventures and joint operations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"joint arrangement"},{"type":"text","text":" exists when two or more parties share control and relevant decisions need unanimous consent. PSAK 111 then asks what the parties hold. Parties to a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"joint venture"},{"type":"text","text":" have rights only to the net assets and use the equity method. Parties to a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"joint operation"},{"type":"text","text":" have rights to the assets and obligations for the liabilities, and each recognises its own share of assets, liabilities, revenue and expenses. PHE's policy uses this split and adds that the structure, the legal form of any separate vehicle and the contractual terms decide it (Note 3y, Daftar 5/73–74, PDF pp. 90–91)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), PHE holds a 10 percent participating interest in a block, and the operator spends 50,000 on a development well."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Joint operation (direct interest)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Joint venture (interest held through a company)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Oil and gas properties 5,000 / Cr Cash call payable 5,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"No entry until the company reports profit; then Dr Investment / Cr Share of profit"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A participating interest held directly under a joint operating agreement is the usual upstream case. Where a majority of partners decide, no joint control exists and the arrangement falls outside PSAK 111, but each partner still recognises its own share. PHE nonetheless carries its Malaysian block interests on this line at cost, which its policy note does not explain (section 10)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Long-term investments across industries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Industries with their own rules move the boundaries of section 1's scale, mostly toward fair value."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Banks."},{"type":"text","text":" A bank's securities book is classified portfolio by portfolio by business model, so the same government bond can be at amortised cost in the treasury's hold-to-collect book and at FVTPL on the trading desk. A reclassification is allowed only when the business model itself changes, and then prospectively from the first day of the next reporting period, with no restatement; FVOCI equity is never reclassified (PSAK 109; ref. 6)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Insurers, venture capital and funds."},{"type":"text","text":" An associate or joint venture held by a venture capital organisation, mutual fund, unit trust or investment-linked insurance fund may be measured at FVTPL instead of the equity method, investee by investee at initial recognition (PSAK 228.18). For example (illustration), a fund holds 25 percent of a start-up bought for 1,000; the start-up earns 400 and the stake's fair value rises to 1,250."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equity method income = 25% × 400 = 100; FVTPL income = 1,250 − 1,000 = 250"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Investment entities."},{"type":"text","text":" An investment entity carries even its subsidiaries at FVTPL instead of consolidating them. A subsidiary that is not itself an investment entity and provides services for the parent's investment activities is still consolidated (PSAK 110.31–32; ref. 7)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Islamic finance."},{"type":"text","text":" Sharia investments follow the PSAK 4xx series. A sukuk holder classifies the investment under PSAK 410 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Akuntansi Sukuk"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 110), which since its 2015 revision mirrors PSAK 109: amortised cost, FVOCI or FVTPL by business model and contractual terms (ref. 7). A musyarakah partner under PSAK 406 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Akuntansi Musyarakah"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 106) records its capital contribution as an investment in the partnership (STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Separate financial statements."},{"type":"text","text":" A parent's own statements may carry subsidiaries, associates and joint ventures at cost, under PSAK 109, or by the equity method (PSAK 227). This is the case Kieso's presentation describes."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 16"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 112 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Disclosure of Interests in Other Entities"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 67, IFRS 12) asks for each material associate's summarised financial information and a reconciliation to its carrying amount. PHE gives the movement and summarised figures for DSLNG and Seplat (Note 16b, Daftar 5/108, PDF p. 125)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Opening"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Share of profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Other changes"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividends"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Closing"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Donggi Senoro LNG, 29%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"299,171"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"53,860"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(37,779)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(49,300)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"265,952"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Seplat, 20.46%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"211,954"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31,177"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(9,576)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(18,782)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"214,773"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Others"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3,113"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"39,905"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(12,255)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"30,763"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Total"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"514,238"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"124,942"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"(59,610)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"(68,082)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"511,488"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"DSLNG reconciles and Seplat does not."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"DSLNG: share of net assets 265,944.5 against 265,952 (section 5); share of profit 29% × 185,700 = 53,853 against 53,860."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"20.46% of Seplat's net assets of 6,396,881 − 4,554,275 = 1,842,606 is 376,997, against 214,773. PHE's policy takes the share of an associate's profit after non-controlling interests in the associate's own subsidiaries (Note 3l, Daftar 5/55, PDF p. 72), so part of Seplat's 1,842,606 may belong to its minorities; the note gives no split."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The table's total exceeds Note 16's 511,425 by 63, the amount of \"other investment\"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Associates turned just over half their 2024 profit into cash."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cash conversion = dividends / share of profit = 68,082 / 124,942 = 54.5%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Return on equity-method investments = 124,942 / ((514,238 + 511,488) / 2) = 24.4%"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"Others\" (39,905 of profit on an opening 3,113) inflates the second ratio. DSLNG and Seplat alone earn 85,037 / ((511,125 + 480,725) / 2) = 17.1% and pay out 68,082 / 85,037 = 80.1%."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Other income, net, carries the share of associates as 162,620 = 124,942 − 11,236 + 48,914 of \"dividend receivables actualisation\" (Note 42, Daftar 5/150–151, PDF pp. 167–168). The 11,236 reverses a 2023 gain that Note 16b explains as a dividend from PRDL, a Venezuelan associate, above the investment's carrying amount (section 7). Note 42's footnote calls the reversal a restatement of Seplat's minority interests, and the FS does not reconcile the two."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The larger component, investments in oil and gas blocks (591,684, or 53.6 percent of the line), fits none of the textbook categories. Note 16a describes it as investments of the subsidiary PIEP in Malaysian Blocks K, SK-309, SK-311 and SK-314A, measured at cost (Daftar 5/107, PDF p. 124). Note 2d lists the same blocks among the Group's foreign participating interests (Block K 24 percent, SK 309 and SK 311 25.5 percent each, the last two under joint operating contracts; Daftar 5/20, PDF p. 37). The schedule treats them like a cost-model asset, with amortisation of 81,381 and impairment of 2,263 in 2023, and an impairment test with no loss in 2024. The Malaysian balance moved 609,421 + 45,966 − 63,703 = 591,684. The Iraqi balance of 479,099 left the line in full, labelled \"termination of recognition\", although Note 2f still lists the 20 percent West Qurna-1 interest. That deduction accounts for most of the line's 499,649 fall. The FS gives no policy for the category and does not say where the 479,099 went."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Upstream note: blocks, service contracts and partners abroad"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Iraqi interest is a 20 percent share of the West Qurna-1 technical service contract, under which contractors earn remuneration and cost recovery fees instead of a share of production (Note 2f, Daftar 5/23–24, PDF pp. 40–41). Seplat and PRDL are held through Maurel & Prom, a 71.09 percent subsidiary domiciled in Paris (Note 1, Daftar 5/8, PDF p. 25; Note 42). The 20.46 percent is M&P's holding, consolidated in full, so PHE's look-through interest in Seplat is 71.09% × 20.46% = 14.5% and 28.91 percent of M&P's result belongs to non-controlling interests. The line mixes an Indonesian LNG plant, oil producers in Nigeria and Venezuela, and Malaysian contract interests."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"12. Related lines"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"oil-gas-properties-net"},{"type":"text","text":": where directly held participating interests (joint operations) are normally recognised."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-non-current-assets"},{"type":"text","text":": where PHE keeps its quoted FVOCI debt instruments and other financial-asset investments."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/production-expenses","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-income-expense-net"},{"type":"text","text":": where PHE presents its share of profit of associates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-receivables-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-receivables-net"},{"type":"text","text":": dividends declared but not yet received."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/cash-and-cash-equivalents","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"cash-and-cash-equivalents"},{"type":"text","text":": short-dated debt instruments classified as cash equivalents instead of investments."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-comprehensive-income"},{"type":"text","text":": the OCI side of FVOCI investments and of associates' OCI."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Least-confident assertions"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why PHE presents its Malaysian participating interests on this line at cost, and where the Iraqi 479,099 went, are not disclosed. Reading the category as a contract interest measured like a cost-model asset rests on the movement schedule alone."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Non-controlling interests inside Seplat are the cause the FS itself points to (Note 3l; Note 42 footnote) for the gap between 376,997 and 214,773, but the split is not given, and acquisition-date fair value adjustments from PHE's purchase of M&P may also play a part. The completion of Seplat's Mobil Producing Nigeria acquisition on 12 December 2024 was checked on the web; its effect on the gap was not."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Taking a dividend above a zero carrying amount to profit follows common practice, which PSAK 228 does not state. The Note 16b and Note 42 descriptions of the 11,236 (PRDL dividend; Seplat minority restatement) were not reconciled."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The musyarakah treatment under PSAK 406 and the separate-statement options under PSAK 227 are stated from knowledge; their texts were not opened."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"IFRS 9 paragraph numbers are not given for the classification tests because the official text was not opened; the tests themselves were checked against secondary sources."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); Note 1 (Daftar 5/8, PDF p. 25); Note 2d (Daftar 5/20, PDF p. 37); Note 2f (Daftar 5/23–24, PDF pp. 40–41); Note 3g (Daftar 5/40–41, PDF pp. 57–58); Note 3l (Daftar 5/54–55, PDF pp. 71–72); Note 3y (Daftar 5/73–74, PDF pp. 90–91); Note 16 (Daftar 5/107–108, PDF pp. 124–125); Note 42 (Daftar 5/150–151, PDF pp. 167–168). PHE cites PSAK by the new numbers (e.g. \"PSAK 216 (sebelumnya PSAK 16)\"). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes this series follows, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 17 – Investment\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan; Kieso IFRS edition): motives, classification by business model and cash flows, amortised cost with the effective interest method, HFCS at fair value, trading, equity holdings below 20 percent (trading and non-trading), 20–50 percent equity method, above 50 percent consolidation, impairment, recycling. Incurred-loss impairment corrected to ECL; \"three months\" for trading and \"subsidiary as long-term investment\" qualified. Impairment and recycling, last in the notes, are placed next to debt investments, where PSAK 109 applies them. NOTES."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes, \"AKL – Chapter 2 – Stock Investment\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan; Beams "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advanced Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":"): fair value/cost method against the equity method, one-line consolidation, acquisition costs, allocation of excess cost over book value, bargain purchase, interim and step acquisitions, sale of interest, purchase directly from the investee, investees with preference shares. US GAAP treatments of negative goodwill, step acquisitions and loss of influence corrected to PSAK 228; old PSAK 22 reference replaced by PSAK 103. Interim acquisitions, purchases directly from the investee and preference-share investees are in the notes but not covered here. Losses beyond zero, impairment of an associate and joint arrangements have no note and follow IAS 28 and IFRS 11. NOTES."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Textbooks the lecture notes summarise: Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intermediate Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":", IFRS edition, chapter \"Investments\" (edition inferred as 3rd, not checked); Beams, Anthony, Bettinghaus and Smith, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advanced Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":", chapter 2 \"Stock Investments: Investor Accounting and Reporting\" (edition inferred as 13th, not checked). Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 228 (IAS 28 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures"},{"type":"text","text":"), paras. 5–6, 10–11, 15, 18, 22, 28, 32, 38–39, 41A–43. VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation text of IAS 28, 2018 edition, hosted by ICAB). Inclusion of directly attributable costs in cost: IFRS Interpretations Committee, July 2009, NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 109 (IFRS 9): FVOCI election for equity, no recycling, dividends to profit and the B5.7.1 exception. VERIFIED (web, BDO "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"IFRS 9 explained"},{"type":"text","text":"; ifrscommunity.com; IFRS Foundation, \"Sale of equity investments measured at fair value through OCI\" agenda papers; PHE Note 3g). Amortised-cost conditions: VERIFIED (web, secondary: ICAM IFRS 9 guidance, 2018). Reclassification on a change of business model: VERIFIED (web, HMRC CFM21880). ECL stages and B5.2.3: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 110 (IFRS 10) paras. 31–32: VERIFIED (web, IFRS 10 text in EU Regulation 2016/1703). PSAK 410 classification categories: VERIFIED (web, secondary: IAI, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"PSAK Syariah for Academics: Akuntansi Sukuk"},{"type":"text","text":"; Kontan, Feb 2015). PSAK 110, 111, 112, 227, 236, 105, 117, 406, 410: numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia"},{"type":"text","text":"); other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Seplat's completion of the Mobil Producing Nigeria Unlimited acquisition, 12 December 2024: VERIFIED (web, S&P Global Commodity Insights, 12 Dec 2024)."}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb
