-- non-controlling-interest-control-attribution-and-ownership-changes: content_json backfill (the document content was serialised from)
with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidation adds 100 percent of a controlled subsidiary's assets, liabilities, income and expenses, even when the parent owns less than all of it. The non-controlling interest (NCI) is the outside owners' share of that subsidiary's equity. It is equity of the group, presented separately from the parent's owners' equity, and it absorbs its share of profit, other comprehensive income (OCI) and losses, even into a deficit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"At acquisition the acquirer chooses, deal by deal, to measure NCI at fair value or at its proportionate share of the identifiable net assets (PSAK 103). At the acquisition date the choice changes goodwill and NCI by the same amount and nothing else."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"While control lasts, buying from or selling to the NCI is a transaction between owners. No gain or loss is recognised, no new goodwill arises, and the difference goes to the parent's equity. Only gaining or losing control is remeasured through profit or loss."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report non-controlling interest of 674,235 at 31 December 2024 against 644,429 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 4.5 percent of total equity (4.5 percent a year earlier) and 2.2 percent of total assets (2.1 percent). Of the year's profit of 3,121,035, 119,560 is attributed to NCI (Daftar 2/2, PDF p. 15)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After reading this page a student should be able to measure NCI at acquisition, roll it forward through profit, OCI and dividends, record a change in ownership with and without loss of control, compute NCI in a chain of holdings, and read a group's NCI disclosures."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. What a non-controlling interest is"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI exists because consolidation is all-or-nothing on the asset side and proportionate on the ownership side. PSAK 110 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) defines it as equity in a subsidiary not attributable, directly or indirectly, to a parent."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The lecture notes (AKL Chapter 3) explain why the group is reported as one entity. Separate legal companies under one management are one economic unit, so their statements are combined line by line and the parent's investment account is replaced by the subsidiary's assets and liabilities. At 90 percent ownership all of the subsidiary's cash is still consolidated, and the outsiders' 10 percent is shown as a claim inside equity."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Control decides who consolidates."},{"type":"text","text":" The notes date from the old PSAK 4 era and say control is usually obtained through \"hak mayoritas (lebih dari 50%)\", with exceptions for indirect holdings. PSAK 110 replaced that with three elements, all of which must be present: power over the investee's relevant activities, exposure or rights to variable returns, and the ability to use that power to affect those returns. PHE's policy repeats them (Note 3c, Daftar 5/33, PDF p. 50) and adds that, with less than a majority of votes, it considers contractual arrangements, other contractual rights and potential voting rights (Daftar 5/34, PDF p. 51). Majority ownership is the usual route to control, but the test is the three elements. A 45 percent holder whose remaining shares are spread among thousands of small holders may control; a 60 percent holder whose shareholder agreement gives the 40 percent holder a veto over the budget may not."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The notes' PSAK 4 exclusions, temporary control and restrictions on transferring funds, no longer exist; a subsidiary held for sale is consolidated under PSAK 105 (IFRS 5)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"NCI is equity."},{"type":"text","text":" The notes first place NCI \"pada bagian sesi liabilities ... dibawah heading non current liabilities\" and then cite GAAP: NCI \"harus ditampilkan dalam balancesheet sebagai bagian yang terpisah dari komponen equity\", which can be misread as outside equity. The current rule puts it within equity, separately from the parent's owners' equity (PSAK 110 para. 22). The outside shareholders own residual interests that nobody owes them, so they are not a liability. The same logic places NCI's share of profit below profit for the year as an attribution, not as an expense."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit for the year = profit attributable to owners of the parent + profit attributable to NCI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE 2024: 3,121,035 = 3,001,475 + 119,560"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Measuring NCI at acquisition"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When a parent buys less than 100 percent of a business, the subsidiary's identifiable assets and liabilities still enter the group at full fair value under PSAK 103 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3). The question is what figure to put on the NCI. PSAK 103 para. 19 gives a choice, made separately for each business combination, for NCI that are present ownership interests entitling holders to a proportionate share of net assets on liquidation:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"fair value"},{"type":"text","text":", usually the market price of the shares the parent did not buy, or"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"proportionate share"},{"type":"text","text":" of the acquiree's identifiable net assets."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill is the residual in both cases."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill = consideration transferred + NCI + fair value of previously held interest − identifiable net assets at fair value"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's policy states that it makes this choice for each combination (Note 3d, Daftar 5/35, PDF p. 52)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys 60 percent of a listed oilfield-services company for 300,000 cash. The target's book net assets are 350,000. Its equipment is worth 50,000 more than book, with ten years of life left, so identifiable net assets at fair value are 400,000. The 40 percent left in public hands trades at a total market value of 180,000."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fair value method"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Proportionate share method"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"180,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"40% × 400,000 = 160,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"300,000 + 180,000 − 400,000 = 80,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"300,000 + 160,000 − 400,000 = 60,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Goodwill recognised for"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"parent and NCI (\"full goodwill\")"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"parent only"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 20,000 gap is the NCI's share of goodwill. The fair value method books it, and the proportionate method leaves it out. Nothing else differs. The elimination entry on the consolidation worksheet under the proportionate method:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Share capital and retained earnings, subsidiary 350,000; Dr Equipment 50,000; Dr Goodwill 60,000 / Cr Investment in subsidiary 300,000; Cr NCI 160,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The lecture notes measure NCI by grossing up the price, as Beams does: \"Implied fair value of sun ($10.200.000÷90%)\", so NCI is 10 percent of that implied value. PSAK 103 asks for the NCI's own fair value instead, because the parent's price per share usually includes a control premium that the outside shares do not carry. Grossing up the illustration gives 300,000 / 60% = 500,000, NCI of 200,000 and goodwill of 100,000, which overstates both by 20,000 against the market price of the minority shares."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Acquisition costs are expensed under either method (Note 3d). Goodwill itself and its impairment are covered in the Intangible Assets essay."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Profit, OCI and dividends after acquisition"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After acquisition NCI moves with three things: its share of the subsidiary's profit after consolidation adjustments, its share of OCI, and dividends paid to the outside owners."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI at end = NCI at start + NCI share of profit + NCI share of OCI − dividends to NCI ± changes in ownership"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The share of profit is computed on the subsidiary's profit "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"as the group sees it"},{"type":"text","text":". Extra depreciation on fair value uplifts reduces it. So do unrealised profits on upstream sales (a subsidiary selling to its parent), which the notes for AKL Chapter 5 allocate between parent and NCI, while downstream profits fall wholly on the parent."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), continue the section 2 subsidiary under the proportionate method. In its first year it reports profit of 70,000, pays dividends of 30,000, and records a translation loss in OCI of 10,000. The equipment uplift of 50,000 depreciates over ten years."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Group-basis profit = 70,000 − 50,000 / 10 = 65,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of profit = 40% × 65,000 = 26,000; parent share = 60% × 65,000 = 39,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of OCI = 40% × (10,000) = (4,000)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividends to NCI = 40% × 30,000 = 12,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI at end = 160,000 + 26,000 − 4,000 − 12,000 = 170,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The check is that NCI equals 40 percent of the subsidiary's net assets at group carrying amounts, 400,000 + 65,000 − 10,000 − 30,000 = 425,000, and 40% × 425,000 = 170,000. Under the fair value method NCI would be 190,000, the same 20,000 of goodwill above it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The worksheet entries follow the order in the notes for AKL Chapter 4, with the parent using the equity method in its own books:"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Step"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Entry"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Remove the parent's equity-method income, OCI share and dividends"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Income from subsidiary 39,000 / Cr Dividends 18,000; Cr Investment in subsidiary 21,000; and Dr Investment in subsidiary 6,000 / Cr Translation reserve 6,000 to reverse the parent's own OCI entry"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Record NCI's share"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr NCI share of profit 26,000 / Cr Dividends 12,000; Cr NCI 14,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Eliminate opening equity, recognise uplift, goodwill and opening NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"the section 2 entry"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Depreciate the uplift"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Depreciation expense 5,000 / Cr Equipment 5,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Attribute OCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr NCI 4,000 / Cr Translation reserve 4,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The parent's investment closes at 300,000 + 39,000 − 18,000 − 6,000 = 315,000, and steps 1 and 3 remove 300,000 + 21,000 − 6,000 = 315,000 of it. Step 5 then puts the translation loss into the group once, 6,000 to the parent and 4,000 to NCI."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"PHE's numbers."},{"type":"text","text":" PHE attributes 119,560 of profit and 111,073 of total comprehensive income to NCI (Daftar 2/2, PDF p. 15), so NCI's share of OCI was 111,073 − 119,560 = (8,487). The group's whole OCI was 3,083,675 − 3,121,035 = (37,360)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of profit = 119,560 / 3,121,035 = 3.8%; NCI share of OCI = 8,487 / 37,360 = 22.7%"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI took a far larger share of the OCI loss than of profit. Elnusa, 51.10 percent owned (Note 1b, Daftar 5/6, PDF p. 23), is the only group entity with the rupiah as functional currency (Note 3a, Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46), so the translation loss of 14,810 is its loss."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of translation loss = 48.90% × 14,810 = 7,242, about 85% of NCI's OCI loss of 8,487"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The hedge loss of 21,940 arises in near-wholly-owned companies, so NCI takes little of it. PHE does not attribute OCI by subsidiary."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Losses that exceed the NCI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Losses are attributed to NCI even when this drives NCI into deficit (PSAK 110 para. B94). PHE's policy says so in the same words (Note 3c, Daftar 5/34, PDF p. 51). The older rule in PSAK 4 and pre-2008 IAS 27 charged the excess to the parent unless the minority had a binding obligation to fund it, and later profits went to the parent until the losses it had absorbed were recovered."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a 70 percent-owned subsidiary has net assets of 20,000, so NCI is 30% × 20,000 = 6,000. It then loses 50,000."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Current rule (PSAK 110)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Old rule (PSAK 4, pre-2008 IAS 27)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Loss attributed to NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"30% × 50,000 = 15,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6,000, capped at the NCI balance"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Loss attributed to parent"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"70% × 50,000 = 35,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"50,000 − 6,000 = 44,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI after the loss"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6,000 − 15,000 = (9,000)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"0"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A deficit NCI is the outside owners' share of a subsidiary whose liabilities exceed its assets. It does not mean they owe the group anything."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Changes in ownership without loss of control"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 110 para. 23 treats any change in the parent's ownership that leaves control intact as a transaction with owners in their capacity as owners. Once the group view is taken, the group has not sold or bought any asset. Shares in the group have changed hands between two classes of its owners. So:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"no gain or loss is recognised and goodwill is not remeasured;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI is adjusted to reflect the new relative interests;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"the difference between the consideration and the NCI adjustment goes directly to equity attributable to the parent (para. B96)."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The notes for AKL Chapter 8 state the same rule: if control is kept, the sale \"akan diperlukan sebagai equity transaction. Tidak ada gain or losses yang diakui.\" They record the difference in additional paid-in capital, a US practice. PSAK names no equity account; Indonesian groups commonly use an \"other equity component\" or a reserve for transactions with NCI, and PHE's statement of changes in equity has a column for \"equity adjustments (other equity component)\" (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), take the section 3 subsidiary at the start of its second year: net assets at group carrying amounts 425,000, parent 60 percent, NCI 170,000."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Parent sells 5% for 25,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Parent buys 20% from NCI for 80,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ownership after"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"55% parent, 45% NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"80% parent, 20% NCI"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI adjustment"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"+5% × 425,000 = +21,250"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"−20% × 425,000 = −85,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Difference to parent's equity"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"25,000 − 21,250 = 3,750 credit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"85,000 − 80,000 = 5,000 credit"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Entry"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 25,000 / Cr NCI 21,250; Cr Other equity 3,750"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr NCI 85,000 / Cr Cash 80,000; Cr Other equity 5,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under the proportionate method the NCI adjustment above uses net assets only. Whether a share of goodwill should also move to or from NCI is a policy question that PSAK 110 does not settle, and practice differs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"In the parent's separate statements, with the investment at cost under PSAK 227 (IAS 27), sale (a) shows no gain, because the 5 percent cost 300,000 × 5/60 = 25,000. At any other price the gain or loss there would be moved into equity on consolidation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The subsidiary issues shares to outsiders."},{"type":"text","text":" The parent's percentage falls without the parent selling anything. The notes use a subsidiary with 10,000 shares and equity of 200,000, 80 percent held by the parent, issuing 2,000 new shares to outside investors at 35 each."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equity after = 200,000 + 2,000 × 35 = 270,000; parent's share = 8,000 / 12,000 = 66.67%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Parent's interest = 66.67% × 270,000 = 180,000, against 80% × 200,000 = 160,000 before"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI = 33.33% × 270,000 = 90,000, against 40,000 before"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consolidated entry: Dr Cash 70,000 / Cr NCI 50,000; Cr Other equity 20,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Issuing below book value moves equity the other way. At 15 per share the notes find a decrease of 6,667. A subsidiary buying back its own shares from outside holders works in reverse. The parent's percentage rises, NCI falls by the share bought back, and any difference again stays in equity."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Accumulated OCI, such as a translation reserve, is reallocated to the new percentages (PSAK 221 para. 48C; see the OCI essay)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Gaining and losing control"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Control obtained in stages."},{"type":"text","text":" When a parent that already owns part of an investee gains control, the earlier holding is remeasured to fair value at the acquisition date and the gain or loss goes to profit or loss (PSAK 103 para. 42). PHE's policy says this (Note 3d, Daftar 5/35, PDF p. 52). The notes for AKL Chapter 8 give an example: 5 percent bought for 7,000 and another 5 percent for 8,000, then 80 percent for 210,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Implied value of 100% = 210,000 / 80% = 262,500"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fair value of the earlier 10% = 10% × 262,500 = 26,250; carrying amount = 7,000 + 8,000 = 15,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gain on remeasurement = 26,250 − 15,000 = 11,250"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The notes write the calculation as \"($262.500 -$7.000 - $8.000)\", which does not give 11,250. The steps follow the notes in carrying the earlier 10 percent at cost and valuing it from the parent's price. Under PSAK 109 the stake would already be at fair value, so the para. 42 gain is only the movement since the last reporting date, and it stays in OCI if the FVOCI election was made. Valuing it at 10% of 262,500 also assumes no control premium, the simplification section 2 criticised."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Control lost."},{"type":"text","text":" When the parent loses control it derecognises the subsidiary's assets (including goodwill) and liabilities and the NCI, recognises the consideration and any retained interest at fair value, and takes the difference to profit or loss (PSAK 110 paras. 25 and B98–B99). Amounts in OCI are reclassified as if the parent had disposed of the assets directly, so the translation reserve is recycled."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gain or loss = (fair value of consideration + fair value of retained interest + carrying amount of NCI) − (carrying amount of net assets + goodwill)"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The notes give this formula under the heading \"Sale of an interest resulting in consolidation\"; the event is a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"de"},{"type":"text","text":"consolidation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), back in the section 3 subsidiary with the parent at 60 percent: net assets 425,000, goodwill 60,000, NCI 170,000, and the parent's share of the translation reserve 60% × (10,000) = (6,000). The parent sells 40 percent for 230,000 and keeps 20 percent, now an associate, with a fair value of 115,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gain = (230,000 + 115,000 + 170,000) − (425,000 + 60,000) = 515,000 − 485,000 = 30,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation reserve recycled = (6,000); net gain in profit or loss = 24,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 230,000; Dr Investment in associate 115,000; Dr NCI 170,000 / Cr Net assets of subsidiary 425,000; Cr Goodwill 60,000; Cr Gain on loss of control 30,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Loss on reclassification of translation reserve 6,000 / Cr Translation reserve 6,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 115,000 becomes the cost of the associate under the equity method. The Long-Term Investments essay covers that method."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Indirect holdings"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a chain of ownership the NCI in a grandchild has two layers, its own outside owners and the outside owners of the intermediate company. The notes for AKL Chapter 9 recommend an allocation schedule worked from the bottom up: each company first takes its share of the company below it, then passes its own profit up."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE has two such chains in Note 1. Elnusa, 51.10 percent owned, owns subsidiaries such as PT Elnusa Petrofin at 99.99 percent. Etablissements Maurel et Prom (M&P), listed in Paris, is 71.09 percent owned by PT Pertamina Internasional Eksplorasi dan Produksi (PIEP), which PHE owns at 99.999998 percent (Note 1b, Daftar 5/4, 5/6 and 5/8, PDF pp. 21, 23, 25)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's effective interest in Elnusa Petrofin = 51.10% × 99.99% = 51.09%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's effective interest in M&P = 99.999998% × 71.09% = 71.09%"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), suppose Elnusa earns 20,000 from its own operations and Petrofin earns 10,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Elnusa level: attributable to Elnusa's owners = 20,000 + 99.99% × 10,000 = 29,999; NCI in Petrofin = 0.01% × 10,000 = 1"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE level: attributable to PHE = 51.10% × 29,999 = 15,329.49"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Total NCI = 1 + 48.90% × 29,999 = 1 + 14,669.51 = 14,670.51"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Check: 15,329.49 + 14,670.51 = 30,000"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Presentation and disclosure"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Statement of financial position:"},{"type":"text","text":" a separate line within equity (PSAK 110 para. 22; PSAK 201 para. 54(q), formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Statement of profit or loss and OCI:"},{"type":"text","text":" profit and total comprehensive income each split between owners of the parent and NCI (PSAK 201 para. 81B)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Statement of changes in equity:"},{"type":"text","text":" a column reconciling opening and closing NCI."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Statement of cash flows:"},{"type":"text","text":" dividends paid to NCI are financing cash flows, as the notes for AKL Chapter 4 say. Cash paid or received in changes of ownership without loss of control is also financing (PSAK 207 para. 42A, formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7). Only obtaining or losing control is investing (para. 39)."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 112 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Pengungkapan Kepentingan dalam Entitas Lain"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 67, IFRS 12) paras. 12 and B10–B11 ask, for each subsidiary with NCI material to the group, for its name and principal place of business, the NCI's ownership and voting share, the profit allocated to NCI, accumulated NCI, dividends paid to NCI and summarised financial information."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Non-controlling interest across industries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Listed holding groups."},{"type":"text","text":" Indonesian conglomerates often hold majority stakes in subsidiaries that are themselves listed, so the outside shareholders of each listed child sit in the parent's NCI. Consolidated profit and profit attributable to the parent can then diverge widely, and analysts work from the attributable figures."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Banks and sharia units."},{"type":"text","text":" A conventional bank's sharia business unit ("},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"unit usaha syariah"},{"type":"text","text":", UUS) is a division, so it has no NCI. A spin-off into a separate sharia bank ("},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"bank umum syariah"},{"type":"text","text":") creates a subsidiary, and NCI appears if outside investors subscribe. UU 4/2023 on financial-sector development (P2SK) left the spin-off conditions to OJK (art. 68), and POJK 12/2023 requires a spin-off within two years once the unit's assets reach 50 percent of the parent bank's assets and/or IDR 50 trillion. Inside a sharia subsidiary, temporary "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"syirkah"},{"type":"text","text":" funds (profit-sharing deposits) sit between liabilities and equity under the sharia presentation standard and are not NCI."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Consolidated funds."},{"type":"text","text":" An insurer or asset manager that controls a mutual fund consolidates it. The outside unitholders can redeem their units for cash, so their interest is a financial liability in the consolidated statements, even if the fund itself presents the units as equity (PSAK 232, IAS 32, para. AG29A). For example (illustration), an insurer holds 70 percent of a fund with net asset value of 100,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Liability to outside unitholders = 30% × 100,000 = 30,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fund earns 8,000: Dr Change in third-party unitholders' interest (expense) 2,400 / Cr Liability to unitholders 2,400"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 2,400 reduces consolidated profit, unlike an NCI share, which is only an attribution."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Put options written over NCI."},{"type":"text","text":" A parent that promises to buy the outside owners' shares on demand records a liability for the present value of the redemption amount (PSAK 232 para. 23), because it has a contract to deliver cash."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's NCI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE has no note for NCI. The movement comes from the statement of changes in equity (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2023"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI at 1 January"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"644,429"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"539,466"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit attributable to NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"119,560"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"125,852"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"OCI attributable to NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(8,487)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(2,008)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividends declared to NCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(27,797)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(18,881)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equity adjustment in subsidiaries"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(53,470)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"NCI at 31 December"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"674,235"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"644,429"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 1 shows where the outside owners sit (Daftar 5/4–5/8, PDF pp. 21–25): 48.90 percent of Elnusa (IDX: ELSA); 28.91 percent of M&P through PIEP; 45 percent of PT Badak NGL (55 percent owned); and stakes of 0.000002 to 1 percent in dozens of companies held at 99 percent or more, including Elnusa's own subsidiaries. M&P's own NCI in its subsidiaries also enters PHE's NCI. PHE gives no split of the 674,235 by subsidiary."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI return = 119,560 / ((644,429 + 674,235) / 2) = 119,560 / 659,332 = 18.1%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Parent return = 3,001,475 / ((13,534,126 + 14,433,117) / 2) = 3,001,475 / 13,983,622 = 21.5%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividends declared in 2024 against 2023 profit: NCI 27,797 / 125,852 = 22.1%; parent 2,079,749 / 2,599,686 = 80.0%"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's single shareholder group takes 80 percent of profit as dividends (Note 34, Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165), while the subsidiaries with outside owners, mostly listed companies, distribute far less of theirs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 2024 \"equity adjustment in subsidiaries\" is the one ownership event in the line. NCI fell by 53,470, the parent's equity rose by 6,138 (6,140 in other equity component, less 2 in retained earnings), and total equity fell by 47,332. That is arithmetically what section 5(b) produces: buying out outside owners carried at 53,470 for 47,332 would credit the parent with 6,138. No such transaction or cash flow is disclosed. Note 42 reports a gain on sale of subsidiaries of 34,242 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167) and the cash flow statement a net investing receipt of 64,578 from sales and additions of investments in subsidiaries (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17). Those more likely relate to TPDC's exercise in January 2024 of its call option over M&P's Mnazi Bay interest in Tanzania, which cut M&P's share from 80 to 60 percent (Note 5g, Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105); a sale to an outsider would not reduce NCI."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Upstream note: partners are not NCI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most of PHE's blocks are shared with other contractors. Those partners hold participating interests in a production sharing contract, which is a joint arrangement, so each party records its own share of assets, liabilities, revenue and costs. Their shares never enter PHE's NCI. The many companies held at 99 percent fit Indonesian company law, which requires a limited company to be founded by at least two persons and to restore two shareholders within six months if it falls to one (UU 40/2007 art. 7(1) and 7(5)–(6))."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"12. Related lines"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/share-capital","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"share-capital"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/share-capital","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"additional-paid-in-capital"},{"type":"text","text":": the parent's contributed equity, and common-control restructurings."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/share-capital","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-equity-component"},{"type":"text","text":": where differences from transactions with NCI may sit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/retained-earnings","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"retained-earnings"},{"type":"text","text":": dividends to the parent's owners and the profit attributable to them."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-comprehensive-income"},{"type":"text","text":": translation and hedge reserves, their attribution and recycling."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"long-term-investments"},{"type":"text","text":": associates and joint ventures, including a retained interest after loss of control."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/intangible-assets-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"intangible-assets-net"},{"type":"text","text":": goodwill on business combinations."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Least-confident assertions"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 2024 equity adjustment (NCI −53,470, parent +6,138) has no disclosed transaction or cash flow. Reading it as a purchase or redemption of NCI shares rests on the numbers alone, and any cash paid would sit in an undisclosed financing line. Linking the investing receipt and the Note 42 gain to TPDC's call option (Note 5g) is also inference; PHE does not connect them."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Elnusa's translation explains about 7,242 of NCI's OCI loss of 8,487. The other 1,245 is unexplained (hedge losses in subsidiaries with small outside stakes, or associates' OCI, are candidates); PHE does not attribute OCI by subsidiary."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"No PSAK 112 para. 12 table of subsidiaries with material NCI was found in PHE's FS (searched for NCI, KNP and the subsidiaries' names). With 674,235 of NCI spread over Elnusa, M&P and Badak NGL, at least one is likely material, so the disclosure is either elsewhere or missing."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Who holds the 1 percent stakes in PHE's 99 percent subsidiaries is not disclosed. If they are held by other PHE companies they are not NCI at all. PT Pertamina Pedeve Indonesia holds 0.0032 percent of PHE itself (Note 32, Daftar 5/147, PDF p. 164), which makes it the likely nominee; if so, the stakes are NCI in PHE's statements though inside Pertamina's group. The ownership percentages of M&P (71.09 percent) and Elnusa (about 51 percent) were checked only against secondary and older sources."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The banking rules in section 9 (P2SK art. 68, POJK 12/2023 thresholds and two-year window) were seen only in news reports, and a revision of P2SK was under discussion in 2026. The presentation of temporary syirkah funds on consolidation follow general practice, not a checked rule."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13); statement of profit or loss and OCI (Daftar 2/1–2/2, PDF pp. 14–15); statement of changes in equity (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 1b (Daftar 5/4–5/8, PDF pp. 21–25); Note 3a (Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46); Note 3c (Daftar 5/33–5/34, PDF pp. 50–51); Note 3d (Daftar 5/35, PDF p. 52); Note 5g (Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105); Note 32 (Daftar 5/147, PDF p. 164); Note 34 (Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165); Note 42 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk). Figures cross-checked to the PHE FS 2024 data extract."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes this page follows, \"AKL – Chapter 3 – A Introduction to Consolidated Financial Statements\", \"AKL – Chapter 4 – Consolidation Technique and Procedures\", \"AKL – Chapter 5 – Intercompany Profit Transactions: Inventories\", \"AKL – Chapter 8 – Consolidation: Change in Ownership Interest\" and \"AKL – Chapter 9 – Indirect and Mutual Holding\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan), summarising Beams et al., "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advanced Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":". NOTES. Corrections made here: the notes test control at more than 50 percent (PSAK 110 control model), keep the old PSAK 4 exclusions, first place NCI under liabilities, measure NCI from the implied value of the parent's price (PSAK 103 uses NCI's own fair value), write the step-acquisition gain as \"($262.500 −$7.000 − $8.000)\", and head the loss-of-control case \"resulting in consolidation\". Chapter 3's one-year-later elimination entry also reads \"Retained earnings $20.000 / Goodwill $40.000\" while the worksheet shows goodwill of 20,000; with the subsidiary's year-end retained earnings of 40,000 the entry should be Dr Retained earnings 40,000, Dr Goodwill 20,000. Chapter 8 gives the opening investment as $228.000 in the text and $288.000 in the schedule; 288,000 is consistent with 90% × 300,000 + 18,000. Chapter 8's investment schedule also reads \"Net Income (80% x $32.000) $28.800\" where the subsidiary's profit is 36,000. Section 9 has no note."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 110 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian"},{"type":"text","text":" (IFRS 10): paras. 22, 23, 25 VERIFIED (web, AASB 10 text, standards.aasb.gov.au); B96 VERIFIED (web, secondary: Grant Thornton Malaysia, MFRS hot topic Nov 2016); B94 deficit rule VERIFIED (web, secondary: IASB 2008 IAS 27 amendment summaries) and stated in PHE's Note 3c; B98–B99 and the definition in Appendix A STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 103 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis"},{"type":"text","text":" (IFRS 3), para. 19 measurement choice VERIFIED (web, IFRIC agenda papers 2010; Grant Thornton \"Recognising and measuring NCI\"; ACCA DipIFR article); para. 42 STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 112 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Pengungkapan Kepentingan dalam Entitas Lain"},{"type":"text","text":" (IFRS 12), paras. 12 and B10–B11 VERIFIED (web, IFRS Interpretations Committee agenda decision, January 2015; AASB 12 Appendix B). PSAK 207 (IAS 7) paras. 39, 42A VERIFIED (web, ESMA enforcement extract 23; IASB update April 2003)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Renumbering PSAK 65 → 110, PSAK 22 → 103, PSAK 67 → 112, sharia PSAK 112 (waqf) → 412: VERIFIED (web, IAI, \"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia\"). PSAK 105, 201, 221 para. 48C, 227, 228, 232 paras. 23 and AG29A, PSAK 109 (IFRS 9): numbers from the IAI rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"UU 40/2007 on limited companies, art. 7(1), as amended by the job-creation law (now UU 6/2023): VERIFIED (web, secondary: hukumonline, smartlegal.id); art. 7(5)–(6) NOT VERIFIED. UU 4/2023 (P2SK) art. 68 and POJK 12/2023 on sharia business units (thresholds and two-year window): VERIFIED (web, secondary only: Kontan, Fortune IDN, Bisnis, 2023–2026)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ownership of Elnusa (41.1 percent transferred from Pertamina to PHE and 9.9 percent bought from Dana Pensiun Pertamina in 2021, about 51 percent) VERIFIED (web, Pertamina news release; Kontan). PIEP's 71.09 percent of M&P VERIFIED (web, AMF threshold filing, September 2021). Both match PHE's Note 1."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beams, Anthony, Bettinghaus and Smith, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Advanced Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":", chs. 3, 4, 5, 8, 9, the textbook the lecture notes summarise. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED."}]}]}]}]}$j$: