-- other-comprehensive-income-recycling-translation-hedges: content_json backfill (the document content was serialised from)
with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Other comprehensive income (OCI) holds gains and losses on remeasuring assets and liabilities that the standards keep out of profit or loss, because putting them there would make profit a worse guide to performance. Profit plus OCI is comprehensive income, every change in equity in the period except transactions with owners and retrospective adjustments."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Each OCI item is either recycled or not. Translation differences on foreign operations, cash flow hedge results and fair value changes on debt investments at FVOCI later leave OCI, mostly by reclassification to profit or loss. Revaluation surpluses, remeasurements of defined benefit plans and fair value changes on equity investments at FVOCI never are, though they may be moved within equity to retained earnings."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When an item is recycled, the reclassification adjustment takes it out of OCI in the same period it enters profit or loss, so comprehensive income counts it once. The tax on each item follows it into OCI."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report accumulated other comprehensive income of 103,851 at 31 December 2024 against 132,724 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The balance is 0.69 percent of total equity of 15,107,352 (0.94 percent a year earlier). The flow behind it is in the statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income: translation adjustments of −14,810 (2023: 2,595), cash flow hedges, net of tax, of −21,940 (2023: −2,649), and a remeasurement of the employee benefit liability of −610 (2023: 1,430), a total of −37,360 (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After reading it a student should be able to say why an item goes to OCI, record translation, hedge, FVOCI and revaluation entries, and follow an amount into accumulated OCI and, where required, back to profit or loss."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Why other comprehensive income exists"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"OCI exists because some changes in the value of assets and liabilities say little about how the period went. The lecture notes on Kieso's chapter 4 open with the income statement's purpose, to evaluate past performance and predict future performance. A gain that comes only from the rupiah weakening, or from rates moving a ten-year swap, makes that prediction harder, yet leaving such changes off the balance sheet would carry assets and liabilities at stale amounts."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The standards resolve this with a second section of the performance statement. The 2018 Conceptual Framework (in Indonesia the KKPK, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Kerangka Konseptual Pelaporan Keuangan"},{"type":"text","text":") makes profit or loss the primary source of information about performance and lets the IASB, standard by standard and only in exceptional circumstances, send a change in the current value of an asset or liability to OCI where that makes profit more relevant or more faithful (paras. 7.15–7.17). It presumes that OCI is recycled later when that improves profit for that period (para. 7.19). No general principle selects the items."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The two sections add up to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"comprehensive income"},{"type":"text","text":", the change in equity from everything except transactions with owners in their capacity as owners (PSAK 201.7, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1). If every non-owner change in equity passes through one statement, nothing reaches equity unreported, apart from retrospective corrections of opening balances (PSAK 208) and transfers between components. PHE's 2024 figures show the identity."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit for the year 3,121,035 + OCI (−14,810 − 21,940 − 610) = 3,121,035 − 37,360 = 3,083,675 total comprehensive income"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Presenting OCI: the two groups and reclassification adjustments"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An entity presents profit or loss and OCI either in one statement with two sections or in two statements, the second starting from profit (PSAK 201.10A), as the lecture notes on chapter 17 set out. PHE uses one statement (Daftar 2/1–2/2, PDF pp. 14–15). Within the OCI section the items are grouped by what will happen to them later (PSAK 201.82A):"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Will not be reclassified to profit or loss"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"May be reclassified to profit or loss"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revaluation surplus on fixed assets and intangibles (PSAK 216, PSAK 238)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation differences on foreign operations (PSAK 221)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Remeasurements of defined benefit plans (PSAK 219)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Effective portion of cash flow hedges and net investment hedges, and the cost of hedging (PSAK 109)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fair value changes on equity investments designated at FVOCI (PSAK 109)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fair value changes on debt investments at FVOCI (PSAK 109)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Own-credit changes on liabilities designated at fair value (PSAK 109)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Insurance finance income or expense, where the OCI option is taken (PSAK 117)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Share of associates' and joint ventures' OCI that will not be reclassified"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Share of associates' and joint ventures' OCI that may be reclassified"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A loss in the right-hand column is one that profit will see later; one on the left never reaches profit. OCI is also attributed between owners of the parent and non-controlling interests (para. 81B), because a subsidiary's OCI belongs partly to its outside shareholders. From 1 January 2027 PSAK 118 (IFRS 18) replaces PSAK 201; it keeps the OCI section and its two groups and changes mainly the profit-or-loss section above it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Reclassification adjustments."},{"type":"text","text":" When a recycled item is realised, its gain or loss enters profit, but it was already counted in comprehensive income when it went to OCI, so it must leave OCI in the same period. That deduction is a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"reclassification adjustment"},{"type":"text","text":" (paras. 7, 92–93). The lecture notes on Kieso's chapter 17 introduce it with debt investments held to collect and sell, measured at fair value through OCI (FVOCI). Interest at the effective rate, expected credit losses and exchange differences go to profit, the remaining fair value change to OCI, and on derecognition the cumulative OCI goes to profit (PSAK 109.4.1.2A, 5.7.10–5.7.11)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys US dollar bonds at par for 10,000 on 1 January 2024, coupon 5 percent paid annually, held to collect and sell. At the end of 2024 their fair value is 10,400. On 31 December 2025, after the second coupon, it sells them for 10,250."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2025"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two years"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Interest income (profit or loss)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"500"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"500"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gain on sale (profit or loss)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"250"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"250"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"500"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"750"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"1,250"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"OCI: fair value change"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"400"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"−150"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"250"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"OCI: reclassification adjustment"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"−250"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"−250"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"OCI"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"400"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"−400"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"0"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Comprehensive income"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"900"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"350"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"1,250"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024: Dr Debt investment 400 / Cr OCI, fair value change 400"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2025, to fair value at sale: Dr OCI, fair value change 150 / Cr Debt investment 150"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sale: Dr Cash 10,250 / Cr Debt investment 10,250"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reclassification: Dr OCI, reclassification adjustment 250 / Cr Gain on sale of debt investment 250"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Over two years comprehensive income is 1,250, the coupons of 1,000 plus 250 of gain over cost. Without the reclassification adjustment the total would be 1,500, with the 250 counted in 2024's OCI and again in 2025's profit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The lecture notes need three corrections here. Their recycling example for \"perusahaan Ali\" reads \"Fair value of portofilio : $34.000 / Less cost of portofolio : $34.000 / Unrealized gain : $4.000\"; the cost must be 30,000. Their Saratoga sale states a price of \"$95.000.000\" but debits cash of $98,500,000, and its entry (\"Cash $98.500.000 / Debt Investment $98.185.648,32 / Gain on sale of debt investment $314.351,68\") removes only the amortised cost, leaving both the fair value adjustment and the unrealised holding gain on the books. Under PSAK 109 the asset leaves at fair value and the gain is reclassified at the sale, as in the last journal above. And their FVOCI impairment example follows an incurred-loss model; under PSAK 109 the expected credit loss on FVOCI debt is charged to profit with the other side in OCI, so the asset stays at fair value (para. 5.5.2)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Accumulated OCI in equity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What passes through OCI collects in equity as "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"accumulated other comprehensive income"},{"type":"text","text":", one of the components of equity in the lecture notes on Kieso's chapter 15. As those notes put it, the statement of changes in equity reconciles each component from opening to closing, with OCI analysed by item there or in the notes (PSAK 201.106–106A). Groups often keep one reserve per kind (translation, cash flow hedge, fair value, revaluation surplus), because the components leave equity by different routes: reclassification to profit or loss, transfer within equity to retained earnings for items never recycled, or, for hedges of non-financial items, removal into the asset's carrying amount."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE keeps one column for all of it. Its statement of changes in equity moves accumulated OCI from 132,724 to 103,851 by the owners' share of the year's OCI and nothing else (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Owners' share of OCI = total comprehensive income to owners 2,972,602 − profit to owners 3,001,475 = −28,873"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"132,724 − 28,873 = 103,851"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Translation of foreign operations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation differences arise because a group reports in one currency and some of its members keep their books in another. PSAK 221 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 10, IAS 21) separates the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"functional currency"},{"type":"text","text":", that of the primary economic environment that mainly drives an entity's prices and costs, from the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"presentation currency"},{"type":"text","text":" the statements are presented in. Foreign-currency monetary items are retranslated into the functional currency through profit or loss (see the Foreign Exchange Loss essay; PHE's foreign exchange loss, net, of 4,479 in 2024 is of this kind). Translation into another presentation currency is a separate step."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's functional currency is the US dollar for every entity except Elnusa, whose functional currency is the rupiah, and the consolidated statements are presented in dollars (Note 3a and 3r, Daftar 5/29 and 5/66, PDF pp. 46 and 83). Elnusa's statements are translated as PSAK 221.39 requires, and PHE's policy restates it (Note 3r(iii), Daftar 5/67, PDF p. 84): assets and liabilities at the closing JISDOR rate, income and expenses at average rates, and \"all of the resulting exchange differences are recognized in other comprehensive income\"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Why the difference goes to OCI."},{"type":"text","text":" It arises from translating opening net assets at a new closing rate and profit at average rates (para. 41). Neither changes Elnusa's rupiah cash flows, and nothing is realised until the parent sells, so the difference stays out of profit until disposal."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), take Elnusa's net assets in rupiah as Rp5,000,000 million at the start of 2024, about half its total assets before elimination of 621,898 (Note 1b), and its profit as Rp500,000 million, with no dividends. The closing rates implied by PHE's Note 48 are Rp15,439 per dollar for 2023 (6,377,542 / 413.080, trade payables) and Rp16,157 for 2024 (8,369,504 / 518.011) (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242). Assume an average rate for 2024 of Rp15,850."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Opening net assets = 5,000,000 / 15,439 = 323,855"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit = 500,000 / 15,850 = 31,546"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Closing net assets = 5,500,000 / 16,157 = 340,410"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation difference = 340,410 − 323,855 − 31,546 = −14,991"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"From opening net assets: 5,000,000 × (1 / 16,157 − 1 / 15,439) = −14,392; from profit: 500,000 × (1 / 16,157 − 1 / 15,850) = −599"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The rupiah lost 4.44 percent against the dollar (1 − 15,439 / 16,157), so the subsidiary is worth fewer dollars. PHE owns 51.10 percent of Elnusa (Note 1b, Daftar 5/6, PDF p. 23), so 48.90 percent of the difference belongs to non-controlling interests (para. 41)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI = 14,991 × 48.90% = 7,331; owners = 14,991 × 51.10% = 7,660"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr OCI, translation adjustments 14,991 / Cr Net assets of Elnusa (translated) 14,991, attributed as 7,660 to the owners' translation reserve and 7,331 to NCI"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Recycling on disposal."},{"type":"text","text":" When the foreign operation is disposed of, the owners' cumulative translation difference is reclassified to profit or loss as part of the gain or loss on disposal (para. 48). Losing control counts as a disposal even if an interest is kept (para. 48A). The NCI's share is derecognised with the NCI and never recycled (para. 48B). If PHE sold all of Elnusa and the owners' reserve for it held −7,660, that loss would leave equity with the sale."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Loss on disposal of subsidiary 7,660 / Cr Translation reserve 7,660"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A partial sale that keeps control is a transaction with owners. The proportionate share of the reserve is re-attributed to NCI, not recycled (para. 48C; see the Non-controlling Interest essay)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Cash flow hedges"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hedge accounting exists to fix a timing mismatch. A derivative is measured at fair value through profit or loss (PSAK 109.4.1.4), while the hedged risk may be a cash flow that does not exist yet, such as next year's floating-rate interest, so the swap's value would swing through profit years before the interest it fixes. PSAK 109 offers three relationships (para. 6.5.2), all restated in PHE's policy (Note 3g(iv), Daftar 5/47–5/51, PDF pp. 64–68): a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"fair value hedge"},{"type":"text","text":", where both sides go to profit; a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"cash flow hedge"},{"type":"text","text":" of variable cash flows from a recognised item or a highly probable forecast transaction; and a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"hedge of a net investment"},{"type":"text","text":" in a foreign operation, accounted for like a cash flow hedge."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Qualifying."},{"type":"text","text":" The relationship must be designated and documented at inception and meet three effectiveness requirements: an economic relationship between hedged item and hedging instrument, credit risk not dominating the value changes, and a hedge ratio equal to the one actually used (para. 6.4.1)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The mechanism."},{"type":"text","text":" For a cash flow hedge the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"cash flow hedge reserve"},{"type":"text","text":" is set at the lower, in absolute amounts, of the cumulative gain or loss on the instrument and the cumulative change in value of the hedged cash flows since inception. The part of the instrument's result that fits inside that limit is the effective portion and goes to OCI; the rest is ineffectiveness and goes to profit or loss (para. 6.5.11(a)–(c)). The hedged item's change is usually measured with a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"hypothetical derivative"},{"type":"text","text":", a perfect swap on the hedged cash flows."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's subsidiary PT Pertamina EP Cepu (PEPC, 99 percent owned) borrowed for the Jambaran–Tiung Biru (JTB) gas project at SOFR plus a credit adjustment spread and margin, with 1,208,418 outstanding at the end of 2024 (Note 27, Daftar 5/132, PDF p. 149). It has swapped the loan to fixed rates of 1.7910 to 1.9866 percent on notionals of 839,970 at the effective dates, the largest being Conventional Tranche B, 479,970 at 1.7910 percent to March 2029 (Note 27, Daftar 5/133, PDF p. 150), and accounts for the swaps as cash flow hedges (Note 14b, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123). With SOFR far above those rates the swaps are an asset, the unrealised hedge gain of 50,769 (2023: 66,533) in other non-current assets. When markets expect rate cuts, the fixed rate is worth less and the asset falls. For example (illustration), measure from a designation at the start of 2024, so that the year's change is the cumulative change."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Case A: instrument moves more"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Case B: instrument moves less"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cumulative fall in the swap's value"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"12,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cumulative change in hedged cash flows (hypothetical derivative)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cash flow hedge reserve (lower of the two)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ineffectiveness in profit or loss"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"0"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Journal"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr OCI, cash flow hedge 11,000, Dr Finance cost 1,000 / Cr Derivative asset 12,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr OCI, cash flow hedge 10,000 / Cr Derivative asset 10,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The cap keeps OCI within what the hedged cash flows can absorb; Case B shows an under-hedge is not charged to profit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Getting out of the reserve."},{"type":"text","text":" If the hedged transaction produces a non-financial asset or liability, such as equipment bought in euros under a forward, the reserve goes into the asset's initial cost as a basis adjustment. Otherwise, as for PEPC's interest, it is reclassified to profit or loss when the hedged cash flows affect profit (para. 6.5.11(d)). Suppose (illustration) SOFR averages 5.10 percent over a year and the swap settles net on Tranche B's original notional (the amortisation profile is not disclosed)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net settlement received = 479,970 × (5.10% − 1.7910%) = 15,882"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 15,882 / Cr Derivative asset 15,882"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr OCI, reclassification adjustment 15,882 / Cr Finance cost 15,882"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit then carries interest at SOFR plus spread and margin on the loan less 15,882 from the swap, which is 1.7910 percent plus spread and margin on the hedged 479,970, the fixed rate the hedge was meant to deliver. A reserve loss that is not expected to be recovered is reclassified at once (para. 6.5.11(d)(iii)). If hedge accounting stops, the reserve stays while the cash flows are still expected, and goes straight to profit if they no longer are (para. 6.5.12)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Equity investments at FVOCI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An equity investment that is not held for trading may be designated, irrevocably and share by share, at FVOCI (PSAK 109.5.7.5). The lecture notes call these \"non-trading\" equity investments, held, for example, to secure supply of a raw material."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The difference from debt at FVOCI is that nothing is ever recycled. Dividends go to profit or loss unless they clearly recover part of the cost, there is no impairment test, and on sale the cumulative gain or loss may only be transferred within equity (para. B5.7.1). PHE's policy elects FVOCI for its non-listed equity investments (Note 3g(i), Daftar 5/40–5/41, PDF pp. 57–58). Note 16 shows \"other investments\" of 63 without a measurement basis (Daftar 5/107, PDF p. 124)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a stake bought for 1,000 is worth 1,400 at year end, pays a dividend of 50, and is sold the next year for 1,400."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FVOCI (elected)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FVTPL (default)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Year 1 fair value gain 400"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Equity investment 400 / Cr OCI, fair value reserve 400"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Equity investment 400 / Cr Gain on investment 400"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dividend 50"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 50 / Cr Dividend income 50"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 50 / Cr Dividend income 50"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sale at 1,400"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 1,400 / Cr Equity investment 1,400; Dr Fair value reserve 400 / Cr Retained earnings 400"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 1,400 / Cr Equity investment 1,400"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit over two years"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"50"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"450"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Equity ends the same under both, but FVOCI profit never shows the 400, so a sale cannot be timed to lift profit."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Revaluation surplus and remeasurements: never recycled"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two more OCI items never return to profit. Under the revaluation model of PSAK 216 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Property, Plant and Equipment"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16), an increase goes to OCI as "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"revaluation surplus"},{"type":"text","text":", and a decrease is charged first against the surplus held for that asset and only the excess to profit (paras. 39–40). The surplus may be transferred to retained earnings as the asset is used or derecognised (para. 41). The Fixed Assets essay teaches the model."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a building carried at 2,000 with 20 years left is revalued to 2,600. A year later its fair value is 1,800."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Surplus = 2,600 − 2,000 = 600: Dr Building 600 / Cr OCI, revaluation surplus 600"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Depreciation = 2,600 / 20 = 130 against 2,000 / 20 = 100 on the old amount; transfer = 130 − 100 = 30: Dr Revaluation surplus 30 / Cr Retained earnings 30"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Carrying amount = 2,600 − 130 = 2,470; decrease = 2,470 − 1,800 = 670"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Surplus left = 600 − 30 = 570, so Dr OCI, revaluation surplus 570, Dr Revaluation loss 100 / Cr Building 670"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Remeasurements of a defined benefit plan go to OCI and are never reclassified (PSAK 219.122). PHE's −610 is that item; the Employee Benefits essay teaches it. Actuarial gains and losses on other long-term benefits, such as PHE's long-service awards, skip OCI and are \"charged immediately to profit or loss\" (Note 3q(iv), Daftar 5/65, PDF p. 82)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Tax on OCI items"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tax follows the item. Tax on an item recognised in OCI is recognised in OCI (PSAK 212.61A, formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12), as PHE's policy says (Note 3s, Daftar 5/67, PDF p. 84). Each OCI item may be presented net of tax, or before tax with one tax line allocated between the two groups (PSAK 201.91). PHE presents the hedge line \"net of tax\"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), Case A's hedge loss of 11,000 in OCI before tax, at an illustrative tax rate of 40 percent, reduces a future taxable gain, so it creates a deferred tax asset."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deferred tax = 11,000 × 40% = 4,400; OCI net of tax = 11,000 − 4,400 = 6,600"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Deferred tax asset 4,400 / Cr OCI, tax on cash flow hedge 4,400"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The same applies to revaluation surpluses and remeasurements, but a subsidiary's translation differences usually carry no deferred tax, because a parent that controls the timing of reversal and does not expect it soon recognises none (PSAK 212.39; see the Deferred Tax essay)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. OCI across industries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Banks."},{"type":"text","text":" Banks hold large government bond portfolios, many in a hold-to-collect-and-sell business model and so at FVOCI. When yields rise, equity falls through OCI before any bond is sold, and a sale for liquidity recycles the reserve."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Insurers."},{"type":"text","text":" Under PSAK 117 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Insurance Contracts"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 74, IFRS 17), effective for Indonesian insurers from 1 January 2025, an insurer may split insurance finance income or expense between profit and OCI (IFRS 17.88). With its bonds at FVOCI, interest-rate swings on both sides of the balance sheet then meet in OCI."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Utilities and property."},{"type":"text","text":" Revaluation surpluses dominate OCI where the revaluation model is used, such as PLN's 2015 revaluation (see the Fixed Assets essay). The surplus never recycles, so the higher depreciation lowers profit while the surplus moves to retained earnings in step."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Islamic finance."},{"type":"text","text":" A sukuk investor classifies its holding under PSAK 410 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Akuntansi Sukuk"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 110), whose 2015 revision added an FVOCI category for sukuk held to collect and sell, so OCI works as it does for conventional debt in section 2."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Associates."},{"type":"text","text":" An investor's share of an associate's OCI is presented in its own OCI, split into the two groups (PSAK 201.82A; see the Long-term Investments essay). PHE has no such line (see Least-confident)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's OCI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE shows the OCI section on the face of the statement, without a note of its own (Daftar 2/1–2/2, PDF pp. 14–15; Daftar 3, PDF p. 16)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2023"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation adjustments (may be reclassified)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(14,810)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,595"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cash flow hedges, net of tax (may be reclassified)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(21,940)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(2,649)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Remeasurement of employee benefit liability (not reclassified)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(610)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,430"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"OCI for the year"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"(37,360)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"1,376"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Attributable to owners of the parent"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(28,873)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3,384"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Attributable to non-controlling interests"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(8,487)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(2,008)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Accumulated OCI, owners, at year end"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"103,851"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"132,724"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"OCI / profit for the year = −37,360 / 3,121,035 = −1.2%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of OCI = 8,487 / 37,360 = 22.7%"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hedges are 58.7 percent of the year's OCI and translation 39.6 percent. NCI absorbed 22.7 percent of OCI against 3.8 percent of profit (119,560 / 3,121,035). PHE's dollar-priced PSC entities produce no translation differences, so the translation line comes from Elnusa, its only rupiah-functional subsidiary, 48.90 percent outside-owned."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"NCI share of translation loss = 48.90% × 14,810 = 7,242, about 85% of NCI's OCI loss of 8,487"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The other 1,245 is unexplained, since PHE does not attribute OCI by subsidiary; the hedge sits in 99-percent-owned PEPC and the whole remeasurement line is only −610."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 14b carries the hedge as an unrealised gain of 50,769 (2023: 66,533) in other non-current assets (Daftar 5/105, PDF p. 122; see the Advances and Prepayments essay). PHE gives no reconciliation of the hedge reserve or of reclassification adjustments, which PSAK 107 asks for (IFRS 7.24E)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Related lines"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"oci-translation-adjustments"},{"type":"text","text":": the translation line, section 4."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"oci-cash-flow-hedge"},{"type":"text","text":": the hedge line, section 5."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/employee-benefits-liabilities-non-current","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"oci-remeasurement-employee-benefits"},{"type":"text","text":": the remeasurement line, taught in the Employee Benefits essay."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-non-current-assets"},{"type":"text","text":": holds the unrealised hedge gain of 50,769."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"long-term-bank-loans-non-current"},{"type":"text","text":": the JTB loan behind the hedge."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"non-controlling-interest"},{"type":"text","text":": carries NCI's share of OCI."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/retained-earnings","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"retained-earnings"},{"type":"text","text":": receives transfers of non-recycled reserves."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"deferred-tax-assets"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"deferred-tax-liabilities"},{"type":"text","text":": tax on OCI items."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Least-confident assertions"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Elnusa's translation explains about 7,242 of NCI's OCI loss of 8,487; the other 1,245 is unexplained, and in 2023 NCI's OCI fell (−2,008) while translation rose (+2,595), so more than Elnusa drives it. The closing rates are implied from Note 48 (Note 3r prints both years as USD 0.06 per Rp1,000)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The hedge figures do not reconcile. A settlement received lowers the swap asset and leaves OCI by the same amount, so pre-tax hedge OCI should move with the asset (−15,764, give or take the 1,395 of ineffectiveness), yet the line is −21,940 after tax. Note 14b puts the 1,395 \"included in finance costs\", but Note 41 has no row for it; Note 40 shows an \"adjustment of hedging portion\" of 173 in finance income for 2023 and nil for 2024 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167), so the 2024 amount is probably inside interest expense from loans. The swaps' amortisation profile is not disclosed, so section 5's cases are illustrations."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 43e's OCI column (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172) holds −6 and +167 against employee benefits, +310 against \"others\" and −1,689 against the inventory provision, which may be translation of a rupiah subsidiary's balances. There is no hedge row, so the tax implied by \"net of tax\" cannot be located."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 16b shows \"other changes\" in associates of −59,610 (Daftar 5/108, PDF p. 125), but PHE's OCI has no line for the share of associates' OCI (PSAK 201.82A(b)). Whether these changes are OCI, distributions or something else is not disclosed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's policy for listed equity at FVTPL differs between languages (Note 3g(i), Daftar 5/41, PDF p. 58). The Indonesian omits \"tidak\" (\"yang mana oleh Grup diklasifikasikan secara takterbatalkan pada NWPKL\") and so reads as the opposite of the English \"had not irrevocably elected\"; this essay follows the English. The policy also lists quoted FVOCI debt (PDF p. 57), yet OCI has no FVOCI line."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13); statement of profit or loss and OCI (Daftar 2/1–2/2, PDF pp. 14–15); statement of changes in equity (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16); Note 1b (Daftar 5/4 and 5/6, PDF pp. 21 and 23); Note 3a (Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46); Note 3c consolidation, attribution of OCI to NCI (Daftar 5/34, PDF p. 51); Note 3g(i) and 3g(iv) (Daftar 5/40–5/41 and 5/47–5/51, PDF pp. 57–58 and 64–68); Note 3q(iv) (Daftar 5/65, PDF p. 82); Note 3r and 3s (Daftar 5/66–5/67, PDF pp. 83–84); Note 14b (Daftar 5/105–5/106, PDF pp. 122–123); Note 16 and 16b (Daftar 5/107–5/108, PDF pp. 124–125); Note 27 including the swap table (Daftar 5/132–5/133, PDF pp. 149–150); Notes 40–41 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172); Note 48 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 17 – Investment\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): FVOCI debt (\"held for collection and selling\"), non-trading equity investments, impairment, recycling adjustment. NOTES, opened. Covers the one- or two-statement presentation and comprehensive income (\"Recycling adjustment\", p. 14). Corrections in section 2: the Ali cost typo, the Saratoga price and the sale without fair value adjustment or reclassification, the incurred-loss impairment."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes, \"Inter 1 – Chapter 4 – Income Statement and Related Information\" and \"Inter 2 – Chapter 15 – Equity\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): purpose of the income statement; accumulated OCI as a component of equity and the statement of changes in equity reconciling each component (Ch 15 p. 14). NOTES, opened. The notes do not cover translation or hedge accounting; sections 4, 5 and 7–8 follow Kieso (ref. 9) and the standards."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 201 (IAS 1), paras. 82A and 91: VERIFIED (web, IAS 1 amendment text as issued in AASB 2011-9, legislation.gov.au). Paras. 7, 10A, 81B, 92–93, 106–106A: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. PSAK 118 (IFRS 18) replaces PSAK 201 from 1 January 2027: VERIFIED (web, IAI "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"DSAK Terkini"},{"type":"text","text":" vol. 7, 2025; IAI News 30 June 2025; Fortune IDN)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Conceptual Framework for Financial Reporting (2018; KKPK in Indonesia), paras. 7.15–7.19: VERIFIED (web, secondary only: ACCA DipIFR technical article \"Comprehensive income\")."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 221 (IAS 21), paras. 38–41: VERIFIED (web, AASB 121 text, standards.aasb.gov.au). Paras. 48–48C: VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation staff papers and agenda decision quoting the standard)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 109 (IFRS 9): para. 6.5.11 VERIFIED (web, secondary: IATA, \"IFRS 9 recoverability of the cash flow hedge reserve\"; and PHE Note 3g(iv), which restates it). Para. B5.7.1 VERIFIED (web, secondary: SEC comment letter quoting IFRS 9; ACCA). Paras. 4.1.2A, 4.1.4, 5.5.2, 5.7.5, 5.7.10–5.7.11, 6.4.1, 6.5.2, 6.5.12, 6.5.15–6.5.16: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED (6.4.1 content matches PHE Note 3g(iv))."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 216 (IAS 16) paras. 39–41, PSAK 219 (IAS 19) para. 122, PSAK 212 (IAS 12) paras. 39 and 61A, PSAK 107 (IFRS 7) para. 24E, PSAK 208 (IAS 8), PSAK 117 (IFRS 17) para. 88: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. PSAK 117 effective 1 January 2025 for Indonesian insurers: VERIFIED (web, Kontan; Investortrust). PSAK 410 FVOCI category from the 2015 revision: VERIFIED (web, secondary: IAI, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"PSAK Syariah for Academics: Akuntansi Sukuk"},{"type":"text","text":"). New PSAK numbers as used in PHE Note 3b (PSAK 201, 221, 109, 232) and the IAI renumbering rule."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intermediate Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":", IFRS edition, ch. 4 (comprehensive income), ch. 15 (equity), ch. 17 and appendix 17A (investments, derivatives and hedging). Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED. No owner's lecture note covers foreign currency translation; section 4 follows PSAK 221."}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb
