-- production-expenses-structure-of-profit-or-loss: content_json backfill (the document content was serialised from)
with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"An expense line is a classification choice. Under PSAK 201 an entity analyses its expenses either by nature (what was consumed: staff, materials, depreciation) or by function (what the cost was for: production, administration), whichever is more reliable and relevant. An entity that uses function must also disclose depreciation, amortisation and employee benefits by nature."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 201 fixes a list of line items, including impairment of financial assets and the share of associates, but no operating subtotal, so \"gross profit\" and \"operating profit\" mean what each entity decides. Other income and expense is a residual for items outside the main business, and no item may be called extraordinary."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 118 (IFRS 18), effective in Indonesia from 1 January 2027, sorts every income and expense into operating, investing and financing categories, requires operating profit and profit before financing and income taxes as subtotals, and brings management-defined performance measures into an audited note."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report production expenses of 4,647,647 for 2024 against 4,233,242 for 2023, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income, Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). That is 32.4 percent of revenues of 14,330,340 (29.1 percent in 2023) and 49.8 percent of the 9,330,714 PHE calls \"total costs of revenue\". The same statement shows general and administration expenses of 906,779 (2023: 826,447), expense from other operations of 867,866 (2023: 734,750) and other income, net, of 315,440 (2023: expense of 112,753)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After reading it a student should be able to say what the statement of profit or loss must show, classify an expense by nature and by function, build single-step and multiple-step statements, and recast a statement into the PSAK 118 categories."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. What the statement of profit or loss is for"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The statement of profit or loss reports performance over a period, and the period is what makes evaluation possible. The lecture notes on Kieso's chapter 4 give it three uses (evaluating past performance, predicting future performance, assessing risk) and three limits (unmeasurable items such as a good manager, method choice, estimates)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The statement records "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"income"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"expenses"},{"type":"text","text":" as the Conceptual Framework (KKPK in Indonesia) defines them, increases and decreases in assets or liabilities that change equity other than through owners. Expenses enter by three routes, as the theory notes on expenses set out: direct association with revenue (matching), systematic allocation over the periods that benefit (depreciation, depletion), and immediate recognition when no future benefit exists. PHE's policy is shorter: \"Expenses are recognized when incurred on an accrual basis\" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Quality of earnings."},{"type":"text","text":" Because profit is used to predict, the notes stress "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"quality of earnings"},{"type":"text","text":", whether this year's profit is a guide to next year's; they treat smoothing as management's aim and warn that excessive earnings management lowers quality. One-off items lower it too."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gross profit change = 4,999,626 − 5,650,519 = −650,893"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit before income tax change = 4,892,576 − 4,492,029 = +400,547"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lower impairment = 707,087 − 215,666 = 491,421; other income swing = 315,440 − (−112,753) = 428,193"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gross profit fell while pre-tax profit rose 8.9 percent on 2023, mainly through a lower impairment charge and an other-income line that turned from expense to income, part of which belongs to earlier years (section 9)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. What the statement must show"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 201 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Presentation of Financial Statements"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) lists the line items that must appear: revenue, with interest revenue under the effective interest method shown separately; gains and losses on derecognising financial assets at amortised cost; finance costs; impairment losses and reversals on financial assets under PSAK 109 (para. 82(ba)); share of profit or loss of associates and joint ventures under the equity method; tax expense; a single amount for discontinued operations; and profit or loss (paras. 81A–82; insurers add the PSAK 117 lines). The OCI section and the allocation of profit and comprehensive income between owners of the parent and non-controlling interests follow (paras. 81B–82A). Further line items, headings and subtotals are added when they help a reader understand performance (para. 85). An added subtotal must be made of recognised amounts, labelled clearly, presented consistently, and not given more prominence than the required totals (para. 85A)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The OCI section is taught in the Other Comprehensive Income essay, and the split of profit (3,001,475 to owners and 119,560 to non-controlling interests in 2024, Daftar 2/2, PDF p. 15) in the Non-controlling Interest essay. Earnings per share under PSAK 233 (formerly PSAK 56, IAS 33) is required only of entities whose ordinary shares are traded publicly or are being listed, so PHE, a non-listed subsidiary of PT Pertamina (Persero), shows none. The notes give the formula as net income less preference dividends \"dibagi total outstanding share\"; the denominator is the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"weighted average"},{"type":"text","text":" number of ordinary shares outstanding in the period, not the year-end count."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Classifying expenses by nature or by function"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every expense can be described two ways. By "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"nature"},{"type":"text","text":" it is what was consumed: employee benefits, materials, outside services, depreciation. By "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"function"},{"type":"text","text":" it is what the cost was for: cost of sales, distribution, administration. PSAK 201 requires an analysis by one or the other, whichever gives information that is reliable and more relevant (para. 99), and the choice depends on history, industry and the nature of the entity (para. 105)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The nature method needs no allocation, so it is simpler and harder to manipulate. The function method shows the business model, gross margin above all, but rests on judgmental allocations, which is why PSAK 201 makes an entity that classifies by function disclose additional information on the nature of expenses, including depreciation and amortisation and employee benefits expense (para. 104)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary records 6,000 of costs in a year by nature: employee benefits 1,000, contractor and outside services 2,400, materials used 600, depreciation and depletion 1,800, office rent and utilities 200. Production staff take 600 of the employee cost and administration 400; field contractors take 2,000 of the services and consultants 400; 550 of materials go to the field and 50 to offices."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"By nature"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"By function, PHE-style"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Employee benefits"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Production expenses (600 + 2,000 + 550)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3,150"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Outside services"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,400"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Depreciation and depletion"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,800"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Materials used"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"600"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"General and administration (400 + 400 + 50 + 200)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,050"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Depreciation and depletion"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,800"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rent and utilities"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"200"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Total"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"6,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Total"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"6,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit is the same under both. The function column needs a note disclosing employee benefits of 1,000 and depreciation and depletion of 1,800, and it is mixed, because depreciation keeps its nature label. PSAK 201 does not expressly provide for that mixture; PSAK 118 does (section 7). Charging the costs out to functions is one entry."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Production expenses 3,150, Dr General and administration expenses 1,050 / Cr Employee benefits expense 1,000, Cr Outside services 2,400, Cr Materials used 600, Cr Rent and utilities 200"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"What production expense holds at an upstream company."},{"type":"text","text":" For an oil and gas producer the function \"production\" means the cost of lifting hydrocarbons from wells that already exist: operating and maintaining wells and facilities, field support, the field workforce, and the materials issued to routine work (see the Inventories essay). Finding costs are a separate function, exploration, and the cost of the wells themselves reaches profit through depreciation, depletion and amortisation (DD&A); both are taught in the Oil and Gas Properties essay. Analysts divide production expense by volume to get "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"lifting cost"},{"type":"text","text":" per barrel of oil equivalent (boe). For example (illustration), if PHE's group had produced 380 million boe in 2024:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lifting cost = USD 4,647,647 thousand / 380,000 thousand boe = USD 12.23 per boe"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's financial statements give no volumes, so the real figure cannot be computed from them."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Single-step and multiple-step formats"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The same expenses can be laid out in three formats, which the lecture notes describe. A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"single-step"},{"type":"text","text":" statement adds all revenues and income, adds all expenses, and subtracts once. A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"multiple-step"},{"type":"text","text":" statement separates the main business from the rest and shows subtotals along the way. A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"condensed"},{"type":"text","text":" statement itemises revenue but shows expenses in a few totals, with the detail in schedules."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example, take PHE's 2024 figures. In single-step form other income enters as one net figure, because PHE gives no gross amount."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revenues and income = 14,330,340 + 251,561 finance income + 315,440 other income, net = 14,897,341"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Expenses = 9,330,714 costs of revenue + 215,666 impairment + 453,906 finance cost + 4,479 foreign exchange loss = 10,004,765"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit before income tax = 14,897,341 − 10,004,765 = 4,892,576"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The multiple-step form in the lecture notes runs: sales revenue, less cost of goods sold, equals gross profit; less selling and administrative expenses, plus or minus other income and expense, equals income from operations; less financing costs equals income before tax; less tax equals income from continuing operations. PHE uses a multiple-step form but draws the first line differently. Its \"costs of revenue\" (this page keeps that term for PHE's line and \"cost of sales\" for the generic concept) include exploration and general and administration. DD&A of producing wells belongs there under any function layout, as factory depreciation does in a manufacturer's cost of sales (see the Inventories essay). Recast with administration below gross profit, as in the notes, gross profit changes."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's layout"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Administration below gross profit"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revenues"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"14,330,340"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"14,330,340"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Exploration, production, DD&A, other operations"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(8,423,935)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(8,423,935)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"General and administration"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(906,779)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Gross profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"4,999,626"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"5,906,405"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gross margin"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"34.9%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"41.2%"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"General and administration below gross profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"–"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(906,779)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"After both"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"4,999,626"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"4,999,626"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The bottom line is the same, but the gross margin moves 6.3 points, so gross margins compare only between companies that draw the line in the same place. PHE does not say why it draws it there. One reading (see Least-confident) is that under a production sharing contract (PSC) administration and exploration are operating costs recovered from production (section 10), so the entity treats them as part of the cost of earning its entitlement revenue."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Gross profit, operating profit and other income and expense"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Gross profit"},{"type":"text","text":" is net revenue less cost of sales, and the notes call it useful for evaluating performance and predicting future profit. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Operating profit"},{"type":"text","text":" is not defined by PSAK 201. An entity may present it, but leaving out clearly operating items such as inventory write-downs or restructuring costs because they are irregular would mislead (IAS 1.BC56)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The lecture notes put other income and expense inside income from operations and say the IASB requires it (\"IASB menyaratkan\"). PSAK 201 contains no such requirement, since it defines no operating subtotal, and under PSAK 118 not all of it stays there. The notes' example of other income is \"interest revenue akibat dia nabung di bank\" for a trading company; under PSAK 118 that interest goes to the investing category, below operating profit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"What other income and expense holds."},{"type":"text","text":" It collects income and expenses outside the main revenue-producing activity: gains and losses on disposals, penalties received from vendors, donations, contributions. The Conceptual Framework's "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"income"},{"type":"text","text":" includes both revenue, from ordinary activities, and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"gains"},{"type":"text","text":", which may or may not be ordinary; the theory notes on expenses make the same point, that the framework \"tidak memisahkan biaya dengan rugi\"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Netting."},{"type":"text","text":" Income and expenses are not offset unless a standard requires or permits it (para. 32), but gains and losses on disposals of non-current assets are presented net of the carrying amount and selling costs (para. 34(a)). For example (illustration), a subsidiary sells a compressor that cost 500, with accumulated depreciation of 420, for 110."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Carrying amount = 500 − 420 = 80; gain = 110 − 80 = 30"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 110, Dr Accumulated depreciation 420 / Cr Fixed assets 500, Cr Gain on disposal of fixed assets 30"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Only the 30 reaches other income, instead of 110 of income against 80 of expense."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Unusual items, discontinued operations and intraperiod tax allocation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"No extraordinary items."},{"type":"text","text":" PSAK 201 forbids presenting any item as extraordinary, on the face of the statement or in the notes (para. 87). Material items are instead disclosed by nature and amount (para. 97). Typical cases are write-downs of inventories or fixed assets and their reversals, restructuring costs, disposals of fixed assets and investments, discontinued operations, litigation settlements and other reversals of provisions (para. 98). The lecture notes place restructuring severance in other expense; under the function method it can equally go to the staff's function, with its amount disclosed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Discontinued operations."},{"type":"text","text":" A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"discontinued operation"},{"type":"text","text":" under PSAK 105 (formerly PSAK 58, IFRS 5) is a component that has been disposed of or is held for sale and is a separate major line of business or geographical area, part of a single plan to dispose of one, or a subsidiary acquired only to resell, the three cases the lecture notes list. Its result is presented as a single amount after tax, so that the lines above show the business that continues. For example (illustration), take the services segment's 2024 profit before tax of 89,566 (Note 46, Daftar 5/220, PDF p. 237) and suppose the group had decided to sell the whole segment and that it qualified. Assume a tax rate of 22 percent, the general rate under UU HPP (Elnusa, a listed company, may qualify for 19 percent under PP 30/2020 if it meets the float and shareholder conditions; that is ignored here), and a loss of 10,000 on remeasuring the segment to fair value less costs to sell."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit after tax = 89,566 × (1 − 22%) = 69,861"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Remeasurement loss after tax = 10,000 × (1 − 22%) = 7,800"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit from discontinued operations = 69,861 − 7,800 = 62,061"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Intraperiod tax allocation."},{"type":"text","text":" Each section of the statement carries its own tax, so the 62,061 is presented net, and tax expense on continuing operations excludes the tax on the discontinued segment. The notes call this \"interperiod tax allocation\"; the term for allocation within one period is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"intraperiod"},{"type":"text","text":". Interperiod allocation is deferred tax, taught in the Deferred Tax essay."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. PSAK 118: categories, subtotals and management-defined performance measures"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 118 exists because PSAK 201 left the middle of the statement to each entity, so two companies' \"operating profit\" could hold different things. The IASB issued IFRS 18 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements"},{"type":"text","text":" in April 2024, effective 1 January 2027. DSAK IAI ratified PSAK 118 on 28 May 2025 with the same effective date, and it replaces PSAK 201. PHE's 2024 statements were authorised on 28 March 2025 (Note 1c, Daftar 5/8, PDF p. 25), before the ratification, so their list of standards not yet effective does not include it. Early application is permitted, and sharia entities stay under PSAK 401."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Five categories, three new."},{"type":"text","text":" Every item of income and expense goes into one of five categories: operating, investing, financing, income taxes and discontinued operations."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Investing"},{"type":"text","text":" holds the returns on assets that earn largely independently of the entity's other resources: income from associates and joint ventures accounted for by the equity method (always here), and income from cash and cash equivalents and other investments."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Financing"},{"type":"text","text":" holds income and expenses on liabilities that only raise finance, such as bank loans and sukuk, plus interest and the unwinding of discounts on other liabilities: leases, decommissioning provisions, defined benefit liabilities."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Operating"},{"type":"text","text":" is the residual, everything not in another category."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Foreign exchange differences follow the category of the item that produced them. An entity whose main business is lending, such as a bank, classifies its interest income and expense in operating."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Required subtotals."},{"type":"text","text":" Operating profit, profit before financing and income taxes, and profit. Gross profit is optional."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), recast PHE's 2024 statement. Assume that the foreign exchange loss arises on operating balances such as tax receivables and payables, that the gain on sale of subsidiaries stays in operating, and that the net interest on the defined benefit liability, which PHE now charges to costs of revenue and administration (Note 25b, Daftar 5/123, PDF p. 140), moves to financing."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net interest on the defined benefit liability = interest cost 46,048 − interest income 20,760 = 25,288"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Other income in operating = 315,440 − 162,620 share of associates = 152,820"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 118 layout"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gross profit (4,999,626 + 25,288 net interest moved out)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5,024,914"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Impairment of non-financial assets"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(215,666)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Other income, net, excluding associates (includes 57,813 of receivable impairment and correction, a line of its own under PSAK 109)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"152,820"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Foreign exchange loss, net"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(4,479)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Operating profit"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"4,957,589"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Interest income on bank placements and time deposits"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"251,561"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Share of profit of associates and joint venture"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"162,620"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Profit before financing and income taxes"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"5,371,770"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Finance cost (Note 41)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(453,906)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net interest on the defined benefit liability"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(25,288)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Profit before income tax"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"4,892,576"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profit before tax does not move. Within it, 414,181 of investment returns leave operating profit and 25,288 of interest leaves production and administration. Every part of Note 41 is financing for a company whose main business is not lending (see the Finance Cost essay)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Function, nature or both."},{"type":"text","text":" PSAK 118 allows a mixed presentation, the column from section 3, if it gives the most useful structured summary. A cost-of-sales line must include all inventory expense. An entity presenting any line by function must disclose in one note, for each function, the amounts of depreciation, amortisation, employee benefits, impairment losses and reversals, and inventory write-downs and reversals (para. 83). For the section 3 subsidiary it would show employee benefits of 600 in production and 400 in administration."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Management-defined performance measures (MPMs)."},{"type":"text","text":" An MPM is a subtotal of income and expenses that an entity uses in public communications outside the financial statements to express management's view of an aspect of overall performance, other than the subtotals PSAK 118 lists or another standard requires. Each MPM is disclosed in one note with an explanation, a reconciliation to the most comparable listed subtotal, and the tax and non-controlling-interest effect of each reconciling item. The listed subtotals include gross profit and \"operating profit before depreciation, amortisation and impairments\" within the scope of PSAK 236 (para. 118), so an EBITDA computed that way is not an MPM. For example (illustration), from the recast above:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Operating profit before DD&A and impairment = 4,957,589 + 2,443,000 + 215,666 = 7,616,255"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"If PHE published an \"adjusted EBITDA\" that also added back the BUMD contribution of 52,791, that figure, 7,669,046, would be an MPM. Its note would reconcile it to 7,616,255 and show the tax effect of the add-back, at an illustrative 40 percent rate 52,791 × 40% = 21,116."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. The statement of profit or loss across industries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Banks"},{"type":"text","text":" have no cost of sales. Their main income is the margin between interest earned and interest paid, so the statement opens with "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"net interest income"},{"type":"text","text":", and under PSAK 118 that interest is operating. Islamic banks report murabahah margins and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"bagi hasil"},{"type":"text","text":" (profit-sharing) under the PSAK 4xx series instead. For example (illustration), interest income 1,000, interest expense 450, fee income 120, operating expenses 300, expected credit losses 80."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net interest income = 1,000 − 450 = 550; operating income = 550 + 120 = 670; profit before tax = 670 − 300 − 80 = 290"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Insurers"},{"type":"text","text":" follow PSAK 117 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Insurance Contracts"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 74, IFRS 17), effective from 1 January 2025 (PHE Note 3b, Daftar 5/32, PDF p. 49). Premiums received are no longer revenue. The statement shows "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"insurance revenue"},{"type":"text","text":" as service is provided, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"insurance service expenses"},{"type":"text","text":", the net result of reinsurance, and a separate "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"insurance finance income or expense"},{"type":"text","text":" beside investment income. For example (illustration), insurance revenue 1,000, insurance service expenses 820, net reinsurance expense 30, investment income 90, insurance finance expense 60."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Insurance service result = 1,000 − 820 − 30 = 150; net financial result = 90 − 60 = 30; total = 180"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Retailers"},{"type":"text","text":" classify by function. Cost of sales is mostly inventory expense under PSAK 202, so gross margin measures pricing and shrinkage (see the Inventories essay), and store and head-office costs fall below it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Airlines"},{"type":"text","text":" usually classify by nature (fuel, staff, maintenance, airport charges, depreciation), since a flight's cost has no useful split into cost of sales and administration."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 37, 38, 39 and 42"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE classifies by function above gross profit and by nature in the notes (Notes 37–39, Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 37 production expenses"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2023"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Operation and maintenance"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,631,022"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,486,060"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Operation support"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,328,282"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,224,951"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Personnel expense"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"688,343"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"522,231"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Total"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"4,647,647"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"4,233,242"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Production expense growth = 4,647,647 / 4,233,242 − 1 = 9.8%, against revenue growth of 14,330,340 / 14,568,384 − 1 = −1.6%"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Personnel share of production expense = 688,343 / 4,647,647 = 14.8% (2023: 12.3%), up 31.8% in a year"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 38 splits administration into salaries and benefits of 510,007, office and rental of 202,308, consultants of 143,632 and others of 50,382, for a total of 906,779. Expense from other operations is \"the direct costs associated with goods and services provided by Elnusa and PDSI\", the service subsidiaries, led by outside services of 340,911 (up 54.7 percent on 2023), so it is the services segment's cost of sales after intra-group eliminations (Note 39)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 46 gives a second view (Daftar 5/220, PDF p. 237). It splits the same 9,330,714 into cost of revenues 5,980,936 (exploration, production and other operations, 5,980,935 by the face lines), DD&A 2,443,000, and salaries 473,026 and others 433,752, which together are the G&A line (906,778 against 906,779; in 2023 they tie exactly). It also shows an \"operating profit\" of 5,099,400, which is gross profit less impairment plus other income, net, a subtotal that does not appear on the face of the statement."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE discloses depreciation by function only for fixed assets (74,311, Note 12, Daftar 5/101, PDF p. 118) and oil and gas properties (2,187,085, Note 17, Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126). Personnel cost appears in three notes (together 1,453,337), but exploration administration of 123,932 (Note 36) is not split, so the total employee benefits expense PSAK 201.104 asks for cannot be read."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 42 (Daftar 5/150–5/151, PDF pp. 167–168) shows what other income collects: a contribution to regional government-owned enterprises (BUMD) of 52,791, vendor penalties of 72,502, a gain on the sale of subsidiaries of 34,242, a net credit of 57,813 for impairment and correction of receivables, and the share of associates and a joint venture of 162,620 (51.6 percent of the line). PSAK 201.82(c) and (ba) list the last two as face line items. Of the 428,193 swing, 254,946 came from the smaller BUMD contribution and 90,552 from 2023 items that did not recur (a profit-sharing correction and incident costs). The share of associates fell 87,658, and PHE states that its 2024 figure includes 234,223 of PRDL income for 2019–2023 recorded in the year."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Upstream note: cost recovery and lifting imbalances"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under a cost recovery PSC the contractor recovers current-year non-capital costs, current-year amortisation of capital costs and earlier unrecovered costs from production (Note 2a(iii), Daftar 5/10, PDF p. 27). Production, administration and exploration costs all feed that recovery, which fits PHE placing them together above gross profit. PHE also books adjustments for overlifting and underlifting, lifting more or less than its final entitlement, \"as part of direct sales costs\" because they are \"not transactions with customers\" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80). Gross split contracts have no cost recovery; the balances are taught in the Due from the Government essay."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Related lines"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"general-administrative-expenses"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"expense-from-other-operations"},{"type":"text","text":": the other function lines, sections 3, 4 and 9."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"other-income-expense-net"},{"type":"text","text":": the residual line, sections 5 and 9."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"exploration-expenses"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"depreciation-depletion-amortization"},{"type":"text","text":": the rest of costs of revenue, taught in the Oil and Gas Properties essay."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/revenues","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"revenues"},{"type":"text","text":": the top line and entitlement revenue."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/impairment-non-financial-assets","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"impairment-non-financial-assets"},{"type":"text","text":": the line below gross profit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/finance-cost","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"finance-income"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/finance-cost","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"finance-cost"},{"type":"text","text":": the investing and financing categories under PSAK 118."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/inventories-net","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"inventories-net"},{"type":"text","text":": materials issued to production."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Least-confident assertions"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"That PHE puts administration and exploration in costs of revenue because PSC cost recovery treats them as operating costs is this page's reading. PHE does not explain its layout."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The PSAK 118 recast rests on stated assumptions: the foreign exchange loss is treated as operating, the gain on sale of subsidiaries stays in operating, the residual, because the investing category covers associates, joint ventures and unconsolidated subsidiaries (secondary sources; none addresses a consolidated subsidiary directly), and the defined benefit net interest is taken as 46,048 − 20,760 from Note 25b, leaving aside a 241 \"return on plan assets\" that Note 25b also shows in profit or loss."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 38's 2024 rows (510,007 + 202,308 + 143,632 + 50,382) add to 906,329, not the 906,779 printed and carried to the face of the statement, a gap of 450, checked on the PDF page; it equals a transposition of 50,832 to 50,382 in \"others\". Note 37 prints \"2..631.022\". Note 46 calls a 2024 loss of 4,479 \"foreign exchange gain, net\" and prints 2023 DD&A as 2,856,854 against 2,856,853 on the face."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note 46 splits G&A into salaries of 473,026 and others of 433,752; Note 38 puts 510,007 in salaries and benefits. The 36,981 difference in the salary row is not explained. The segment \"operating profit\" of 5,099,400 includes the share of associates, which PSAK 118 would place in investing."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE presents its 162,620 share of associates and its 57,813 receivable credit inside other income, net, though PSAK 201.82(c) and (ba) list both as face line items; PHE may judge them immaterial (para. 31), at 3.3 and 1.2 percent of profit before tax."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE booked 234,223 of PRDL associate income for 2019–2023 in 2024 profit (Daftar 5/151, PDF p. 168), more than the 124,942 \"income of associates\" row it sits in. If it corrects an error, PSAK 208 would require restating comparatives; PHE does not say why it did not."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The PSAK 201 paragraph numbers other than 99, 104 and 105 are from knowledge of IAS 1, not checked against the Indonesian text."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of profit or loss and OCI (Daftar 2/1–2/2, PDF pp. 14–15); Note 1c (Daftar 5/8, PDF p. 25); Note 2a(iii) (Daftar 5/10, PDF p. 27); Note 3b (Daftar 5/30–5/33, PDF pp. 47–50); Note 3p (Daftar 5/61–5/63, PDF pp. 78–80); Note 3v carried interests with BUMD (Daftar 5/71–5/72, PDF pp. 88–89); Note 12 (Daftar 5/101, PDF p. 118); Note 17 (Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126); Note 25b (Daftar 5/123, PDF p. 140); Notes 36–39 (Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166, checked on the page image); Notes 40–42 (Daftar 5/150–5/151, PDF pp. 167–168); Note 46 (Daftar 5/220–5/222, PDF pp. 237–239). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes, \"Inter 1 – Chapter 4 – Income Statement and Related Information\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): uses and limits of the income statement, quality of earnings, intermediate components, single-step, multiple-step and condensed formats, gross profit, income from operations, gains and losses, NCI allocation, EPS, discontinued operations, \"interperiod\" tax allocation. NOTES, opened (4 pp.). Corrections made: other income placed in income from operations, bank interest as other income, EPS denominator, \"interperiod\" for intraperiod. The notes do not cover classification by nature or function or PSAK 118; sections 3 and 7 follow the standards."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes, \"TA – Chapter 10 – Expenses\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): three routes of expense recognition (matching, \"alokasi yang sistematis dan rasional\", \"pengakuan sesegera mungkin\"), \"BEBAN dan RUGI\". NOTES, opened."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 201 (IAS 1), paras. 99, 104 and 105: VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation staff papers AP21A April 2022 and AP21B May 2018; Deloitte DART). Paras. 31, 32, 34(a), 81A–82A (including the full para. 82 list), 85, 85A, 87, 97–98 and BC56: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 118 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Penyajian dan Pengungkapan dalam Laporan Keuangan"},{"type":"text","text":" ratified 28 May 2025, effective 1 January 2027, replacing PSAK 201: VERIFIED (web, IAI, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"DSAK Terkini"},{"type":"text","text":" vol. 7, 2025; IAI news \"Pengesahan PSAK 118\"). IFRS 18 issued April 2024, effective 1 January 2027, earlier application permitted: VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation project page). Categories, equity-method income always investing, interest and unwinding on other liabilities in financing, FX following the item, required subtotals, banks' interest in operating: VERIFIED (web, secondary: ACCA DipIFR technical article \"IFRS 18\"). Mixed presentation, para. 82(a) and para. 83: VERIFIED (web, secondary: BDO Australia). MPM definition, disclosures and the para. 118 list including operating profit before depreciation, amortisation and impairments: VERIFIED (web, secondary: BDO Australia; ifrscommunity.com; ESMA). Early application of PSAK 118 permitted, sharia entities excluded (PSAK 401): VERIFIED (web, PwC Indonesia, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"PSAK vs IFRS 2025"},{"type":"text","text":", pp. 4–5). Net interest on a defined benefit liability in financing (IFRS 18 B54): VERIFIED (web, secondary: EY Financial Reporting Update, June 2026, as cited by the independent recheck)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 105 (IFRS 5), PSAK 233 (IAS 33), PSAK 236 (IAS 36), PSAK 202 (IAS 2): numbers VERIFIED (IAI renumbering rule); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. PSAK 117 effective 1 January 2025: VERIFIED (PHE Note 3b). The 22 percent general corporate rate (UU HPP) is used only as an illustration. PP 30/2020 reduction of 3 points for listed companies with at least 40 percent of shares traded, held by at least 300 parties each under 5 percent: VERIFIED (web, secondary: DDTCNews, MUC, Kontan); whether Elnusa qualifies: NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intermediate Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":", IFRS edition, ch. 4, the textbook the lecture notes summarise. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED."}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb
   where e.id = 'd0e39c63-c632-4985-bbaf-2d0dfb91dcf4'
     and e.section = 'accounting'
     and e.content_json is null
     and md5(e.content) = 'b245022060d293eade4d5315b6da2611'
  returning e.id, e.title, e.snippet, e.status, e.content_json
), rev as (
  insert into public.essay_revisions (essay_id, revision_no, change_type, title, snippet, content_json, status, change_summary, changed_by)
  select up.id, coalesce((select max(x.revision_no) from public.essay_revisions x where x.essay_id = up.id), 0) + 1, 'migration',
         up.title, up.snippet, up.content_json, up.status,
         'content_json backfilled: the TipTap document the stored content was serialised from (accounting import, 22 Oct 2026). content unchanged.', null
    from up
  returning essay_id, revision_no
)
