-- trade-receivables-net-recognition-ecl-derecognition: content_json backfill (the document content was serialised from)
with up as (
  update public.essays e
     set content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"In brief"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A trade receivable is born with revenue, at the moment control of goods or services passes to a customer and the seller's right to payment depends only on time. Revenue is measured at the PSAK 115 transaction price, net of trade discounts and of the discounts, returns and financing the seller expects, and the receivable records the unconditional right to payment."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The \"net\" in the line is an allowance for expected credit losses (ECL) under PSAK 109. Trade receivables without a significant financing component must use the simplified approach, lifetime losses from day one, usually through a provision matrix of loss rates by days past due adjusted for forecasts. The old incurred-loss model, which waited for a loss event, is gone."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A receivable leaves the balance sheet when it is collected, written off because recovery is no longer reasonably expected, or transferred with substantially all its risks and rewards. Factoring without recourse is a sale; factoring with recourse usually stays on the balance sheet as a secured borrowing."}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report trade receivables, net, of 3,146,868 at 31 December 2024 against 2,768,397 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 10.3 percent of total assets and 39.2 percent of current assets (9.0 and 28.0 percent a year earlier), the largest current asset PHE has. This page uses those numbers as the starting point for its examples."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"After reading it a student should be able to say when a receivable arises, measure it at recognition and at the reporting date, record write-offs, recoveries and transfers, and see where banks, Islamic financiers, builders and miners depart from the plain model."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. What a trade receivable is"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A receivable is a claim on someone else for cash. Kieso sorts claims three ways. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Trade receivables"},{"type":"text","text":" arise from selling goods or services in the ordinary course of business; "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"nontrade receivables"},{"type":"text","text":" arise from anything else, such as insurance claims, advances to employees or deposits. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Accounts receivable"},{"type":"text","text":" are open-account promises with no formal instrument; "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"notes receivable"},{"type":"text","text":" are written promises to pay a sum at a date, usually with interest. And receivables are "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"current"},{"type":"text","text":" if collection is expected within the operating cycle or twelve months, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"non-current"},{"type":"text","text":" otherwise (PSAK 201, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's policy uses the first split. Trade receivables are amounts due from customers for crude oil, natural gas, drilling services, oil and gas support services, and energy distribution and logistics services; other receivables are amounts from third or related parties \"beyond the ordinary course of business\" (Note 3h, Daftar 5/51, PDF p. 68). Collection within one period puts a receivable in current assets, otherwise non-current (same note). PHE's separate \"long term receivables\" of 2,087 (2023: 2,997) carry no note reference on the face."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 115 (formerly PSAK 72, IFRS 15) adds a distinction that matters most for builders. A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"receivable"},{"type":"text","text":" is a right to consideration that is unconditional, meaning only the passage of time is required before payment falls due (para. 108). A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"contract asset"},{"type":"text","text":" is a right that still depends on something other than time, typically further performance."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Recognition: the receivable follows revenue"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A receivable appears in the same journal entry as revenue, so \"when do we record it?\" is the revenue question. Kieso's "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intermediate Accounting"},{"type":"text","text":" sets out the five steps of PSAK 115. Identify the contract, identify the performance obligations, determine the transaction price, allocate it, and recognise revenue when each obligation is satisfied, that is, when the customer obtains control."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For a crude seller, control typically passes at lifting, on FOB terms when the cargo crosses the custody point into the buyer's tanker. PHE recognises crude and gas revenue \"based on the actual lifting amount and sold to customers\" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/62, PDF p. 79). For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary lifts 600 thousand barrels for a domestic refinery at USD 75 per barrel, payable in 30 days."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Invoice = 600 × 75 = 45,000"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Trade receivables 45,000 / Cr Revenue from crude oil 45,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"There is no matching \"Dr Cost of goods sold / Cr Inventory\" of the kind a merchandiser records. Note 11 lists only materials, and Note 3p charges the related costs on the amount actually sold, so the cost of producing the barrels has already gone to expense."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Signing a contract creates no receivable. Until delivery each side holds only an unperformed promise, and the two cancel out."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Measurement at recognition"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The receivable starts at the transaction price, the consideration the seller expects to be entitled to (PSAK 115.47). Four things can make that differ from the list price."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Trade discounts"},{"type":"text","text":" are price reductions given to a class of customer. They never reach the books, so a list price of 80 per barrel less a 6.25 percent volume discount is recorded at 75."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Cash discounts"},{"type":"text","text":" reward prompt payment. Kieso gives two methods, mirror images of the purchase methods in the inventories essay. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"gross method"},{"type":"text","text":" records the full invoice and the discount only when taken; the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"net method"},{"type":"text","text":" records the discounted amount and shows a discount the customer forfeits as income. For example (illustration), a PHE drilling subsidiary bills 2,000 on terms of 2/10, net 30, so prompt payment saves the customer 2,000 × 2% = 40."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gross method"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net method"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Invoice"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Trade receivables 2,000 / Cr Service revenue 2,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Trade receivables 1,960 / Cr Service revenue 1,960"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paid within 10 days"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 1,960, Dr Sales discounts 40 / Cr Trade receivables 2,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 1,960 / Cr Trade receivables 1,960"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paid late"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 2,000 / Cr Trade receivables 2,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 2,000 / Cr Trade receivables 1,960, Cr Sales discounts forfeited 40"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 115 replaces this choice with an estimate. A prompt-payment discount is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"variable consideration"},{"type":"text","text":", so the seller includes in revenue the amount it expects, using the expected value or the most likely amount. If 70 percent of such customers take the discount:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Expected transaction price = 2,000 − 70% × 40 = 1,972"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A common treatment keeps the receivable at the invoice amount of 2,000, which is what the customer owes if it pays late, and holds the expected discount of 28 as a refund liability."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Sales returns and allowances"},{"type":"text","text":" follow the same logic. A crude cargo rarely comes back, but price allowances for off-specification quality do happen. If a buyer is expected to claim 1 percent on the 45,000 cargo in section 2, revenue is 44,550 and the 450 sits as a refund liability (or as a credit to the receivable once agreed)."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Revenue from crude oil (allowance) 450 / Cr Refund liability 450"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Time value."},{"type":"text","text":" When payment comes long after delivery, part of what the customer pays is interest, and PSAK 115 takes it out of revenue (paras. 60–65). An entity may ignore it when it expects at contract inception that the gap will be one year or less (para. 63), which is why a 30-day crude invoice is never discounted. The lecture notes this series follows adapt Kieso's long case to a crude cargo sold on 1 July 2019 for a four-year zero-interest note when the market rate is 12 percent. Revenue is the cash price of 1,000,000, the note's face value is 1,000,000 × 1.57352 (the four-year factor at 12 percent) = 1,573,520, and interest is 1,000,000 × 12% × 6/12 = 60,000 for 2019 (accrued at 31 December) and 1,060,000 × 12% = 127,200 for 2020."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary bills 10,500 for a drilling campaign and agrees that the customer pays three annual instalments of 3,500 starting a year after completion on 31 December 2024, with no interest. The customer would pay 8 percent a year to borrow."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PV of 2025 instalment = 3,500 / 1.08 = 3,240.74"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PV of 2026 instalment = 3,500 / 1.08² = 3,000.69"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PV of 2027 instalment = 3,500 / 1.08³ = 2,778.41"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revenue and receivable at 31 Dec 2024 = 9,019.84; interest to be earned = 10,500 − 9,019.84 = 1,480.16"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Year"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Opening"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Interest income at 8%"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Instalment"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Closing"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2025"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9,019.84"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"721.59"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(3,500.00)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6,241.43"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2026"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6,241.43"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"499.31"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(3,500.00)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3,240.74"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2027"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3,240.74"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"259.26"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(3,500.00)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"0.00"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 Dec 2024: Dr Trade receivables 9,019.84 / Cr Service revenue 9,019.84"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 Dec 2025: Dr Trade receivables 721.59 / Cr Interest income 721.59; Dr Cash 3,500 / Cr Trade receivables 3,500"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The current/non-current boundary runs through this receivable. One method puts the present value of the 2025 instalment, 3,240.74, in current assets and 5,779.10 in non-current. The other puts in current assets only the fall in carrying amount during 2025, 9,019.84 − 6,241.43 = 2,778.41, leaving 6,241.43 non-current. PSAK 201 prescribes neither; the choice must be applied consistently."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Subsequent measurement: amortised cost"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9) classifies a financial asset by two tests: the business model (is it held to collect contractual cash flows?) and the cash flows (are they solely payments of principal and interest, SPPI?). A trade receivable passes both, so it is measured at "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"amortised cost"},{"type":"text","text":": the initial amount, plus interest at the effective interest rate (EIR), less cash collected, less the loss allowance. For a 30-day invoice the EIR is effectively nil, so amortised cost is the invoice less cash and allowance. For the instalment receivable above it is the schedule."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's policy says trade and other receivables are \"recognized initially at fair value and subsequently measured at amortized cost using the effective interest method, less provision for impairment\" (Note 3h). PSAK 109 actually starts trade receivables without a significant financing component at the transaction price, which for short invoices is the same number."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Expected credit losses"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Why the model changed."},{"type":"text","text":" Under IAS 39 an asset was written down only when there was objective evidence that it was impaired. That was the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"incurred-loss"},{"type":"text","text":" model. It waited for a loss event, a missed payment or a bankruptcy, so allowances arrived late, typically after the recession that caused them. PSAK 109 replaced it with "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"expected credit losses"},{"type":"text","text":", the probability-weighted present value of cash shortfalls, recognised before any default. Kieso's ageing schedule survives as the provision matrix."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Two approaches."},{"type":"text","text":" The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"general approach"},{"type":"text","text":" has three stages. A new asset carries 12-month ECL (stage 1). After a significant increase in credit risk, presumed when payments are more than 30 days past due, it carries lifetime ECL (stage 2). When it is credit-impaired it carries lifetime ECL and earns interest only on the net amount (stage 3); default is presumed no later than 90 days past due. Both presumptions are rebuttable with reasonable and supportable evidence. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"simplified approach"},{"type":"text","text":" skips the tracking and recognises lifetime ECL from day one. It is required for trade receivables and contract assets without a significant financing component and is a policy choice for those with one, and for lease receivables (PSAK 109.5.5.15)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE applies the simplified approach because its receivables \"do not contain significant financing component\" (Note 3g, Daftar 5/43, PDF p. 60)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"The provision matrix."},{"type":"text","text":" A matrix groups receivables that share credit risk, ages each group by days past due, and applies a loss rate to each bucket, drawn from history and adjusted for forecasts (PHE Note 4b(vi), Daftar 5/84, PDF p. 101). For example (illustration), PHE's third-party gross receivables of 536,508 at the end of 2024 (Note 8a) are split into five buckets. The split is assumed; PHE ages only the total."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Days past due"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gross"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Historical rate"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Adjusted rate (× 1.10)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Allowance"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Not past due"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"380,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"0.30%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"0.33%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,254.00"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1–90"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"120,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1.50%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1.65%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,980.00"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"91–180"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10.00%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11.00%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"660.00"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"181–365"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"18,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"25.00%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"27.50%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4,950.00"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Over 365"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"12,508"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"60.00%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"66.00%"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8,255.28"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Total"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"536,508"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"17,099.28"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"At historical rates = 1,140.00 + 1,800.00 + 600.00 + 4,500.00 + 7,504.80 = 15,544.80"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Forward-looking adjustment = 17,099.28 − 15,544.80 = 1,554.48"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The adjustment needs a reason that links a forecast to customers' ability to pay. PHE names gross domestic product as the most relevant factor and adjusts historical loss rates for its expected change (Note 49c, Daftar 5/229, PDF p. 246). Here the illustration assumes that a forecast one-point fall in GDP growth has, in past cycles, raised loss rates by a tenth. Current receivables carry an allowance too, which the incurred-loss model rarely gave them."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Grouping matters."},{"type":"text","text":" A matrix is valid only for receivables that share credit characteristics. PHE's related-party receivables of 2,627,769 are owed mostly by state-owned companies. Applying the third-party average rate (17,099.28 / 536,508 = 3.19 percent) to them would give an allowance of about 83,751. A rate built from the debtor's own probability of default (PD) and loss given default (LGD), which PHE says its models use (Note 49c), gives a much smaller number. Assume a one-year PD of 0.3 percent, LGD of 40 percent and an average time to collection of 60 days:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,627,769 × 0.3% × 40% × 60 / 365 = 518.35"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Recording."},{"type":"text","text":" The allowance is a contra account, so the ledger keeps each customer at the gross amount. Starting from PHE's opening third-party allowance of 15,815 (Note 8a, 2023):"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Charge for the year = 17,099.28 − 15,815.00 = 1,284.28"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Impairment loss on trade receivables 1,284.28 / Cr Allowance for expected credit losses 1,284.28"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Write-offs and recoveries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A write-off removes the part of a receivable the entity has no reasonable expectation of recovering (PSAK 109.5.4.4; PHE Note 3g, Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61). Under the allowance method it does not touch profit, because the loss was expensed when the allowance was built. For example (illustration), a third-party gas customer owing 1,200 is declared bankrupt with nothing for unsecured creditors."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Event"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Allowance method"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Direct write-off method"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Write-off"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Allowance 1,200 / Cr Trade receivables 1,200"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Bad debt expense 1,200 / Cr Trade receivables 1,200"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"300 recovered later"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Trade receivables 300 / Cr Allowance 300; Dr Cash 300 / Cr Trade receivables 300"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 300 / Cr Bad debt recovery 300"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso keeps the direct method for illustration only. It puts the expense in the year a customer fails instead of the year of the sale, and it carries the receivable at more than will be collected. PSAK 109 does not permit it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The year's expense is whatever brings the allowance to its required balance after write-offs and recoveries."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Impairment expense = closing allowance − opening allowance + write-offs − recoveries"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's 2024 movement has no write-offs. It runs from 92,346 at the start of the year, less a reversal of 57,813 and a foreign exchange deduction of 17,124, to 17,409 (Note 8d, Daftar 5/96, PDF p. 113). The reversal appears as \"Impairment and correction of receivables\" of 57,813 in other income (Note 42, Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167), so 2024 profit gained that amount from releasing allowances."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Derecognition, factoring and offsetting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A receivable can be turned into cash before it is due. Kieso describes two routes. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Assignment or pledging"},{"type":"text","text":" uses receivables as security for a loan; the receivables stay on the books and the loan is a liability. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Factoring"},{"type":"text","text":" sells them to a finance company, the factor, which then collects from the customers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Whether a factoring is a sale turns on PSAK 109's derecognition tests. An asset is derecognised when the rights to its cash flows expire, or when they are transferred together with substantially all the risks and rewards of ownership. If the seller keeps substantially all the risks, typically by guaranteeing the factor against bad debts ("},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"recourse"},{"type":"text","text":"), the asset stays on the balance sheet and the cash is a borrowing. If risks are neither substantially transferred nor kept, the asset goes if the buyer controls it and stays to the extent of continuing involvement if not (PHE Note 3g, Daftar 5/42, PDF p. 59, summarises the same tests)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary factors 20,000 of gas receivables. The factor charges 2 percent and holds back 5 percent, 1,000, against disputes, so it pays 18,600 now."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Without recourse (sale)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"With recourse (secured borrowing)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 18,600, Dr Due from factor 1,000, Dr Loss on sale of receivables 400 / Cr Trade receivables 20,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Cash 18,600 / Cr Liability to factor 18,600"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Customers pay the factor: no entry"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Customers pay the factor 20,000: Dr Liability to factor 18,600, Dr Interest expense 400, Dr Cash 1,000 / Cr Trade receivables 20,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Holdback returned: Dr Cash 1,000 / Cr Due from factor 1,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(holdback included above)"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Total cost is 400 either way. The borrowing route keeps 20,000 of receivables and 18,600 of debt in view until the customers pay."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Offsetting"},{"type":"text","text":" is the other way a receivable can shrink without cash. A receivable and a payable with the same party are shown net only when the entity has a legally enforceable right to set them off and intends to settle net or simultaneously (PSAK 232, formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32, para. 42). PHE's arrangement with PT Pertamina (Persero) shows the difference between settling by offset and presenting net. Pertamina funds operations through cash calls that are offset against what it owes for PHE's sales, and Note 44c computes a net payable of 157,404 at the end of 2024 (Daftar 5/184, PDF p. 201). The balance sheet still carries the 18,434 receivable from Pertamina inside related-party trade receivables and the payables separately (Daftar 5/183, PDF p. 200), so PHE has not applied presentation offset."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Trade receivables across industries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The plain model above fits a seller of goods and services on short credit. Four industries change it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Banks."},{"type":"text","text":" A bank's loans are its trade receivables, but they carry interest and long terms, so the general three-stage approach applies (PSAK 109). For example (illustration), a loan of 10,000 with a 12-month PD of 1 percent and LGD of 40 percent carries 10,000 × 1% × 40% = 40 in stage 1. If a downgrade lifts the lifetime PD to 5 percent, it moves to stage 2 and carries 10,000 × 5% × 40% = 200. The jump from 40 to 200 without any missed payment is the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"cliff effect"},{"type":"text","text":" of the stage model, and PSAK 107 (formerly PSAK 60, IFRS 7) requires banks to reconcile the allowance by stage."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Islamic finance."},{"type":"text","text":" A murabahah is a sale at cost plus a disclosed margin, paid in instalments. Under PSAK 402 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Akuntansi Murabahah"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly PSAK 102) the seller records the receivable at the amount the customer owes, cost plus margin, with the unearned margin as a contra account. When the seller is a financier that bears no significant inventory risk, as a sharia bank usually is, ISAK 401 (formerly ISAK 101) has it recognise the margin over the term by the effective method. A trader that does carry the inventory risk recognises profit when the goods pass on a short deferred sale. For example (illustration), a financier sells goods costing 100 for 120 over 12 months:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dr Murabahah receivable 120 / Cr Inventory 100, Cr Deferred murabahah margin 20"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Each month: Dr Cash 10 / Cr Murabahah receivable 10; Dr Deferred margin / Cr Murabahah income for the month's share of the 20 under the effective method"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Impairment is where murabahah leaves the plain model. ISAK 402 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Penurunan Nilai Piutang Murabahah"},{"type":"text","text":" (formerly ISAK 102) is a bridging interpretation that lets the entity keep its existing impairment policy, incurred loss or regulatory provisioning, until a sharia standard on impairment is issued, so these receivables need not follow PSAK 109's expected credit losses."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Construction."},{"type":"text","text":" Kieso's cost-to-cost bridge example shows why PSAK 115 separates contract assets from receivables. In its first year the builder is 25 percent complete and recognises revenue of 4,500,000 × 25% = 1,125,000, but has billed 900,000 and collected 750,000."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Receivable = billed − collected = 900,000 − 750,000 = 150,000"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contract asset = revenue − billed = 1,125,000 − 900,000 = 225,000"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The receivable is unconditional; the contract asset becomes one only when the next milestone is billed. Kieso's \"Billings on construction in process\" account does the same job in the older vocabulary."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Mining and commodity sellers with provisional prices."},{"type":"text","text":" Concentrate is often invoiced at a provisional price and settled on a later market average. The receivable then carries price risk on top of credit risk, so its cash flows are not solely principal and interest, and it is measured at fair value through profit or loss under PSAK 109, with price changes kept out of PSAK 115 revenue from customers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Lessors"},{"type":"text","text":" hold lease receivables under PSAK 116 (IFRS 16), where the simplified approach is optional."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 8"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 107 asks for credit-risk information, including a reconciliation of the allowance and the exposure by credit-risk grade, for which a provision matrix by days past due may serve, plus concentrations and collateral. PHE gives receivables by customer, currency and age, and the allowance movement (Note 8, Daftar 5/95–5/96, PDF pp. 112–113)."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 Dec 2024"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"31 Dec 2023"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Related parties, gross"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,627,769"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2,129,601"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Less allowance"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(1,586)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(76,531)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Third parties, gross (local 209,952; foreign 326,556)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"536,508"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"731,142"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Less allowance"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(15,823)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(15,815)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Trade receivables, net"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"3,146,868"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"2,768,397"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Coverage, related parties = 1,586 / 2,627,769 = 0.06% (2023: 3.59%)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Coverage, third parties = 15,823 / 536,508 = 2.95% (2023: 2.16%)"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three things stand out. First, the related-party allowance fell by 74,945, from 76,531 to 1,586, which is close to the whole fall in the allowance (74,937), so the 2024 release sits almost entirely in that group, although PHE does not split the movement. Note 44c adds that related-party receivables are fully collectible (Daftar 5/184, PDF p. 201). Second, PT Kilang Pertamina Internasional alone owes 2,237,285, 85.1 percent of related-party and 70.7 percent of all gross trade receivables (Note 44c, Daftar 5/183, PDF p. 200), while Note 8 says there is no significant concentration of credit risk. Third, parts of the note keep the pre-PSAK 109 format. The ageing uses the old labels, including \"past due and impaired, more than 365 days\" for 560,851 against a total allowance of 17,409 (3.1 percent). The risk note says allowances cover \"only … losses that have been incurred\" (Note 49c(iii), Daftar 5/230, PDF p. 247), and the English text of Note 3g reverses the 12-month ECL condition that the Indonesian states correctly (PDF p. 60). The policy itself treats a receivable as in default at 360 days past due (Note 3g, PDF p. 61), longer than the 90-day presumption of section 5, which the standard allows only with supporting evidence."}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The usual analysis compares revenue with the average balance."}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Receivables turnover = 14,330,340 / ((3,146,868 + 2,768,397) / 2) = 4.85 times; days sales outstanding = 365 / 4.85 = 75.3 days"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revenue includes 1,133,213 billed to SKK Migas (Note 35, Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165), and Note 44c shows no trade receivable from SKK Migas at the end of 2024. Excluding it gives 13,197,127 / 2,957,632.5 = 4.46 times, or 81.8 days. For PHE's largest customer, year-end balance over its 2024 revenue of 7,372,296 (Note 35) gives 2,237,285 / 7,372,296 × 365 = 110.8 days."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Upstream note: what the receivable does not contain"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHE's trade receivables cover only barrels it has lifted and sold. Where a subsidiary lifted less than its entitlement, the shortfall is owed by SKK Migas and sits in due from the Government; PHE's policy says such imbalances \"are not transactions with customers\" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80). The domestic market obligation fee sits there too. Crude sales to Pertamina moved to PT Kilang Pertamina Internasional from 1 September 2021 (Note 44c)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Related lines"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/revenues","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"revenues"},{"type":"text","text":": the other side of every trade receivable."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/due-from-government","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"due-from-government"},{"type":"text","text":": underlifting and DMO fees owed by the state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"other-receivables-net"},{"type":"text","text":": nontrade claims under the same policy and allowance method (Note 10)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"long-term-receivables"},{"type":"text","text":": the non-current claims reported separately."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/production-expenses","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"other-income-expense-net"},{"type":"text","text":": where PHE's 57,813 reversal lands (Note 42)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/accounting/fsli/finance-cost","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null}}],"text":"finance-income"},{"type":"text","text":": interest unwound on receivables with a financing component."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Least-confident assertions"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 109 paragraph numbers (5.4.4, 5.5.15) and the 30- and 90-day presumptions were confirmed through secondary summaries, not the standard's text. The derecognition tests are stated at the level of principle."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The claim that a provisionally priced receivable fails the SPPI test and goes to fair value through profit or loss is from knowledge of IFRS 9 practice in mining and was not checked here."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Murabahah points (receivable at cost plus margin, ISAK 401's effective method for financiers, ISAK 402 keeping existing impairment policies) rest on secondary sources; the adopted texts were not opened. Keeping the receivable at the invoice with a refund liability for an expected prompt-payment discount is one treatment; measuring it at the transaction price under PSAK 109.5.1.3 is another."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Placing the 2024 release in the related-party group is inferred from the change in the allowance by customer group; PHE does not split the movement. Reading the ageing labels as a carried-over IAS 39 format is this page's interpretation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"That PHE's 360-day default definition departs from the 90-day presumption is this page's reading; PHE may hold evidence that rebuts it, which it does not disclose."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"textAlign":null,"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); Note 3g (Daftar 5/42–5/44, PDF pp. 59–61); Note 3h (Daftar 5/51, PDF p. 68); Note 3p (Daftar 5/62–5/63, PDF pp. 79–80); Note 4b(vi) (Daftar 5/84, PDF p. 101); Note 8 (Daftar 5/95–5/96, PDF pp. 112–113); Note 35 (Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165); Note 42 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 44c (Daftar 5/183–5/184, PDF pp. 200–201); Note 49c (Daftar 5/229–5/230, PDF pp. 246–247). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes this series follows, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 18 – Revenue Recognition\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): five steps, variable consideration (expected value vs most likely amount), time value of money (the notes' Pertamina–Shell adaptation of Kieso's zero-interest note), sales returns, cost-to-cost bridge example. NOTES. The 2019 interest accrual is dated 1 December in the notes; corrected here to 31 December."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lecture notes, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 17 – Investments\": impairment section. NOTES. Its incurred-loss approach is outdated and is corrected here to PSAK 109 ECL."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The lecture notes have no chapter on cash and receivables (Inter 1 Ch 7 is missing), so sections 1, 3, 6 and 7 follow Kieso's chapter order (ref. 5)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Intermediate Accounting: IFRS Edition"},{"type":"text","text":" (Wiley), chapters \"Cash and Receivables\" (classification, trade and cash discounts, sales returns, allowance and direct write-off methods, assignment and factoring) and \"Revenue Recognition\". Sections 1, 3, 6 and 7 follow the receivables chapter order from knowledge of the textbook; edition and page numbers not checked. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 115 (IFRS 15), para. 108 (receivable as an unconditional right; difference on initial recognition presented as an expense) and para. 107 (contract assets). VERIFIED (web, AASB 15 text, standards.aasb.gov.au). Para. 63 (one-year practical expedient) VERIFIED (web, NSW Treasury TC19-07 quoting AASB 15.63). Paras. 47 and 60–65 otherwise STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 109 (IFRS 9): simplified approach required for trade receivables and contract assets without a significant financing component (5.5.15) VERIFIED (web, IASB AP27C May 2024 and BDO summaries); 30-day and 90-day rebuttable presumptions VERIFIED (web, IASB 2013 staff paper AP5B and RBI Ind AS 109 draft); derecognition and factoring outcomes VERIFIED (web, KPMG India and IFRS Community factoring guidance, secondary). Para. 5.4.4 and classification tests STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 232 (IAS 32) para. 42 offsetting; PSAK 201 (IAS 1); PSAK 107 (IFRS 7), with para. 35N (provision matrix as risk profile) VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation taxonomy illustrative example 19); PSAK 116 (IFRS 16). Numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia"},{"type":"text","text":"); other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSAK 402 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"Akuntansi Murabahah"},{"type":"text","text":", ISAK 401 and ISAK 402. Receivable at cost plus margin and profit timing VERIFIED (web, secondary: journal summaries of PSAK 102 paras. 22–24); ISAK 401 effective method for sellers without significant inventory risk and ISAK 402 as a bridging impairment interpretation VERIFIED (web, secondary: BINUS Accounting, \"Pengesahan PSAK 102 (2019), ISAK 101 dan ISAK 102\", 13 Dec 2019). Adopted texts not opened."}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb
   where e.id = 'ff2f67b2-8acc-408d-84c4-c5d044c7d648'
     and e.section = 'accounting'
     and e.content_json is null
     and md5(e.content) = '4409ed06553e0041de6ce342fc67b0cb'
  returning e.id, e.title, e.snippet, e.status, e.content_json
), rev as (
  insert into public.essay_revisions (essay_id, revision_no, change_type, title, snippet, content_json, status, change_summary, changed_by)
  select up.id, coalesce((select max(x.revision_no) from public.essay_revisions x where x.essay_id = up.id), 0) + 1, 'migration',
         up.title, up.snippet, up.content_json, up.status,
         'content_json backfilled: the TipTap document the stored content was serialised from (accounting import, 22 Oct 2026). content unchanged.', null
    from up
  returning essay_id, revision_no
)
select up.id, rev.revision_no from up join rev on rev.essay_id = up.id;

alter table public.essays enable trigger update_essays_updated_at;
commit;
